1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Father of twenty-four (24) sons. Of which : -Orus -Chapar -Khutulun -Danishmendji -Sarban -Yangichar
2 CONT
2 CONT 1230[1] – 1301) was the grandson of Mongol Khagan Ogedei Khan (1185–1241) and thus leader of the House of Ögedei and the de facto khan of the Chagatai Khanate, a division of the Mongol Empire. He ruled part of modern-day Xinjiang 
2 CONC and Central Asia during the 13th century, and actively opposed his cousin, Kublai Khan, who established the Yuan dynasty in China, until Kaidu's death in 1301. Medieval chroniclers often mistranslated Kadan as Kaidu, mistakenly pl
2 CONC acing Kaidu at the Battle of Legnica. Kadan was the brother of Güyük, and Kaidu's uncle.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life Kaidu was the son of Kashin (whose name is also spelled Qashi)[3] and therefore a grandson of Ögedei Khan and Töregene Khatun, and a great-grandson of Genghis Khan and Börte. His mother's name was Shabkana Khatun from t
2 CONC he Bekrin (Mekrin) tribe of mountaineers that were "neither Mongols, nor Uighurs".[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1260, Marco Polo described Yarkand, part of the area under Kaidu as "five days' journey in extent"; that its inhabitants were mostly Muslim although there were also some Nestorian and Jacobite Assyrians; and that it had plenty 
2 CONC of food and other necessities, "especially cotton."[5] In the Toluid Civil War between 1260 and 1264, Kublai Khan was warring with his own brother Ariq Böke, who was proclaimed Great Khan at Karakorum, Kaidu began to have major co
2 CONC nflicts with Kublai and his ally, the Ilkhanate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, the Chagatayid Khan Alghu, who supported Kublai as Khagan, ravaged the lands of Kaidu. This forced Kaidu to make an alliance with Berke, the khan of the Golden Horde.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaidu
