2 CONT =========
2 CONT
2 CONT Afghânistân Dar Maseer Tareekh : Ghubaar, Mir Ghulam Muhammad : Free Download, Borrow, and Streaming : Internet Archive
2 CONT
2 CONT =======
2 CONT He is part of the ancestry of Etemad Saltaneh I - https://www.geni.com/people/Etemad-Saltaneh-1st-Haj-Ali-Khan-Moghadam-Maraghei-HAJEB-ol-DOWLEH/6000000036834526072?through=6000000036834621840
2 CONT i.e. 7-generations-to-Berdi-Bek-bin-Aureng-bin-Jochi-bin-Chengiz/6000000188012475880
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [...  one of the sons of Genghis named Oktay  (Ogedei Khan 1186-1281 (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%96gedei_Khan ), 
2 CONT who is called the Great Qaan , loved Muslims and they have written books about his care and respect for Muslims. (Of course, these historians mean the non-Mongol Khorasani Muslims at that time ) Oktay asked in the year 634 to send
2 CONC  a group to rebuild the city of Herat, or rebuild it as it is called today. Everyone of his advisors and experts said something; Some were against this work and some were in favor. Finally, those who were in favor of rebuilding He
2 CONC rat told His Excellency Oktay Qa'an that: Because of the
2 CONT weavers of Herat, thousands of families live in Bish Bagal. Let's send them. They said when should we send the fortune-telling in the name of Amir Ezzuddin Moghadam , the governor of the weavers ( in today's language of the people
2 CONC  of Herat, Vakil Shalbafan) and said that we will send him. This is the same Ezzuddin Moghadam who, before the conquest of Herat, came to Prince Tuli Khan's (Tului 1191-1232) service for the first time, with two hundred weavers, w
2 CONC ith precious clothes, and asked Tuli Khan for safety. .... Amir Izzuddin Moghadam was brought to the court and the Qaan ordered him to go to Herat with a hundred people of Heravi*. In this way, Amir Ezzeddin Moghadam, the governor
2 CONC  of cloth wevers, was chosen as the new governor of war-torn Herat by the great Mughal Khan] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ The Shah accepted this proposal and ordered that Ezzeddin Moghadam go to Herat with fifty of Heravi's cloth weavers, and after his sufficiency appears, he should take his relatives and children from the city of Bish Magal to Her
2 CONC at
2 CONT
2 CONT In this way, in the year 635 Hijri, by the order of King Oktay Qaan, Amir Izzuddin Moghadam went to Herat with Abdul Malik Bazdoi, Mahmoud Hassan Farash, Mohammad Dayeh, Pahlwan Shah Warahi, Fakhruddin Emadi, Mubarez Nafat Khanbei
2 CONC  and Saeed Badghisi.
2 CONT The story of Amir Izzuddin Moghadam 's one-year career in Herat is detailed in Saifi's history and other chronicles of Herat. Amir Moghadam went to Turkestan the next year, six hundred and thirty-six, to bring his family and relat
2 CONC ives to Herat. On his way back from Turkestan to Herat, he fell ill in Faryab and died, and his position was passed to his son Amir Mohammad Ezzeddin ]
2 CONT  ref: https://yaadhaa.blogspot.com/2009/08/blog-post.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ==
2 CONT Thousands of Herati cloth weaver families resided in Bishmagal. Historians write that one of the sons of Genghis Khan, named Oktay, who was called the great Qaan, liked Muslims and books have been written about his kindness and co
2 CONC nsideration towards Muslims. (By Muslims, these historians meant the non-Mongolian Khurasanis at that time). Oktay, in the year 634, wanted to send a group to restore the city of Herat to prosperity, or to rebuild it in today's te
2 CONC rms. Among his advisors and experts, everyone had something to say; some were against this action, and some were in favor. Finally, those in favor of reconstructing Herat told the great Qaan Oktay that:
2 CONT There are a thousand families of cloth weavers from Herat residing in Bishmagal. Let's send them. They decided to send whom and drew lots, which fell on Amir Ezzeddin Moghadam, the governor of cloth weavers (in today's language, t
2 CONC he people of Herat's representative of shawl weavers), and said that he would be sent. This is the same Ezzeddin Moghadam who, before the conquest of Herat for the first time, arrived at Prince Tuli Khan's service with two hundred
2 CONC  cloth weavers, with precious garments, and sought amnesty from Tuli Khan. Since then, he has been residing in Bishmagal; it is expedient that this faithful cloth weaver be sent to govern Herat. Amir Ezzeddin Moghadam was brought 
2 CONC to the court, and the Qaan ordered him to go to Herat with a hundred people of Heravi. In this way, Amir Ezzeddin Moghadam, the governor of cloth weavers, was chosen as the new governor of war-torn Herat by the great Mongol Qaan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saifi Heravi brings another version of this story in the History of Herat, which is very sweet and worth reading, and it also contains a pleasant and psychological point:
2 CONT The effect of Herat's climate on the pleasantness of clothes and designs
2 CONT There was a lady among Genghis Khan's ladies named Qatlugh Eshi (or Qatlugh Elshi). One day, this lady brought several sets of illustrated royal gold-woven garments to King Oktay. Oktay liked them and asked whose work these delica
2 CONC te and beautiful garments with such pleasant designs were? Qatlugh lady said: At that time when Tuli Khan was distributing the captives of the city of Herat among the lords and Iniyan (meaning Mongolian princes and nobles), the cl
2 CONC oth weavers were given to me. King Oktay Qaan told the lady to gift these cloth weavers to me, and in return, I will give you whatever you wish. Qatlugh lady gifted the cloth weavers to Oktay, and in return, she received five pros
2 CONC perous villages in Turkestan. The king cherished the cloth weavers and specially nurtured them. Years passed. One day, in the same year, which would be 635, Amir Ezzeddin Moghadam Heravi, the governor of cloth weavers, brought sev
2 CONC eral embroidered gold-woven garments to King Oktay. The king ordered that everything about these garments is excellent and praiseworthy (meaning the weave, color, and design are all beautiful) except that they lack freshness and b
2 CONC rilliance (meaning they are not attractive and do not draw one in). Amir Ezzeddin said that this deficiency is due to the distance from the atmosphere and climate of my city, Herat. In the climate of Herat, even the least garment 
2 CONC comes out delicate and pleasing. Allow me to go to Herat with the Oran tribe (one of the tribes of Heravi weavers), and I will pay double what I now contribute to the treasury every year. The king accepted this proposal and ordere
2 CONC d that Ezzeddin Moghadam go to Herat with fifty of Heravi's cloth weavers, and after his sufficiency appears, he should take his relatives and children from the city of Bish Magal to Herat.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this way, in the year 635 Hijri, by the order of King Oktay Qaan, Amir Ezzeddin Moghadam went to Herat with Abdul Malik Bazdoi, Mahmoud Hassan Farash, Mohammad Dayeh, Pahlwan Shah Warahi, Fakhruddin Emadi, Mubarez Nafat Khanbei
2 CONC , and Saeed Badghisi.
2 CONT The story of Amir Ezzeddin Moghadam's one-year career in Herat is detailed in Saifi's history and other chronicles of Herat. The next year, six hundred and thirty-six, Amir Moghadam went to Turkestan to bring his family and relati
2 CONC ves to Herat. On his way back from Turkestan to Herat, he fell ill in Faryab and died, and his
2 CONT
2 CONT  position was passed to his son Amir Mohammad Ezzeddin.
2 CONT =====
