1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  Salghurid ruler of Fārs (1263-84), daughter of Atābeg Saʿd II. http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/abes-katun-salghurid-ruler-of-fars-1263-84-daughter-of-atabeg-sad-ii
2 CONT
2 CONT While still a child (in Rabīʿ I, 663/December, 1264), she succeeded her cousin Salǰuqšāh b. Salḡūr, whom the Mongols had driven away. Ca. 671/1272 she married the Mongol prince Möngke Temūr (Mangū Tīmūr; d. Moḥarram, 681/April, 12
2 CONC 82), fourth son of the Il-khan Hülegu (Hūlāgū), who ruled Fārs in her name from then on. Ābeš Ḵātūn’s name, however, was mentioned in the ḵoṭba and on coins; she also had the right to a court chapel. The true administration of the
2 CONC  country lay in the hands of the Mongol governor Suḡunǰaq (Sūnǰāq) 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abish_Khatun
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abish_Khatun '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abish Khatun''' (died 1287) also known as Abisha Hadud Khatun and Aubee Khatton was the regnant of Atabeg from 1263 to 1275. She was also the governor of Fars from 1283 to 1287.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Abish Khatun was nominated as ruler by the Ilkhan of the Khwarazham Empire in Persia, after her mother, Terken Khatun, was killed. Her name was read in the khutha and struck on the coinage. In 1274, when she was about fifteen, she
2 CONC  was taken to the Ilkhan's ordu, and married to Tash-Möngke (Mengü Temür), a younger son of Hulagu Khan. This was a marriage, forbidden in Islamic law, between a Muslim woman and a shamanist, but presumably the will of the Ilkhan 
2 CONC transcended all other considerations. She became his chief wife and had two daughters by him, Kürdüjin and Alghanchi. When her husband was sent as governor to Fars, she was retained in the ordu, but 1283, the new Ilkhan, Ahmad Teg
2 CONC üder (1282-84), recalled him from Shiraz and appointed her in his place. Her financial recklessness, coinciding with a drought throughout Fars, meant that she defaulted on her revenue payments, so that Ahmad Tegüder's successor, A
2 CONC rghun (1284-91), ordered her to appear at the ordu. Perhaps relying on the good offices of Öljei Khatun, Hülegü's widow, to protect her from the Ilkhan's wrath, she declined to go and behaved outrageously toward the officials sent
2 CONC  to supersede her. She was eventually forced to capitulate and submitted to the Ilkhan (Öljei Khatun did indeed intercede for her), dying at the ordu in 1287, after having lived (circa 1269-87).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # http://www.guide2womenleaders.com/Iran_Substates.htm
2 CONT
