1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Goharshād (Persian: گوهرشاد Gowharšād; meaning "joyful jewel" or "shining jewel" in Persian; alternative spelling: Gawharshād) was a Persian noble and wife of Shāh Rukh, the emperor of the Timurid Dynasty of Herāt.
2 CONC  She was the daughter of Giāth ud-Din Tarkhān, an important and influential noble during Tīmur's reign. According to family traditions, the title Tarkhān was given to the family by Genghis Khan personally.
2 CONT
2 CONT Goharshad, worked with the Persian architect Qavam ud-Din in the planning and construction of a series of magnificent public buildings, including The Mosque of Gohar Shad, the Imam Reza Shrine .
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, Goharshād once inspected a mosque and a religious school (madrasah) in Herāt accompanied by two hundred female attendants, after it had been cleared of its students, all of whom were male. One youth remained, 
2 CONC having fallen asleep in his cell, and was discovered by an attendant and seduced. When Goharshad found out, she ordered that all two hundred of her attendants be married to the students. This legend epitomizes the liberal Islamic 
2 CONC tradition in Afghanistan stretching back centuries and whose symbol is Goharshād.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Goharshād's tomb is located next to the madrasah that she had built, of which the minaret remains until this day. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A women's university in Kabul that opened in 2003 bears the name of Goharshād
2 CONT
2 CONT For further information please visit the below links
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goharshad
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goharshad_Mosque
2 CONT
2 CONT http://india.smashits.com/wikipedia/Gauhar_Shad
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Goharshād Begum (Persian: گوهرشاد‎‎ Gowharšād; meaning "joyful jewel" or "shining jewel"; alternative spelling: Gawharshād) was a wife of Shāh Rukh, the Emperor of the Timurid Empire of Herāt. She was the daughter of Giāth ud-Din 
2 CONC Tarkhān, an important and influential noble during Tīmur's reign. According to family traditions, the title Tarkhān was given to the family by Genghis Khan personally.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gohar Shad was married to Sharukh probably in 1388, certainly before 1394 when their son, Ulugh Beg was born. It was a successful marriage, according to the ballads of Herat which sing of Shahrukh's love for her. But little is kno
2 CONC wn of their first forty years together, except what concerns her buildings.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Along with her brothers who were administrators at the Timurid court in Herāt, Goharshād played a very important role in the early Timurid history. In 1405 she moved the Timurid capital from Samarkand to Herāt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under her patronage, the Persian language and Persian culture were elevated to a main element of the Timurid dynasty. She and her husband led a cultural renaissance by their lavish patronage of the arts, attracting to their court 
2 CONC artists, architects and philosophers and poets acknowledged today among the world's most illustrious, including the poet Jami. Many exquisite examples of Timurid architecture remain in Herāt today.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her husband in 1447 Goharshad maneuvered her favorite grandson to the throne. For ten years she became the de facto ruler of an empire stretching from the Tigris to the borders of China. When she was well past 8
2 CONC 0, she was executed on 19 July 1457 on the order of Sultān Abū Sa'īd.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, Goharshād once inspected a mosque and a religious school (madrasah) in Herāt accompanied by two hundred female attendants, after it had been cleared of its students, all of whom were male. One youth remained, 
2 CONC having fallen asleep in his cell, and was discovered by an attendant and seduced. When Goharshād found out, she ordered that all two hundred of her attendants be married to the students. This legend epitomizes the liberal Islamic 
2 CONC tradition in Afghanistan stretching back centuries and whose symbol is Goharshād.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/gowhar-sad-aga
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT For further information please visit the below link
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goharshad
