1 NAME Mohammad Khodabandeh Oljeitu Nikolah
1 NAME /سلطان محمد خدابنده اولجایتو/
2 GIVN
2 SURN سلطان محمد خدابنده اولجایتو
1 NAME Харбандэ
2 GIVN Харбандэ
2 SURN
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1280
1 DEAT
2 DATE 16 DEC 1316
2 PLAC Soltaniyeh, Iran
2 ADDR
1 OCCU 8th Ilkhan 1303 - 1316
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oljeitu https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DA%86%D8%B1%DA%AF%D8%B1
2 CONT
2 CONT 8th Ilkhan
2 CONT ============
2 CONT Öljaitü had six sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bastam (1297 - 1309) - with Eltuzmish Khatun;
2 CONT Bayazid (1300 - 1308) - with Eltuzmish Khatun; Tayghur - with Eltuzmish Khatun; Sulayman Shah - with Adil Shah Khatun; Abu'l Khayr - with Oljatai Khatun; Abu Sa'id Bahadur Khan - with Hajji Khatun; Öljaitü also allegedly had an ad
2 CONC ditional son, Ilchi who was claimed as an ancestor of the Arghun and Tarkhan dynasties of Afghanistan and India
2 CONT
2 CONT Öljaitü had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dowlandi Khatun (died 1314) - with Terjughan Khatun, married on 30 September 1305 to Amir Chupan; Sati Beg Khatun - with Eltuzmish Khatun, married firstly on 6 September 1319 to Amir Chupan, married secondly in 1336 to Arpa Ke'un,
2 CONC  married thirdly in 1339 to Suleiman Khan; Sultan Khatun - with Qutlugh Shah Khatun;
2 CONT
2 CONT =============
2 CONT His capital is moved from Tabriz to Soltanieh
2 CONT
2 CONT Oljaitu is also known as Ghurbati Urab Ti
2 CONT
2 CONT Oljaitu Khan was initially baptized as a Christian, named Nicholas after Pope Nicholas IV [ Nicholas IV died in Rome on 4 April 1292 ] . However, like most Mongols, he seems to have had quite lax attitudes towards religion, later 
2 CONC believing in Buddhism, before converting to Islam. Even in this, he was quite easy going, moving between various sects, including supporting the Shi’ite theologian, Allameh Helli. In continuing the usual Ilkhan policy of invading 
2 CONC Syria, Oljaitu sent several embassies to the various Christian kingdoms of western Europe, looking for support. His promises seem to have been real enough to tempt Philip IV of France to want to rise for a new Crusade, although no
2 CONC ne of these in fact ever came to fruition. He did, however, marry a daughter of Andronikos II of Byzantium and helped him cope with the early rise of the Ottomans to power. The increased connections with Europe, including the seve
2 CONC ral embassies sent, brought enough information about Europe to Iran for Rashid od-Din Fazlollah to compose the first ever Persian work of history which also covered the history of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oljaitu died in 1316, leaving the throne to his son, Abu Sa’id Bahador Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The country was flourishing and the army well organized" ref page 262 The Mongols in Iran
2 CONT
2 CONT Pendant son regne, commence la dynastie des Injuides [...The Injuids gained control of parts of Persia, mostly Fars, in 1304 at the beginning of the reign of the Ilkhan Öljeitü. The Ilkhan had given Sharaf al-Din Mahmud Shah contr
2 CONC ol of the injü (or inji; the Mongol word for the royal estates). Sharaf al-Din was reportedly descended from 'Abd-Allah Ansari, an 11th-century mystic of Herat. His son, Amir Ghiyas al-Din Kai-Khusrau, assisted another family, the
2 CONC  Muzaffarids, in their takeover of Yazd. By 1325 Sharaf al-Din had gained nearly an absolute grip on the region. His power displeased Öljeitü's successor Abu Sa'id, who ordered Sharaf al-Din removed and sent a Sheikh Hussein ibn J
2 CONC uban (Cobin) to replace him. Kai-Khusrau, who ruled Shiraz for his father, resisted; and Sheikh Hussein (Cobin) was forced to return with an Ilkhan army. Also during Abu Sa'id's lifetime, Sharaf al-Din was imprisoned in Tabriz for
2 CONC  a failed attempt to murder his successor.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Enthroned at Aljan 19 Jul 1295 with the title Ghiyas-al-din MUHAMMAD Khudabanda Oljaitu
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.academia.edu/788646/_A_Mongol_Princess_Making_Hajj_the_Biography_of_El_Qutlugh_Daughter_of_Abagha_Ilkhan_r._1265-82_Journal_of_Royal_Asiatic_Society_JRAS_21_3_2011_pp._331-359
2 CONT
2 CONT Öljeitü, Oljeitu, Olcayto or Uljeitu, Öljaitu, Ölziit (Mongolian: ᠦᠯᠵᠡᠢᠲᠦ ᠺᠬᠠᠨ, Өлзийт хаан), also known as Muhammad Khodabandeh (Persian: محمد خدابنده - اولجایتو‎‎, khodābandeh from Persian meaning the "man of God" or "servant of
2 CONC  God"; 1280 - December 16, 1316), was the eighth Ilkhanid dynasty ruler in Tabriz, Iran from 1304 to 1316. His name "Ölziit" means "blessed" in the Mongolian language.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of the Ilkhan ruler Arghun, brother and successor of Mahmud Ghazan (5th successor of Genghis Khan), and great-grandson of the Ilkhanate founder Hulagu.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Arghun had one of his sons baptized, Khordabandah, the future Oljaitu, and in the Pope's honour, went as far as giving him the name Nicholas", Histoire de l'Empire Mongol, Jean-Paul Roux, p.408
2 CONT
2 CONT Khodabandeh renforce l'Islam dans tout son territoire. Reconnu souverain par les russes, le Khawrazm (Turkmenistan et Uzbekistan actuels), Belgrade, le Sultanat de Rome, la Syrie du Karakorum, la Sind et l'Iraq.
2 CONT
2 CONT Noble et pieux, il meurt apres 13 ans de regne. Il a fait construite la ville de Sultaniyeh.la proclame sa capitale et y a sa sepulture.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ḡāzān’s successor Öljeitü (r. 1304-1316) set out for Baghdad in 1309, on the way visiting the Ayvān-e Madāʾen (the remains of the Sassanian palace at Ctesiphon), where he knelt three times in front of the Ṭāq-e Kesrā. In Baghdad, 
2 CONC at the instigation of Saʿd-al-Din Sāvaji, he committed Aṣil-al-Din to be tortured, bringing to an end the influence of the family of Ḵᵛāja Naṣer-al-Din Ṭusi. Öljeitü’s devotional shift from Sunnism to Shiʿism occurred at this time
2 CONC  (see Eqbāl, pp. 318-9; Boyle, pp. 401-2).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT *[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%96ljait%C3%BC wikipedia]
