1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Amir Hasan was a Chupanid prince of the Ilkhanate in the early 14th century. He was the first son of Chupan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hasan had been made viceroy of Khurasan and Mazandaran during his father's lifetime. When Coban fled to Herat and was subsequently executed, Hasan took refuge with Ozbek Khan of the Blue Horde. He served under the khan and died of
2 CONC  wounds he received in battle. Hasan had three sons: Tales, the governor of Isfahan, Fars, and Kerman, who accompanied him to the Blue Horde and died there; Haji Beg; and Quc Hosayn, who was put to death in 1343 on the orders of t
2 CONC he Ilkhan Suleiman Khan.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The name Jalayir is derived from that of a large and important Mongol tribe. The dynasty of the same name, whose rule began in 740/1340 with Shaikh Hassan i Buzurg's seizure of power in Baghdad and ended with the death of Husain I
2 CONC I at the siege of Al Hilla, is also quite often referred to as the Ilka dynasty or the Ilkanids. The name originates with Shaikh Hassan's great grand father Ilge or Ilka Noyan.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Shaikh Hasan's great grand father's name was Ilge (<Ilka) Noyan who was also one of Hulegu's generals took a significant part in the mongol conquest of Central Asia and the Near East. His son, likewise, attained to high rank in t
2 CONC he military aristocracy of the Il Khanide empire and several of them were able to take princesses of the house of Hulegu in marriage. Thus Ilge's grand son Husain (d-1322) married a daughter of the Il-Khan Arghun named Oljetei who
2 CONC  became the mother of Shaikh Hasan, the father of the dynasty.] ref. page 5 of the Cambridge History of Iran - volume 6 http://bit.ly/1OVRDfX
2 CONT
2 CONT He is the strongest personality in the massive struggles for power which took place at the end of the Mongol rule in Iran. Conflicts with the Chobanids, especrially Hassan e Kucek (murdered in 1343) and the latter's brother, Malek
2 CONC  Ashraf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meme epoque que Abou Saeed Bahador
2 CONT
2 CONT Gouverneur de l'Azarbaidjan
2 CONT
2 CONT Ennemi de Arghun Aqa, mais soutient Toqa Temur
2 CONT
2 CONT Founder of the Jalayerid dynasty in Baghdad while his rival Hasan Kuchak Chupani rules Tabriz through Soleiman a puppet Ilkhan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally defeated by Sheikh Hassan Chupani (Kuchek)in 1361 AD, he went to Irak where he reigned to the end of his life. ref. page 194 Iranian History at a Glance  http://bit.ly/1Pu8qqA
2 CONT
2 CONT [Jalayirid Sultanate AD 1336 - 1432
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jalayirids were a Mongol successor state to the Il-Khans who managed to secure south-western Persia for themselves, ruling it from Baghdad. Their founder had been the governor of Anatolia until the death of the Il-Khan Sultan 
2 CONC Abu Said. While attempting to take control of Persia, they tried to maintain puppets on the throne from western Persia, always in opposition to their main rivals, the Chobanids in north-western Persia, while both sides used the su
2 CONC rviving Il-Khan Puppets themselves.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jalayir (or Yyalair) seem to have originated as a unit of the Darliqin Mongols, forming one of the three core tribes of the Khamag Mongol confederation which was a precursor to the Mongol empire. During the rise of the empire 
2 CONC they spread across Central Asia and also entered the Middle East under Chingiz Khan and his commanders in the thirteenth century. Those Jalayirs who remained in Central Asia eventually adopted Turkic language and could be found in
2 CONC  many of the subsequent Mongol splinter and successor states, starting with the Golden Horde. Those who ventured into Persia were responsible for founding the Jalayirid sultanate. Their name is the Mongolian version of a Turkic na
2 CONC me which was used to describe the ruling dynasty of the Second Uigur Kaganate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Included in the Jalayirid territories were parts of the collapsed Turkic sultanate of Rum, but the expanding Ottoman Turks had already taken much of the western Anatolian lands. Instead, the Jalayrids were confined to territory th
2 CONC at corresponded to modern Iraq and Iran (western Persia).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1336 - 1356 Tajuddin Hasan Buzurg Ex-governor of Anatolia. First sultan from 1340.] ref:http://www.historyfiles.co.uk/KingListsMiddEast/EasternJalayirids.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Ties to the Golden dynasty through his mother [ shaykh Hasan Uljata'i (the one from Uljatai) not Jalayirid or Ilkanid, names from his father's family] ref: p 156 Kingship and Ideology in the Islamic and Mongol worlds" by Anne F. B
2 CONC roadbridge http://bit.ly/1OVRDfX
2 CONT
2 CONT JALAYERIDS (sometimes called the Ilakāni by Persian historians), a dynasty of Mongol origin which ruled over Iraq, and for several decades also over north-western Persia, from the collapse of the Il-khanate in the late 1330s until
2 CONC  the early 15th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Hasan-i Buzurg
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Hasan, called "Buzurg" ("The Great"), was the first of several de facto independent Jalayirid rulers of Iraq and central Iran. He was the son of Husain and Öljetey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hasan Buzurg was married to Bagdad Katun the daughter of Amir Coban. Coban later became the most influential person at the court of Abu Sa'id Mirza. However, the Ilkhan soon became enamored with Bagdad Katun, and sought to have he
2 CONC r divorce Hasan Buzurg. Coban sent the two of them to Qarabagh[disambiguation needed] in an attempt to rid Bagdad Katun from Abu Sa'id's mind, but the effort failed, and Bagdad Katun was forced to marry the Ilkhan. After the murde
2 CONC r of Coban in 1327, Bagdad Katun and the Grand Vizier Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad competed for influence over Abu Sa'id. Ghiyah al-Din spread a rumor that Bagdad Katun and Hasan Buzurg were plotting against the Ilkhan; Hasan Buzurg wa
2 CONC s arrested in 1332. His mother convinced the Ilkhan to spare his life, and he was imprisoned in the castle of Kamakh. In 1333, however, he was cleared and was sent to be come governor of Rüm. During Abu Sa'id's lifetime, Hasan Buz
2 CONC urg was also called upon by Ghiyas al-Din to help stop rampant tax abuses in eastern Persia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the death of Abu Sa'id, several parties competed for the Ilkhanid throne. In 1336 Hasan Buzurg saw his chance and raised a child, Muhammad Khan, to claim the position. On July 24 Hasan Buzurg and Muhammed met the forces 
2 CONC of Musa Khan and 'Ali Padshah in the Ala-Tagh area; Musa was defeated and 'Ali Padshah killed. Hasan Buzurg pursued Musa on his way to Baghdad and created many losses for his enemy. He then proceeded to Tabriz, where he married th
2 CONC e granddaughter of Coban and the wife of Abu Sa'id, Delsad Katun, who was pregnant with Abu Sa'id's daughter. Meanwhile, the amirs in Khurasan adopted their own candidate for Ilkhan, Togha Temur. Togha set off in 1337 to subdue we
2 CONC stern Persia. Azarbaijan and 'Iraq-i 'Ajam were taken. In March he arrived before Sultaniyeh, the former capital of the Ilkhans, and Hasan Buzurg withdrew to Arran. Musa's forces, initially battling Togha's, now joined the invader
2 CONC . Togha and Musa met Hasan Buzurg at Soghurlug in the Maragheh area on June 15; Hasan defeated them, took Musa prisoner shortly after, and executed him. Togha gave up the campaign and withdrew to eastern Persia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly afterwards, however, several descendants of Coban united under his grandson Hasan Kucek, who used a slave to impersonate his father in order to legitimatize his cause. The Chobanids battled with Hasan Buzurg on July 16, 13
2 CONC 38 at Naushahr in the Alataq area. Hasan Buzurg was defeated; his puppet khan, Muhammed, was killed. Shortly afterwards, the Chobanids conquered Azarbaijan. A peace was declared, but Hasan Buzurg sought to be rid of the Chobanid t
2 CONC hreat. He offered the Ilkhanid throne to Togha Temur, who invaded early in 1339. Hasan Kucek, however, offered the hand of Sati Beg in marriage, and when Togha responded warmly to the proposal, he forwarded the letters to Hasan Bu
2 CONC zurg. The latter, enraged, halted his expedition to support Togha, who was forced to withdraw during the summer of that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT [The Chobanids were not completely wiped out from Persia. A daughter of Chupan's, Baghdad Katun, had caught the eye of Abu Sa'id. During Chupan's lifetime, she had been married to Hasan Buzurg, the future founder of the Jalayirids
2 CONC , but after Chupan fled Hasan Buzurg divorced her, and she married Abu Sa'id. She quickly gained influence over the Ilkhan and exercised the wide powers given to her. She was alleged (but never proven) to have been involved in any
2 CONC  conspiracies against the Ilkhan, but was believed by some to have caused Abu Sa'id's death in 1335. Abu Sa'id's successor Arpa Ke'un executed her.]
2 CONT
2 CONT [The amazing thing is that this prince was able to maintain his position in these troubled times right up to his death in 757/1356] ref The Cambridge History of Iran - volume 6 page 5 - The Jalayerids 
2 CONT
2 CONT BAGHDAD
2 CONT
2 CONT ii. From the Mongol Invasion to the Ottoman Occupation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baghdad on the eve of the Mongol invasion. The Mongol capture of Baghdad in 1258 came at a time when Persian influence was on the rise but the city as a whole in decline. The Persian influence had increased in recent decades throu
2 CONC gh Iranian viziers and officials serving the caliphs, the rise of Shiʿite power (especially in West Baghdad) and their theological literature, the spread of Sufi ideas and convents (ḵānaqāhs) as well as the patronage of Persian an
2 CONC d Persian poetry by some caliphs and their officials (Ṣafā, Adabiyāt III/1, pp. 118-24; Eqbāl, Tāriḵ-e Moḡol, pp. 178-81). But gradual decay, repeated fire and flood, and factionalism had deprived the metropolis and cultural cente
2 CONC r of Islam of much of its former glory (see PLATE 1). Sectarian fights between the Shiʿites and Sunnites were not infrequent, and in a recent incident, the son of the caliph al-Mostaʿṣem had massacred the Shiʿites of Karḵ and plun
2 CONC dered the shrine of Imam Musā al-Kāẓem. The mob (ʿāmma)and ʿayyārun (see ʿAYYĀR. ii) frequently engaged in looting, arson and murder (e. g., in 1246, 1255, 1256), and the caliphs and their officials remained weak and corrupt (Duri
2 CONC , p. 902). Baghdad had become a cluster of quarters, isolated from each other by wastelands, and only sparsely protected. East Baghdad (al-Šarqiya) was mainly Sunnite and more densely populated. It contained the caliphal quarter, 
2 CONC numerous mosques, madrasas, palaces and gardens, but a good part of it was also in ruins. A semicircular wall with both ends resting on the Tigris protected it. West Baghdad (al-Ḡarbiya) was mainly Shiʿite, but it consisted of a s
2 CONC eries of separate units, most notably Karḵ to the south, each surrounded by a wall and an area of wasteland. Most had their own Friday mosque. Only two bridges connected the two sections, and repeated floods (in 1243, 1248, 1253, 
2 CONC 1255, and the worst of them in 1256) greatly damaged the city’s quarters and hampered repairs (ibid., pp. 901-2 with literature).
2 CONT
2 CONT As was usual in gloomy times, apocalyptic ideas found fertile ground. “Disorder and lawlessness had engulfed Baghdad and its inhabitants had lost their affection for the Abbasids and hated them and interpreted the disasters as por
2 CONC tents of the end of their state, and had consequently become totally divided by partisanship” (Rašid-al-Din, p. 698). The Shiʿites alleged that in al-Jafr (said to have been the book on celestial conjunctions composed by Yaʿqub b.
2 CONC  Esḥāq al-Kendi, astrologer to al-Maʾmun), the history of the Abbasids had been fully forecast, with the indication that “the fall of Baghdad would take place in the middle of the seventh century [i.e., 13th cent. C.E.]” (Ebn Ḵald
2 CONC un, II, p. 218). The invasion by Hülegü (Hulāgu) was spurred on, some said, by Persians who co-operated with him (Ṣafā III/1, pp. 137-42; Eqbāl, pp. 186-88), partly out of their hatred for the caliphate because the Abbasids had ea
2 CONC rlier denied them help in their stand against the Mongol invaders (Ṣafā, p. 119 citing Ebn-al-Aṯir). Even some of the Persian ministers of the caliph were accused of betrayal (Ṣafā, pp. 137-41; Eqbāl, pp. 186-87). The Christians o
2 CONC f Baghdad were also suspected of collaboration, hoping, it was said, to terminate the Islamic caliphate at the hand of the pro-Christian Mongols (Menhāj-e Sarāj p. 705, cited by Ṣafā, p. 114; for the relations between Hülegü and t
2 CONC he Christians see Spuler, Mongolen2, pp. 206-14).
2 CONT
2 CONT The fall of Baghdad and its subsequent administration. The capture of Baghdad by Hülegü was a momentous event that shook the Muslim world. The city suffered heavily. Many of its inhabitants and country people who had taken refuge 
2 CONC there were put to the sword, and numerous buildings, including the Caliphal mosque (Jāmeʿ al-ḵolafāʾ) and the shrine of the Kāẓemayn, madrasas, and houses were ruined, and “Baghdad became a provincial centrer in all respects” (Dur
2 CONC i, p. 902; for detail see Boyle, pp. 346-9).
2 CONT
2 CONT With the capture of Baghdad, Persians became instrumental in the intellectual, cultural, and political revival of that city. The event did not affect a number of people who seem to have been Persian. For instance, the merchants wh
2 CONC o used to travel to Khorasan had already formed a connection with Mongol rulers and had secured decrees from them in their own favor. After the city fell, those merchants applied to the Mongol commanders, entreating them to appoin
2 CONC t guards to watch over their houses and to protect their lives and possessions and those of other individuals who had taken refuge with them (Ebn al-Fowaṭi, p. 329). The administration of Mesopotamia in general and Baghdad in part
2 CONC icular followed the normal Mongol system. Each province was given to an overall governor (wāli or ḥākem; for Khorasan and Mesopotamia the term used was wazir), a deputy-governor (nāʾeb), a military commander (šeḥna), and a number 
2 CONC of judges (Spuler, p. 339, n. 13). A complete list of such officials for ʿErāq-e ʿArab under the Il-khanids has been collected by ʿAzzāwi (pp. 201-370) and Spuler (pp. 348-51). The great number of Persians included in this list is
2 CONC  indicative of the important role they played in the life of Baghdad and Mesopotamia after the Mongol invasion. Only the šeḥnas of Baghdad were invariably chosen from the Mongol notables.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amongst the notable Persians whose houses were exempted from pillage and became places of refuge was Faḵr-al-Din, known as Ebn al-Dāmḡāni, the ṣāḥeb-diwān (approximately ‘Civil and Financial Administrator-General’ of Iraq under th
2 CONC e Caliph al-Mostaʿṣem [on the office see Spuler, Mongolen2, pp. 295, 309-11, 348-50; Boyle, pp. 336-7]). Hülegü reinstated him in his post, because, unlike Ebn-al-ʿAlqami, he was not accused of previous connection with the Mongols
2 CONC . Faḵr-al-Din died in 1259 and was succeeded by Najm-al-Din b. al-Moʿin. Two wālis also served at that time: Najm-al-Din Abu Jaʿfar Aḥmad ʿAmrān (called wazir-e rāstdeh) for East Baghdad and its suburbs, and Tāj-al-Din ʿAli b. al-
2 CONC Dawāmi for the Euphrates region. From 1260, the whole of Mesopotamia and Fārs were governed by a Mongol lord, Sujunjāq, but he usually delegated his authority to his nāʾeb, ʿAlāʾ-al-DinʿAṭā-Malek Jovayni (see below).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jovaynistewardship. The political sway of Persians in Baghdad after the Mongol takeover reached its high point during the tenure of ʿAlāʾ-al-Din ʿAṭā-Malek Jovayni (brother of Šams-al-Din Moḥammad Jovayni, the ṣāḥeb-diwān of t
2 CONC he Il-khanid empire during the reigns of Hülegü, Abaqa, and Aḥmad Tegüder [Takudār]). In 1258/59, Hülegu appointed as deputy-governor of Mesopotamia and Khuzistan his own secretary, the learned historian and shrewd politician ʿAlā
2 CONC ʾ-al-Din ʿAṭā-Malek Jovayni. He was not the sole authority in Baghdad, however: a certain ʿEmād-al-Din ʿOmar b. Moḥammad Qazvini shared the administration of the city. With those nominations Baghdad and Iraq came under the civil a
2 CONC nd fiscal control of Persians, although they were nominally subordinate to the Mongol military commander of Baghdad. In practice, Sujunjāq left the running of the affairs mainly in the hand of ʿAlāʾ-al-Din (Eqbāl, pp. 200-1), whic
2 CONC h task he performed with remarkable efficiency and determination, so much so that he was at time called the ṣāḥeb-diwān of ʿErāq (Spuler, p. 341). ʿAlāʾ-al-Din was a benevolent official and truly loyal in furthering the Il-khanid 
2 CONC regime. But his position and prestige provoked envy among his colleagues, both Mongol and Persian, and several times he was the object of plots by them. One of his great enemies was the nominated leader (naqib) of the ʿAlids, Tāj-
2 CONC al-Din ʿAli b. Moḥammad ʿAlawi, better known as Ebn al-Ṭeqṭaqā, who, having acquired great wealth, requested Abaqa Khan to dismiss ʿAlāʾ-al-Din. His missive fell into the hands of the latter, who secretly had him murdered by a gan
2 CONC g outside the walls of Baghdad. That happened while Abaqa Khan was in the city. ʿAlāʾ-al-Din subsequently had the murderers of this eminent man put to death, but he confiscated his landed properties on the pretext that he had tax 
2 CONC arrears.
2 CONT
2 CONT ʿAlāʾ-al-Din and his brother suffered greatly because of the intrigues of another opponent, Majd-al-Molk Yazdi, who had been nominated Mošref-al-mamālek, i.e., Controller-General of revenues for the entire Il-khanid state. Althoug
2 CONC h Majd-al-Molk owed his rise and position to Šams-al-Din Moḥammad Jovayni, the ṣāḥeb-diwān, he proved to be the most ardent enemy of the Jovayni brothers. He accused them of being in league with the Mamluks of Egypt, and Yesü-Buqā
2 CONC , a son-in-law of Hülegü, brought the charge to the notice of Abaqa. When it could not be substantiated, the brothers were restored to favor (Boyle, p. 363; cf. Eqbāl, p. 201). In 1279 Majd-al-Molk again accused the brothers of th
2 CONC e same treason and added that Šams-al-Din had embezzled huge sums (2,500,000 dinars) from the Treasury. Hülegü, then in Syria, tried ʿAlāʾ-al-Din and issued his death warrant, but later pardoned him.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were saved by the intercession of a wife of Abaqa (Boyle, p. 363; Eqbāl, pp. 217-8; Spuler, p. 348). After several more attempts of this nature, Majd-al-Molk himself was exposed and brutally killed when Solṭān Aḥmad Tegüder a
2 CONC scended the throne, and ʿAlāʾ-al-Din was set free and his properties restored, and he was once again installed as governor of Baghdad. But this latest success was short-lived. One of the plotters was Aṟḡun, the eldest son of Abaqa
2 CONC , who sought to seize power from the new Il-khan Solṭān Aḥmad Tegüder and saw the Jovayni brothers as obstacles to his cause (Spuler, p. 79). He came to Baghdad in 1283, revived the old accusations and began arresting and torturin
2 CONC g ʿAla-al-Din’s agents. The news reached the latter while in Arrān, and he died of a stroke (Barthold-Boyle, p. 606; Eqbāl, pp. 220-24). He was buried in Tabriz. He had lived for sixty years and had held the office of ṣāḥeb-diwān 
2 CONC of Iraq and Baghdad for 21 years and a few months.
2 CONT
2 CONT ʿAlāʾ-al-Din came from a scholarly family with a long history of vizierate at the courts of various dynasties (see Barthold-Boyle, p. 606) and, like his father and brother, had been for years in the service of Mongol rulers. He ha
2 CONC d spent some time in Mongolia at the court of the Great Khan and had commenced there (in 1252) the composition of his masterfully documented history of the Mongol conquest (Tāriḵʾ-e jahān-gošāy), which he completed in about 1260 (
2 CONC ibid., p. 607). With this background, he was eminently qualified to run the affairs of the state in the domain entrusted to him. In addition, ʿAlāʾ-al-Din showed shrewd diplomacy. He married Šams-al-Żoḥḥā, the widow of the Abbasid
2 CONC  prince Abu’l-ʿAbbās Aḥmad, probably to gain the favor of the common people in Baghdad because she was a charitable lady and benevolent toward them (Ebn Fowaṭi, p. 410). The union proved effective in strengthening his standing amo
2 CONC ng the populace. During ʿAlāʾ-al-Din’s tenure, she endeavored to aid them and to supply their needs. She founded a madrasa outside Baghdad and, naming it ʿEṣmatiya, dedicated it to the followers of all the four schools of Sunni la
2 CONC w in an attempt to bring about some reconciliation between them. Close by she had a mausoleum built for herself, wherein she was buried in 1279. Her daughter by Abu’l-ʿAbbās Aḥmad, Rābeʿa, was married in 670/1271-72 to Šaraf-al-Di
2 CONC n Hārun, son of Šams- al-Din the ṣāḥeb-diwān, receiving a marriage settlement (ṣadāq) of 100,000 gold dinars, and with the stipulation by her mother that the bridegroom should not drink wine, a condition that was accepted. Rābeʿa 
2 CONC died in 1286 and was buried beside her mother. A little later her husband was killed by Aṟḡun’s order to exterminate Šam-al-Din’s family members.
2 CONT
2 CONT ʿAlāʾ-al-Din worked hard for the prosperity of Iraq and for the revival of Baghdad (see ʿAzzāwi, pp. 236-314; Spuler, p. 348 with further references). It is said that he spent 10,000 gold dinars in digging a canal linking Anbār to
2 CONC  Kufa and Najaf and founded 150 villages along its banks (Barthold-Boyle, p. 606). He also abolished the poll tax that the Mongols had levied on the people of Baghdad (Ebn al-Fowaṭi, p. 239). In 1266 he built, outside Baghdad and 
2 CONC opposite Ẓafariya Gate, a mansion with porticoes and a bathhouse, the whole surrounded by a large garden where various fruit trees (even pistachios) were cultivated (ibid., p. 358). Two years later he ordered the construction of a
2 CONC  water mill below the dyke of Mostanṣeriya College, by means of which system water was raised from the Tigris and transferred to a cistern, from which it would flow into the pond in the College courtyard, and thence to another cis
2 CONC tern outside the College in front of Ayvān-e sāʿāt. The surface covering the open space in the college itself, as well as the paintings on its walls, were repaired. These works were carried out and supervised by an Iranian named Š
2 CONC ams-al-Din ʿAmid Ḵorāsāni. ʿAlāʾ-al-Din also restored the dyke of Qamariya Mosque (on the west bank of the Tigris), which had been ruined under al-Mostaʿṣem. In 1271 he ordered the minaret of the Jāmeʿ al-Ḵolafāʾ to be rebuilt. A 
2 CONC month after its completion it collapsed, but fortunately caused no casualty (Ebn al-Fowaṭi, p. 371). It was rebuilt by order of Šams-al-Din the ṣāḥeb-diwān in 1279. In 1271 a fire heavily damaged the Neẓāmiya College in Baghdad an
2 CONC d killed many people. ʿAlāʾ-al-Din ordered the reconstruction of the college building out of its endowments. He likewise constructed an inn (rebāt) at Najaf. He further built a maʾman (‘place of safety’, i.e., a warehouse complex 
2 CONC with a divān or office, mosque, caravansery, bathhouse and market) at a place called Nahr Jaʿfar for the merchants who used to bring in their goods from Baṣra (ibid., p. 372). When in 1276 many fires broke out in Baghdad markets a
2 CONC nd houses, ʿAlāʾ-al-Din caused cisterns to be built in various districts and streets; these were kept filled with water to be used in extinguishing future fires.
2 CONT
2 CONT During of ʿAlāʾ-al-Din’s twenty-one years of administration, several Persians were appointed to high juridical positions in Baghdad. The first was Neẓām-al-Din Bondijāyeni, the Supreme Judge (qāżi al-qożāt) of Baghdad (Spuler, p. 
2 CONC 348). His successors were Serāj-al-Din Hanāyesi, Qāżi Faḵr-al-Din ʿAbd-Allāh b. ʿAbd-al-Jalil Ṭehrāni, deputy of Neẓām-al-Din Bondijāyeni, who was also nominated as inspector of weights and measures (moḥtaseb) of Baghdad, and Qāżi
2 CONC  Neẓām-al-Din Moḥammad Heravi, titled Šayḵ-al-Eslām, who was the judge of West Baghdad from 1274 to 1279.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the Persians who took a part in the administration of Baghdad was the famous mathematician and philosopher Ḵᵛāja Naṣer-al-Din Ṭusi. He visited the city in 1263-4 to look into the state of pious foundations or endowments (aw
2 CONC qāf), and to inspect the situation of the soldiers and the slaves. From there he made for Wāseṭ and Baṣra. He collected in Iraq a large number of books for the observatory that he was setting up in Marāḡa (Ebn al-Fowaṭi, p. 350). 
2 CONC Four years later he revisited Baghdad, this time accompanying Abaqa Khan, after whose departure he remained there, inspecting and reorganizing the awqāf, paying monthly salaries and the pensions (aḵbāz, lit. ‘pieces of bread’) of 
2 CONC the jurists, theology teachers and Sufis (ibid., p. 376). It seems that after the death of Ḵᵛāja Naṣir-al-Din the supervision of awqāf in Iraq was entrusted to his son Ṣadr-al-Din and to his brothers, who held it for a long time e
2 CONC xcept for a brief period when it was assigned to the “governor” of Baghdad (ibid., p. 456).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1283 Ḵᵛāja Šaraf-al-Din Hārun succeeded his uncle at Baghdad while Šams-al-Din Zardeyār became deputy-governor (Spuler, p. 349). The inhabitants, who liked Ḵᵛāja Šaraf-al-Din on account of his marriage to the daughter of Aḥmad,
2 CONC  son of al-Mostaʿṣem, welcomed him, and poets composed poems in his praise (Ebn al-Fowaṭi, p. 423). The same writer (p. 427) commends him for following his uncle’s policy characterized by “justice and good conduct.” He was so lear
2 CONC ned that on one of his earlier visits to Baghdad he had occupied the “chair” in Neẓāmiya College and taught for an audience, which included his uncle and other senior officials as well as theology teachers, scholars, and jurists. 
2 CONC Like his father, Šaraf-al-Din patronized science, art and literature. He continued to honor and patronize Ṣafi-al-Din ʿAbd-al-Moʾmen Ormavi, the famous Persian musicologist, who had served the caliph al-Mostaʿṣem and after him Hül
2 CONC egü and Šams-al-Din. This artist died in 1285 at the age of eighty. Almost immediately, Šaraf-al-Din himself was captured and killed by the newly enthroned Aṟḡun (see above).
2 CONT
2 CONT The tenure of Saʿd-al-Dawla. Aṟḡun appointed the two Mongol princes Jüškeb and Baidu (Bāydu) to the government of Baghdad and Diārbakr respectively. In the same year he sent over a Mongol chief named Tunskā to Baghdad as military 
2 CONC governor. The latter appointed a Persian Jew, Saʿd-al-Dawla, son of Hebat-Allāh Abhari, as his own lieutenant and also as his ḥājeb (major-domo). This extraordinarily capable and efficient person was soon running the affairs of Ba
2 CONC ghdad so well that the governor, Qotloḡš@ā@h, was unable to assert himself (Rašid-al-Din Faẓl-Allāh, p. 2080). Consequently, Saʿd-al-Dawla’s opponents suggested to Aṟḡun that, being also an incomparable physician, Saʿd-al-Dawla ha
2 CONC d better wait on the Il-khan; Aṟḡun accepted the suggestion, and summoned him to Tabriz. When in attendance on Aṟḡun, Saʿd-al-Dowla accused his enemies in Baghdad of mishandling fiscal affairs, and undertook to collect from Baghda
2 CONC d tax arrears amounting to 500 tumans (5 million dinars). Accordingly, Aṟḡun dispatched him to Baghdad, where he exacted that sum from suitable donors with the help of beating and torture, and presented it to the Il-khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 687/1287-8 Aṟḡun entrusted the governorship of Baghdad to Arduqiā, one of Saʿd-al-Dowla’s allies, and concurrently nominated a certain Bāydu as the šeḥna, Šaraf-al-Din Semnāni as the “malek of Baghdad,” and Saʿd-al-Dawla as the
2 CONC  mošref. A year later Arduqiā and Saʿd-al-Dawla brought Aṟḡun a coffer full of money and other valuables. Elated, Aṟḡun ordered all the opponents of Saʿd-al-Dawla to be killed and appointed him to the vizierate. Saʿd-al-Dawla reac
2 CONC hed a very high position in the administration, and assumed semi-regal power. He entrusted the government of Baghdad to his brother Faḵr-al-Dawla, and appointed as his aides Mohaḏḏeb-al-Dawla and Jamāl-al-Din Dastejerdiāni. But Sa
2 CONC ʿd-al-Dowla’s power did not last long; in 1291 when Aṟḡun fell ill (he died in the same year), Saʿd-al-Dawla was overpowered and killed by his enemies. Faḵr-al-Dawla, and Mohaḏḏeb-al-Dawla were arrested and tortured to reveal thei
2 CONC r treasures, and then executed (Ebn al-Fowaṭi, pp. 446-7).
2 CONT
2 CONT Baghdad in the later Il-khanid period. Geyḵātu, the second son of Abaqa Khan, succeeded Aṟḡun. His reign was marked by revolts and financial problems. His governor of Baghdad was a certain Qawām-al-Molk, a cousin of Qoṭb-al-Din, g
2 CONC overnor of Tabriz, while two Mongol amirs were responsible for financial administration (Spuler, p. 350). Geyḵātu’s murder in 1294 was followed by political unrest. In the same year, through the efforts of Amir Nowruz, Aṟḡun’s eld
2 CONC est son Ḡāzān embraced Islam in the presence of Ṣadr-al-Din Ebrāhim Ḥamuʾi, and in Ḏu’l-ḥejja of the same year, again thanks to Amir Nowruz’s endeavor, he ascended the throne. Two Persians, Emām-al-Din Yaḥyā Qazvini and Faḵr-al-Di
2 CONC n al-Rāzi al-ʿAlawi, were entrusted with the administration of Mesopotamia (including Baghdad). A year later, Nur-al-Din ʿAbd-al-Raḥmān b. Tāšān became the governor and ṣāḥeb-diwān. In the following year,Jamāl-al-Din Dastejerdiāni
2 CONC  was appointed the governor and his brother ʿEmād-al-Din replaced Nur-al-Din, who had recently died. Ḡāzān sent Amir Toḥta as governor of Mesopotamia and executed Dastejerdiāni, giving his place and the title ṣāḥeb-diwān to Zayn-a
2 CONC l-Din Moḥammad Ḵāledi, brother of Ṣadr-al-Din (Spuler, p. 350 with literature). In the meantime, Ḡāzān himself came to Baghdad, whence he made the pilgrimage of the tombs of Emām Musā al-Kāẓem and Abu Ḥanifa and attended the Frida
2 CONC y prayers held at Suq-al-Solṭan mosque. He left Baghdad after a few months. A year later Ḡāzān put Ṣadr-al-Din Ḵāledi to death and had his fugitive brother Zayn-al-Din captured and killed. In the same year, Shaikh Ẓahir-al-Din Kāz
2 CONC eruni, a learned man who had performed various important administrative services in Baghdad, passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1298 Ḡāzān revisited Baghdad, and engaged in works of public utility. He ordered a canal to be dug upstream of Ḥella, and named it Nahr Ḡāzāni. He also ordered fiscal reforms, including the stabilization of the weight of gold a
2 CONC nd silver coins. Emām-al-Din Yaḥyā Qazvini’s office was made independent, with a view to guaranteeing Baghdad’s tax revenues. According to Kāšāni (p. 82), Buluḡān Ḵātun Ḵorāsāni, daughter of Amir Tesu, had built a town near Baghda
2 CONC d, and had named it Ḵorāsān. After her death, a ḵānaqāh was built there, the custodianship of which was entrusted to Ḵᵛāja Rašid-al-Din Fażl-Allāh Hamadāni.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 708/1308 the vizier Saʿd-al-Din Sāvaji, wishing to discredit and eventually to eliminate Ḵᵛāja Aṣil-al-Din, son of Ḵᵛāja Naṣir-al-Din Ṭusi, appointed him governor of Baghdad and Iraq: no sooner had Aṣil-al-Din settled down in B
2 CONC aghdad than he gave way under the pressure of drafts and payment orders issued to him as part of Saʿd-al-Din’s machination. Ḡāzān’s successor Öljeitü (r. 1304-1316) set out for Baghdad in 1309, on the way visiting the Ayvān-e Madā
2 CONC ʾen (the remains of the Sassanian palace at Ctesiphon), where he knelt three times in front of the Ṭāq-e Kesrā. In Baghdad, at the instigation of Saʿd-al-Din Sāvaji, he committed Aṣil-al-Din to be tortured, bringing to an end the 
2 CONC influence of the family of Ḵᵛāja Naṣer-al-Din Ṭusi. Öljeitü’s devotional shift from Sunnism to Shiʿism occurred at this time (see Eqbāl, pp. 318-9; Boyle, pp. 401-2).
2 CONT
2 CONT Baghdad had a famous textile factory named Ferdows Factory. Saʿd-al-Din Sāvaji nominated a rival, the vizier Tāj-al-Din ʿAlišāh Tabrizi, as manager of this factory in order to keep him away from the court. When Öljeitü visited Bag
2 CONC hdad in 1311, Saʿd-al-Din charged one of his close associates to audit the account of the factory and to point out the malpractice of Tāj-al-Din. The latter, outmaneuvering Saʿd-al-Din, displayed and then presented to Öljeitü rich
2 CONC  costumes and pieces of fine cloth made there. Saʿd-al-Din apparently snubbed Tāj-al-Din and Ḵᵛājā Rašid-al-Din Fażl-Allāh, also present there, which contributed to his later downfall and execution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Öljeitü was succeeded in 1316 by his son Abu Saʿid, then ten or twelve years old, with Amir Čobān (Čubān/Čupān) acting as regent. Early during Abu Saʿid’s reign, Kᵛāja Rašid-al-Din Fażl-Allāh, who had long served Ḡāzān and Öljeitü
2 CONC  as vizier, was put to death at the instigation of Tāj-al-Din and some other officials. In 737/1326, Abu Saʿid entrusted the vizierate to Rašid-al-Din’s son Ḡiāṯ-al-Din Moḥammad. Ḡiāṯ-al-Din was “angelic in nature” and forgiving i
2 CONC n the extreme (Boyle, p. 412 citing Mostawfi). He built in Baghdad the mosque known as Jāmeʿ Moḥammad Fażl, which still stands in Roṣāfa district (ʿAzzāwi, II, pp. 47-48). In 1340 a part of the Ṭāq-e Kesrā in Madāʾen collapsed (ib
2 CONC id., II, p. 49, quoting Taqwim al-tawāriḵ). Early in Ḡiāṯ-al-Din’s tenure, a Persian named Serāj-al-Din ʿOmar Qazvini built a Friday mosque in Baghdad, and to this day a quarter in the city bears the name Serāj-al-Din. Ebn Baṭṭuṭa
2 CONC  reports having visited this mosque, where he heard that very Shaikh Serāj-al-Din preach (ʿAzzāwi, II, p. 165). Abu Saʿid died in 1335, and the disintegration of Il-khanid power and a period of civil wars followed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jalayerids’ rule in Baghdad. ShaikhḤasan who had divorced his wife Baḡdād Ḵātun, Amir Čobān’s daughter, on Abu Saʿid’s orders, was the son of AmirḤosayn called Gurkān (‘son-in-law,’ since he was Aṟḡun Khan’sson-in-law), son of
2 CONC  Āqbuqā, son of Ilkā Nuyān, of the Jalāyer, one of the large clans of the Mongols. After Abu Saʿid’s death, ShaikhḤasan rose in rebellion and succeeded in establishing his rule in Iraq by about 1340 and founding the Jalayerid dyna
2 CONC sty (Boyle, p. 415). Better known as Shaikh Ḥasan-e Bozorg, he reigned till 1357. His relative, MalekAšraf Čubāni (grandson of Amir Čobān), ruler of Azerbaijan, attacked him in 1347, but he fortified Baghdad, and with the help of 
2 CONC his wife Delšād Ḵātun and his aide Ḵᵛāja Marjān defied all assaults on the city. Under the Jalayerids Baghdad became the capital of a Persian dynasty. Ḥasan-e Bozorg left a good name, and was praised for his equity and his fair po
2 CONC licy. He did a great deal for the prosperity of Baghdad and the welfare of its inhabitants (ʿAzzāwi, II, p. 79). He welcomed the Persian poet Ḵᵛāju Kermāni, and earned his praise. The poet’s Divān contains pleasant memories from h
2 CONC is days at his court. When Ḥasan died, another famous poet, Salmān Sāvaji, composed a moving elegy. His governor of Baghdad, Kᵛāja Marjān, a former Greek slave belonging to Öljeitü (hence his neṣba Oljāyeti), also sponsored the bu
2 CONC ilding of many charitable establishments in the city, including a mosque(with a madrasa appended to it), and a hospital (see the detailed study in ʿAzzāwi, II, pp. 84-129). The Marjān madrasa /mosque still exists. It has two stori
2 CONC es built in the same fashion as that of the Neẓāmiya College. With its exquisite architecture, calligraphic inscription, and decoration, it seems to have been designed by Iranian architects. Certainly, the endowment inscriptions (
2 CONC dated 758/1357) set into its walls were executed by a famous Persian artist named Aḥmad-šāh the Tabrizi Painter (ibid., II, pp. 84-94). A caravansary he built in Baghdad also survives and still functions (Blair and Bloom, pl. 22).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ref page 194 de  The Book of Iran. Iranian History at a Glance2005 by Reza Shabani and Mahmood Farrokhpey
2 CONT
2 CONT The founder of the dynasty of the Jayerides was Sheikh Hassan Jalayeri or Sheikh Hassan e Bozorg. He was the son of Amir Hossein Gurkan and the nephew of Abquqa II Kanuyan. Sultan Abu Sayeed capture Sheikh Hassan's wife, Baqdad Kh
2 CONC atun, and emprisonned Sheikh Hassan for a while. After Sultan Abu Sayeed, Sheikh Hassan was among the claimers of power. His major rival was Sheikh Hassan e Chupani. Finally Sheikh Hassan Jalayeri was defeated by Sheikh Hassan e C
2 CONC hupani in 1361 AD and went to Iraq where he reigned to the end of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Les Jalerides ont regne de 1361 a 1457
