1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Shiburgan Governor of Iraq in 1339
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Amir Chupan's 6th son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon her brother's (Abu Said) accession in 1316, Sati Beg was betrothed to the amir Chupan (Coban), one of the most powerful individuals in the Ilkhanid court. They were wed in 1319; their marriage produced a son, Surgan. When Chu
2 CONC pan (Coban) and Abu Sa'id came into conflict in 1327, Sati Beg was returned to the Ilkhan. Chupan (Coban) was executed that same year at Abu Sa'id's insistence; Sati Beg and Surgan were spared.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Abu Sa'id's death in 1335, the Ilkhanate began to disintegrate. By 1336, Sati Beg and Surgan had taken the side of the founder of the Jalayirid dynasty, Hasan Buzurg. After the latter seized control of western Persia, Su
2 CONC rgan was made governor of Karabakh (in modern Azerbaijan), where he and his mother moved to. However, when a grandson of Coban, Hasan Kucek, defeated Hasan Buzurg in July 1338, Sati Beg and Surgan defected to his camp. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hasan Kucek was murdered late in 1343; Sati Beg's son Surgan found himself competing for control of the Chobanid lands with the late ruler's brother Malek Asraf and his uncle Yagi Basti. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was defeated by Malek Asraf, he fled to his mother and stepfather (Arpa Keun?). The three of them then formed an alliance, but when Hasan Buzurg decided to withdraw the support he promised, the plan fell apart, and they fl
2 CONC ed to Diyarbakr. Surgan was defeated again in 1345 by Malek Asraf and they fled to Anatolia. Coinage dating from that year appears in Hesn Kayfa in Sati Beg's name; this is the last trace of her. Surgan moved from Anatolia to Bagh
2 CONC dad, where he was eventually executed by Hasan Buzurg; Sati Beg may have suffered the same fate, but this is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sorghan was executed by Hassan Bozorg in Diyarbekir 1345.   Governor of Qarabagh 1336.   Governor of Iraq 1340.
2 CONT ====
2 CONT Après la mort d'Abu Sa'id en 3 AH, le gouvernement patriarcal turco-azerbaïdjanais a commencé à se désintégrer. Sati Beg et son fils se sont rangés du côté de Hassan Bozorg, fondateur de la dynastie des Jalairi. Après la victoire 
2 CONC de Hassan Bozorg, Surqan a atteint la province du Karabakh au nord de l'Azerbaïdjan. (ref: http://terken-terim.blogspot.com/2007/03/bgim-xatn-sat-bk-xan.html) 
2 CONT More about this source textSource text required for additional translation information Send feedback Side panels
