1 NOTE {geni:about_me} in  " Histoire Secrète des Mongols "  ~" Secrète " au sens de " Réservée " aux descendants de Gengis~Qahan ~~Restitution du texte mongol et traduction française des chapitres I à VI,   Paul Pelliot,   Paris,   1949
2 CONC .  (  oeuvres posthumes  )
2 CONT
2 CONT Chapitre 1. 
2 CONT
2 CONT §48 -  Le fils unique de Baï~shïngqor~doqshin, Tumbïnaï~sätchän  ( Mafflu~le~Sagace )  eut deux fils,  Qabul~qahan et   Säm~Qatchülä  ( Furtif~Durard ) .  Le fils de Säm~Qatchülä  fut   Bültätchü~ba'atur  ( Tir~au~Flanc~le~Preux )
2 CONC  .  Qabul~qahan eut sept fils ;  l'aîné était  Ökin~barqaq  ( Fille~Barqaq ),  et les autres  Bartan~ba'atur  ( Bartan~le~Preux ) ,  Qutuqtu~münggür  ( Bienheureux~Münggür ) ,  Qutula~qahan  ( l'Empereur~Qutula ) ,  Qulan  (Hémion
2 CONC e ) ,  Qada'an  ( Duraille ) et Tödö'än otshïgïn  ( Renfort~le~Benjamin )  ;  tels sont les sept .
2 CONT
2 CONT §49 -  Le fils de Ökin~barqaq   fut   Qutuqtu~yürki  ( Vigilant~le~Coeur~Vaillant ) .  Qutuqtu~yürki  eut deux fils,  Sätchä~bäki  ( Sagace~le~Bey ) et  Taïtchu ( Prince ) ;  c'est eux qui eurent le nom de clan de Yürki  ( Les~Coe
2 CONC urs~Vaillants ) .
2 CONT
2 CONT §50 -  Bartan~ba'atur  ( Bartan~le~Preux )  eut les quatre fils suivants :  Mänggätu~Kïyan  ( Tavelure~le~Kiyan ) ,  Näkün~taïdjï  ( Prince~Servant ) ,  Yäsügäi~ba'atur  ( Neuvain~le~Preux ) et  Daritaï~otchïgïn  ( Ulcéreux~le~Ben
2 CONC jamin ) .  Le fils de  Qutuqtu~münggür  fut   Büri~bökö  ( Loup~le~Lutteur ) ;  lors du festin dans la forêt du fleuve Onon,  c'est ce Büri~bökö  qui fendit l'épaule de  Bälgütäi  ( Faste ) .
2 CONT
2 CONT §51 -  Qutula~qahan  ( l'Empereur~Qutula ) eut trois fils,  Djötchi  ( L'Hôte ) ,  Gïrma'u  ( Incapable ) et  Altan  ( Or ) .  Le fils de Qulan~ba'atur  ( Hémione ) fut  Yäkä~Tchärän  ( Grande~Longévité )  ;  c'est lui qui était l
2 CONC e maître des deux "darqan"  ("exempts ")   Badaï  et    Kïshïlïq  ( Intrépide ) .  Qada'an  ( Duraille ) et  Tödö'än~ötshïgïn  ( Renfort~le~Benjamin )  furent tous deux sans descendance.
2 CONT The **Borjigin** clan, to which Qaidu Khan belonged, is a significant lineage in Mongol history, including notable figures like Genghis Khan and his descendants. Here’s an overview of the family tree connecting **Orus**, Qaidu Kha
2 CONC n, and the **Bayshinghor-Doqshin Borjigin** lineage:
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Borjigin Family Tree
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. **Bayshinghor (Baysinghor) Borjigin**
2 CONT    - **Role**: Bayshinghor, also known as Baysinghor, was a prominent member of the Borjigin clan. His name appears in various historical records and genealogies related to the Mongol Empire.
2 CONT    - **Time Period**: His exact dates are not well-documented, but he lived around the late 12th to early 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. **Doqshin Borjigin**
2 CONT    - **Role**: Doqshin is another figure within the Borjigin lineage. His connections are often mentioned in genealogical records related to the Mongol aristocracy.
2 CONT    - **Time Period**: Similar to Bayshinghor, Doqshin’s dates are not precisely documented but are generally around the early to mid-13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. **Orus Borjigin**
2 CONT    - **Role**: Orus was a prominent Mongol chieftain and the father of Qaidu Khan. He was part of the Borjigin clan and played a role in the early Mongol Empire.
2 CONT    - **Time Period**: His role was significant during the late 12th and early 13th centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. **Qaidu Khan**
2 CONT    - **Role**: Qaidu Khan was the son of Orus and a prominent leader in the Chagatai Khanate, one of the four principal khanates of the Mongol Empire. His rule was marked by his efforts to assert control over the Mongol territorie
2 CONC s and his conflicts with the Yuan dynasty.
2 CONT    - **Time Period**: He ruled approximately from 1260 to 1301 CE.
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Family Tree Lineage Summary
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. **Bayshinghor Borjigin**
2 CONT    - Ancestor to several notable figures in the Borjigin clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. **Doqshin Borjigin**
2 CONT    - Likely a contemporary or slightly later than Bayshinghor.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. **Orus Borjigin** (Son of Doqshin or Bayshinghor)
2 CONT    - Father of **Qaidu Khan**.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. **Qaidu Khan** (Son of Orus)
2 CONT    - Prominent leader in the Chagatai Khanate.
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Key Points
2 CONT
2 CONT - The **Borjigin** clan is the broader lineage to which Qaidu Khan belongs. The clan's influence spans several generations, with various figures contributing to the history of the Mongol Empire.
2 CONT - **Orus Borjigin**, as the father of Qaidu Khan, links the lineage to the leadership of the Chagatai Khanate and the broader Mongol political structure.
2 CONT - **Bayshinghor** and **Doqshin** are important figures in the Borjigin clan’s history, though their exact roles and dates are less well-documented compared to later figures like Genghis Khan and his direct descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT The precise genealogical connections between these figures can be complex and sometimes speculative due to the nature of historical records and the oral traditions of the Mongol aristocracy.
