1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrios_Doukas_Komnenos_Koutroules
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Demetrios''', later renamed '''Michael, Doukas Komnenos Koutroules Angelos''' (Greek: Δημήτριος (Μιχαήλ) Δούκας Κομνηνός Κουτρούλης Ἄγγελος; fl. 1278–1304) was the third son of the ruler of Epirus, Michael II Komnenos Doukas (r
2 CONC uled 1230–68), also surnamed Koutroules, and his wife Theodora of Arta.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1278, he married Anna Komnene Palaiologina, the daughter of the Byzantine emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos (r. 1259–82), and received from his father-in-law the supreme dignity of Despot.[2][3] From this marriage, he had two so
2 CONC ns, Andronikos and Constantine. From a second marriage to a daughter of George I Terter, Tsar of Bulgaria, he had several children more.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned as fighting in the ranks of the Byzantine army against the troops of Charles of Anjou in the Siege of Berat, as well as twenty years later against the Alans.[2] In 1304, he was accused of conspiring against Emperor
2 CONC  Andronikos II Palaiologos (r. 1282–1328) and was imprisoned. Nothing further is known of him.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ALBANIA.htm#DemetriosAdied1304
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         '''DEMETRIOS Doukas Komnenos Angelos''' (-after 13 Mar 1304).  Pachymeres records that "Michaeli…despotæ" had three legitimate sons, of whom "Nicephorus et Demetrius"[83].  He adopted the name '''MIKHAEL''', and was kno
2 CONC wn as "Kutrules" after the death of his father.  Emperor Mikhael VIII, hoping to use Mikhael against his brother Nikephoros, allied himself with Charles I King of Sicily [Anjou-Capet], awarded Mikhael the title despot in 1278 and 
2 CONC married him to his daughter[84].  Pachymeres records that "Michaelis despotæ" was accused of treason and imprisoned, dated to 13 Mar 1304[85].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly (Dispensation Nov 1278) ANNA Komnene Palaiologina, daughter of Emperor MIKHAEL VIII & his wife Theodora Doukaina Komnene Palaiologina Batatzaina (1260-[1299/1300]).  Ioannes Kantakuzenus records that "Michaeli duci
2 CONC " married "Annam sororem Andronici senioris"[86].  A synodal decision dated Nov 1278 granted dispensation for the marriage “résultant de leur sixième degré d’affinité”[87].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (1301) as her second husband, ANA of Bulgaria, repudiated wife of STEFAN UROŠ II MILUTIN King of Serbia, daughter of GEORGI I Tsar of the Bulgarians & his first wife Maria ---.  Pachymeres records that "Sphentisth
2 CONC labus Bulgarorum" arranged the marriage of "repudiatæ a crale Serbiæ suæ sororis, Terteris filiæ" and "Michaelem despotam"[88].  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mikhael "Kutrules" & his first wife had [three] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
