1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Share some things about...........
2 CONT Kunigunda Rostislavna Přemyslovna (Arpad) is our third cousin 21 times removed.
2 CONT Janet Milburn 5/12/23
2 CONT ___________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda of Slavonia
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda Rostislavna (1245 – September 9, 1285) [Czech: Kunhuta] was Queen consort of Bohemia and its regent from 1278 until her death. She was a member of the House of Chernigov, and a daughter of the ruler of Slavonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was presumably born in Russia, in the domains of her paternal grandfather Michael of Chernigov, Grand Prince of Kiev, to Rostislav Mihailovich, future ruler of Belgrade and Slavonia, and his wife Anna of Hungary. After the dea
2 CONC th of her father's father, Kunigunda's family relocated to Hungary, where her mother's father, Bela IV of Hungary, made her father governor of certain Serbian-speaking regions in the Danube Valley. Her father proclaimed himself Ki
2 CONC ng of Bulgaria in 1256 but did not stay there to defend his kingship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda was married to King Premysl Otakar II of Bohemia (ca. 1233 – 1278) in Bratislava on October 25, 1261, following his divorce from Margaret, Duchess of Austria (ca. 1204 – 1266) who was elderly and barren, and unable to pr
2 CONC ovide heirs for the King. Kunigunda, 41 years Margaret's junior, bore Otakar several children, the youngest being his only legitimate son Wenceslaus (Vaclav, 1271-1305).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Life as Queen
2 CONT
2 CONT Genuine heirs of the Babenberg asserted their rights against King Otakar in Austria. The young Kunigunda's grandfather Bela IV had been Otakar's rival.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Czech king Otakar tried in 1278 to recover his lands lost to Rudolph I of Germany. He made allies and collected a large army, but he was defeated by Rudolph and killed at the Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen on the March on
2 CONC  August 26, 1278.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moravia was subdued and its government entrusted to Rudolph's representatives, leaving Kunigunda, now Queen Regent of Bohemia in control of only the province surrounding Prague, while the young Wenceslaus was betrothed and married
2 CONC  to one of Rudolph's daughters, Judith.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda married secondly a Bohemian magnate, Zavish, Lord of Falkenstejn and Rozmberk in Prague June 2, 1284. Zavish survived her, married again, and was executed on August 24, 1290.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda's son Wenceslaus II kept the kingdom of Bohemia, and also succeeded in obtaining Poland and Hungary although not very sustainably. Ultimately she is one of the pivotal ancestresses of the Luxembourg and the Habsburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda Rostislavna (1245 – 9 September 1285) [Czech: Kunhuta] was Queen consort  of Bohemia and its regent from 1278 until her death. She was a member of the House of Chernigov , and a daughter of the ruler of Slavonia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT She was presumably born in Ruthenia, in the domains of her paternal grandfather Michael of Chernigov , Grand Prince of Kiev, to Rostislav Mihailovich , future ruler of Belgrade and Slavonia, and his wife Anna of Hungary . After th
2 CONC e death of her father's father, Kunigunda's family relocated to Hungary, where her mother's father, Bela IV of Hungary , made her father governor of certain Serbian-speaking regions in the Danube Valley. Her father proclaimed hims
2 CONC elf Emperor of Bulgaria < in 1256 but did not stay there to defend his title.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda was married to King Premysl Otakar II of Bohemia  (ca. 1233 – 1278) in Bratislava  on 25 October 1261, following his divorce from Margaret, Duchess of Austria (ca. 1204 – 1266) who was elderly and barren, and unable to p
2 CONC rovide heirs for the King. Kunigunda, 41 years Margaret's junior, bore Otakar several children, the youngest being his only legitimate son Wenceslaus (Václav, 1271-1305).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: Queen of Bohemia
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda was married to King Premysl Otakar II of Bohemia  (ca. 1233 – 1278) in Bratislava on 25 October 1261, following his divorce from Margaret, Duchess of Austria (ca. 1204 – 1266) who was elderly and barren, and unable to pr
2 CONC ovide heirs for the King. Kunigunda, 41 years Margaret's junior, bore Otakar several children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry of Bohemia (1262 – 1263)?. Died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunde of Bohemia  (January, 1265 – 27 November 1321). Married Boleslaus II of Masovia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of Bohemia (5 September 1269 – 17 May 1296). Married Rudolf II, Duke of Austria 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenceslaus II of Bohemia  (17 September 1271 – 21 June 1305). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Genuine heirs of the Babenberg  asserted their rights against King Otakar in Austria. The young Kunigunda's grandfather Bela IV had been Otakar's rival.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Czech king Otakar tried in 1278 to recover his lands lost to Rudolph I of Germany . He made allies and collected a large army, but he was defeated by Rudolph and killed at the Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen  on the March 
2 CONC on 26 August 1278.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moravia  was subdued and its government entrusted to Rudolph's representatives, leaving Kunigunda, now Queen Regent of Bohemia in control of only the province surrounding Prague, while the young Wenceslaus was betrothed and marrie
2 CONC d to one of Rudolph's daughters, Judith.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda married secondly a Bohemian magnate, Zavish, Lord of Falkenstejn and Rozmberk in Prague 2 June 1284. Zavish survived her, married again, and was executed on 24 August 1290.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kunigunda's son Wenceslaus II kept the kingdom of Bohemia, and also succeeded in obtaining Poland and Hungary although not very sustainably. Ultimately she is one of the pivotal ancestresses of the Luxembourg  and the Habsburg.
