1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_of_Moscow Anna Vasilievna of Moscow] (1393 – August 1417) was a Byzantine Empress consort by marriage to John VIII Palaiologos. She died while her husband was still the junior co
2 CONC -emperor of the Byzantine Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of Vasily I of Moscow and Sophia of Lithuania. Her maternal grandparents were Vytautas the Great and his first wife, Anna.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT She married John VIII in 1414. Her husband was the eldest surviving son of Manuel II Palaiologos and Helena Dragaš. John was named Despotes in 1416 and seems to have assumed the position of co-emperor shortly thereafter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was second in status only to her mother-in-law among the women of the Byzantine court. The history of Doukas records her dying of the "plague" in 1417. She is thought to be a victim of bubonic plague. Following the Black Deat
2 CONC h this plague continued to strike parts of Europe sporadically until the 17th century, each time with reduced intensity and fatality, suggesting an increased resistance due to genetic selection.
