1 NOTE {geni:about_me} { The son of Umar Shaikh Mirza, the second son of Amir Taimur, Pir Muhammad Jahangir,  was slain in A.D. 1405, A.H. 808.  ref An Oriental Biographical Dictionary by Thomas William Beale. 
2 CONT ==============
2 CONT https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Pir_Muhammad_(son_of_Umar_Shaikh)
2 CONT
2 CONT Pir Muhammad Mirza (c.1379 – 8 May 1409) was a Timurid prince and a grandson of the Central Asian conqueror Timur by his eldest son Umar Shaikh Mirza I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike many of his relatives, Pir Muhammad did not assert a claim to the throne in the aftermath of Timur's death. Instead, he consolidated and maintained the position that he had previously been given by his grandfather. He was a
2 CONC ssassinated by his own soldiers while campaigning in 1409.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pir Muhammad was born c.1379 and was the eldest son of Umar Shaikh Mirza I by his wife, the Mongol princess Malikat Agha. His father, the eldest of the four sons of Timur, died in 1394 when Pir Muhammad was about fifteen years old
2 CONC . His mother, a daughter of the Khan of Moghulistan, Khizr Khoja, was subsequently remarried to Umar Shaikh's younger brother Shah Rukh.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his father's death, Pir Muhammad was appointed to his former post as governor of Fars, which had been conquered from the Persian Muzaffarid dynasty by Timur a few years earlier.[2] However, Pir Muhammad appears to have e
2 CONC arned his grandfather's ire during this time, being punished twice by Timur for suspected misbehaviours. It is perhaps due to these chastenings that the prince chose to adopt a relatively cautious position in the succession strugg
2 CONC le following Timur's death.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Timur died in 1405, whilst leading his army east in a campaign against the Ming Dynasty of China.[4] As he had no unambiguously appointed heir, a succession dispute erupted among his surviving sons and grandsons.[5] Pir Muhammad a
2 CONC nd his younger brothers, Iskandar and Rustam, all held neighbouring governorships at this time.[6] However, Pir Muhammad, twenty-five years old and long established in his position, was the most powerful among his siblings.[7] Sev
2 CONC eral regional cities, such as Yazd and Abarkuh, quickly declared their support for the prince and some of his advisers urged him to seek the endorsement of the Abbasid Caliph in Egypt. However, Pir Muhammad instead opted to declar
2 CONC e his support for his uncle Shah Rukh.[8] Given that the latter was also their step-father, Pir Muhammad saw that he and his brothers were duty-bound in providing their loyalty.[a] However, this vassalship appears to have been in 
2 CONC name only, as Pir Muhammad effectively acted as an independent monarch within his domains.[9]
