1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphrosyne_Doukaina_Kamaterina
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios III Angelos
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to Euphrosyne Doucaena Camaterina Alexios had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene Angelina, who married (1) Andronicus Contostephanus, and (2) Alexius Palaeologus, by whom she was the grandmother of Emperor Michael VIII Palaeologus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Angelina, who married (1) the sebastokrator Isaac Komnenos, great-nephew of emperor Manuel I Comnenus, and (2) Theodore Lascaris, emperor of Nicaea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudocia Angelina, who married (1) King Stefan I Prvovenčani of Serbia, then (2) Emperor Alexius V, and (3) Leo Sgouros, ruler of Corinth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina or better Kamatera (c. 1155 – 1211) was the wife of the Byzantine Emperor Alexios III Angelos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne was the daughter of Andronikos Doukas Kamateros, a high-ranking official who held the titles of megas droungarios and pansebastos (died 1176), and (accordingly to genealogist David Hughes) his second wife, as her second
2 CONC  husband, married in 1158, Qirwerne(je), sister of the 7th Zagwe Ethiopian Emperor Gebre Mesqel Lalibela (1119-1159).[1] She was related to the Emperor Constantine X and Irene Doukaina, empress of Alexios I Komnenos. Both of her b
2 CONC rothers had rebelled against Andronikos I Komnenos; one was imprisoned and the other was blinded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne married Alexios Angelos, the older brother of the future Emperor Isaac II Angelos in c. 1169. Although Isaac II bestowed many titles and honors upon his brother, Alexios seized the throne on April 8, 1195, desposing Isa
2 CONC ac and proclaiming himself emperor. In this he was assisted by Euphrosyne, who had organized a party of aristocratic supporters. Euphrosyne took control of the palace and quelled the opposition herself, securing the accession of h
2 CONC er husband to the throne by wholesale bribery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne was a dominating woman with a talent for politics, and she virtually ruled the Empire in the name of Alexios III, who was concerned primarily with pleasure and idle pursuits. She issued commands herself and even altered
2 CONC  Alexios' decrees when it suited her. Euphrosyne and Alexios were criticized for their love of finery and the enrichment of their relatives at state expense. Her own brother, Basil Kamateros, and her son-in-law, Andronikos Kontost
2 CONC ephanos, accused Euphrosyne of adultery with one of her ministers, a nobleman named Vatatzes. Alexios III believed the allegations and had Vatatzes executed. Euphrosyne was stripped of her imperial robes and banished to a convent 
2 CONC at Nematarea in October 1196. However, her relatives convinced Alexios to reinstate her, and she was recalled six months later in spring 1197.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1203, faced with the Fourth Crusade and the return of his nephew, Alexios IV Angelos, Alexios III fled Constantinople with a magnificent treasure and some female relatives, including his daughter Eirene. Euphrosyne was left beh
2 CONC ind and was immediately imprisoned by the new regime. Alexios IV was soon strangled by Alexios Doukas Mourtzouphlos, the lover of Euphrosyne's daughter Eudokia, who then proclaimed himself emperor as Alexios V. In April 1204 Euphr
2 CONC osyne fled the city along with her daughter and Alexios V, and they made their way to Mosynoupolis, where Euphrosyne's husband Alexios III had taken refuge. Alexios III had Alexios V blinded and abandoned to the crusaders, who had
2 CONC  him executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne and Alexios III fled across Greece to Thessalonica and Corinth, but were finally captured by Boniface of Montferrat and imprisoned. In 1209 or 1210 they were ransomed by their cousin Michael I of Epirus, and Euphrosyne 
2 CONC spent the remainder of her life in Arta. She died in 1210 or 1211.
2 CONT
2 CONT By her husband, Alexios III Angelos, Euphrosyne had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene Angelina, who married (1) Andronikos Kontostephanos; (2) Alexios Palaiologos, by whom she was the grandmother of Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Angelina, who married (1) the sebastokratōr Isaac Komnenos, great-nephew of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos; (2) Emperor Theodore I Laskaris of Nicaea. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudokia Angelina, who married (1) King Stefan I Prvovenčani of Serbia; (2) Emperor Alexios V Doukas; (3) Leo Sgouros, ruler of Corinth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina or better Kamatera (c. 1155 – 1211) was the wife of the Byzantine Emperor Alexios III Angelos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne was the daughter of Andronikos Doukas Kamateros, a high-ranking official who held the titles of megas droungarios and pansebastos (died 1176), and (accordingly to genealogist David Hughes) his second wife, as her second
2 CONC  husband, married in 1158, Qirwerne(je), sister of the 7th Zagwe Ethiopian Emperor Gebre Mesqel Lalibela (1119-1159).[1] She was related to the Emperor Constantine X and Irene Doukaina, empress of Alexios I Komnenos. Both of her b
2 CONC rothers had rebelled against Andronikos I Komnenos; one was imprisoned and the other was blinded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne married Alexios Angelos, the older brother of the future Emperor Isaac II Angelos in c. 1169. Although Isaac II bestowed many titles and honors upon his brother, Alexios seized the throne on April 8, 1195, desposing Isa
2 CONC ac and proclaiming himself emperor. In this he was assisted by Euphrosyne, who had organized a party of aristocratic supporters. Euphrosyne took control of the palace and quelled the opposition herself, securing the accession of h
2 CONC er husband to the throne by wholesale bribery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne was a dominating woman with a talent for politics, and she virtually ruled the Empire in the name of Alexios III, who was concerned primarily with pleasure and idle pursuits. She issued commands herself and even altered
2 CONC  Alexios' decrees when it suited her. Euphrosyne and Alexios were criticized for their love of finery and the enrichment of their relatives at state expense. Her own brother, Basil Kamateros, and her son-in-law, Andronikos Kontost
2 CONC ephanos, accused Euphrosyne of adultery with one of her ministers, a nobleman named Vatatzes. Alexios III believed the allegations and had Vatatzes executed. Euphrosyne was stripped of her imperial robes and banished to a convent 
2 CONC at Nematarea in October 1196. However, her relatives convinced Alexios to reinstate her, and she was recalled six months later in spring 1197.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1203, faced with the Fourth Crusade and the return of his nephew, Alexios IV Angelos, Alexios III fled Constantinople with a magnificent treasure and some female relatives, including his daughter Eirene. Euphrosyne was left beh
2 CONC ind and was immediately imprisoned by the new regime. Alexios IV was soon strangled by Alexios Doukas Mourtzouphlos, the lover of Euphrosyne's daughter Eudokia, who then proclaimed himself emperor as Alexios V. In April 1204 Euphr
2 CONC osyne fled the city along with her daughter and Alexios V, and they made their way to Mosynoupolis, where Euphrosyne's husband Alexios III had taken refuge. Alexios III had Alexios V blinded and abandoned to the crusaders, who had
2 CONC  him executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphrosyne and Alexios III fled across Greece to Thessalonica and Corinth, but were finally captured by Boniface of Montferrat and imprisoned. In 1209 or 1210 they were ransomed by their cousin Michael I of Epirus, and Euphrosyne 
2 CONC spent the remainder of her life in Arta. She died in 1210 or 1211.
2 CONT
2 CONT By her husband, Alexios III Angelos, Euphrosyne had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene Angelina, who married (1) Andronikos Kontostephanos; (2) Alexios Palaiologos, by whom she was the grandmother of Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Angelina, who married (1) the sebastokratōr Isaac Komnenos, great-nephew of Emperor Manuel I Komnenos; (2) Emperor Theodore I Laskaris of Nicaea. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudokia Angelina, who married (1) King Stefan I Prvovenčani of Serbia; (2) Emperor Alexios V Doukas; (3) Leo Sgouros, ruler of Corinth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphrosyne_Doukaina_Kamaterina
