1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Shaikh_Hussain_Jalayir
2 CONT ====
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Hasan Jalayir (died October 9, 1374) was briefly Jalayirid ruler (1374). He was the eldest son of Shaikh Awais Jalayir. After his father died, Hasan succeeded him, but was never popular among the people. Shortly after he be
2 CONC gan his reign, he was executed by his amirs, who then put his brother Husain on the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his (Sheikh Hossain's) Beiram, the Khajeh, a Turkman whose followers were called Qara Qoyunlu, had gained some power in establishing themselves in the southern region of Van lake, in the vicinity of Sanjar, Mush and Musel, 
2 CONC  continually threatening Iraq and Azarbaijan. To nullify this threat Sheikh Hossain decided to use force but a peace treaty was signed instead. Sultan Hossein was murdered by his brother, Sultan Ahmad, in 1405 AD...]  ref : page 1
2 CONC 95 of  
2 CONT The Book of Iran. Iranian History at a Glance (Paperback)
2 CONT by Reza Shabani (Author), Mahmood Farrokhpey (Translator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Hussain Jalayir (died April or May 1382) was a Jalayirid ruler (1374–1382). He was the son of Shaikh Awais Jalayir. Following the execution of his brother Shaikh Hasan Jalayir, the amirs placed Shaikh Hussain Jalayir on the
2 CONC  throne. Almost immediately he had to deal with an invasion by his brother-in-law Shah Mahmud of the Muzaffarids of Iran. Shah Mahmud, who was the son-in-law of Shaikh Awais Jalayir, advanced a claim on Tabriz and took the city. I
2 CONC llness, however, forced him to abandon the region. This was followed by an invasion by the leader of the Muzaffarids, Shah Shuja, but despite taking the city, he was also forced to retreat due to a rebellion in Qazvin. It was only
2 CONC  in the summer of 1376 that Shaikh Hussain Jalayir took up residence in Tabriz. In the following spring, he undertook a successful campaign against the Black Sheep Turkmen under Bairam Khwaja, who had been raiding from the west.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaikh Hussain Jalayir's reign was marred by conflict with his surviving brothers; namely, Shaikh Ali Jalayir, Sultan Ahmed Jalayir, and Shaikh Bayazid Jalayir. Shaikh Hussain Jalayir also lost his popularity among many amirs who 
2 CONC disliked his support of the powerful 'Adil Aqa, the governor of Ray. Shaikh Hussain Jalayir was faced with a series of uprisings, hostility among his brothers, and troubles with his external enemies. When 'Adil Aqa turned on him, 
2 CONC Shaikh Hussain Jalayir lost his last powerful supporter. Sultan Ahmed Jalayir advanced from Arbadil and occupied Tabriz near the end of April 1382; he ordered Husain's execution. Ahmad would then himself take the throne, but confl
2 CONC ict between him and 'Adil Aqa and Bayazid meant that the state fell into a period of disunity.
