1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rostislav Mstislavich (Russian and Ukrainian: Ростислав Мстиславич) (c. 1110–1167), Kniaz' (Prince) of Smolensk (1125–1160), Novgorod (1154) and Velikiy Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kiev (1154, 1159–1167). He was the so
2 CONC n of Mstislav I of Kiev and Christina Ingesdotter of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Yaroslav II of Kiev was driven out of Novgorod, Rostislav was invited to become the ruler of Novgorod. He accepted, and became the prince on April 17, 1154. Then, learning that Iziaslav II had died, Rostislav left Novgorod t
2 CONC o take the Kievan throne. Indignant that their prince had abandoned them and angered that "he did not make order among them, but tore them more apart", the citizens of Novgorod drove out Rostislav's son, David, who was their gover
2 CONC nor. They replaced him with Mstislav Yurievich, the son of Yury Dolgoruky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rostislav ruled Kiev for one week before Iziaslav III of Kiev forced him to flee to Chernigov.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left five children: 3 sons, princes David Rostislavich of Novgorod, Mstislav Rostislavich of Smolensk, and Roman I of Kiev, and 2 daughters Elena Rostislavna of Kiev-Smolensk (died 1204) and Agrafiya Rostislavna (died 1237).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia - https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rostislav_I_of_Kiev
