2 CONT >3. '''DMITRY Aleksandrovich ([1250]-Volok 1294[864]).  He was appointed Prince of Pereyaslavl in 1253 by his father. He was installed as Prince of Novgorod by his father in 1259, but was removed by his uncle Iaroslav Grand Prince
2 CONC  of Vladimir in 1264 after his father died[865].  The Novgorod Chronicle records that he was restored in Novgorod on the death of Prince Iaroslav, only to be removed again in 1273 in favour of his uncle Vasily, but succeeded again
2 CONC  in 1277 on the death of the latter[866].  He succeeded his uncle in 1277 as DMITRY I Grand Prince of Vladimir.  He was deposed by the Mongols in 1281, apparently for refusing to visit Sarai to swear allegiance.  However, he gaine
2 CONC d support of the Mongol commander Nogai who had acquired a powerful position as an independent leader among the Golden Horde and returned as Grand Prince in 1283[867].  Dmitry was deposed in 1293 by the new khan Tokhta, who gave h
2 CONC is support to the restoration of his brother Andrei.  Prince of Kostroma 1276-1293.  m ---.  The name of Dmitry´s wife is not known.  Dmitry & his wife had five children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>a)         IVAN Dmitrievich "Tikhiy" ([1276]-15 May 1302).  Prince of Kostroma 1293-1294.  Prince of Pereyaslavl Zalesskii 1294-1302.   m ([1292/96]) --- Dmitrievna of Rostov, daughter of DMITRY Borisovich Prince of Rostov & his
2 CONC  wife ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>b)         ALEKSANDR Dmitrievich (-1292).  He "died among the Tartars"[870]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>c)         IVAN Dmitrievich (-1290).  
2 CONT
2 CONT >>d)         MARIA Dmitrievna (-1300).    m DAUMANTAS [Dowmont] TIMOFEI [of Lithuania] Prince of Pskov, son of --- (-1299).
2 CONT >>e)         --- Dmitrievna . 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmitry_of_Pereslavl
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry Alexandrovich (ca. 1250-1294) was Grand Duke of Vladimir-Suzdal from 1276 until 1281 and then from 1283 until 1293. Dmitry was the second son of Alexander Nevsky. When his elder brother Vasily died young, Dmitry remained th
2 CONC e chief heir to his illustrious father. As early as 1259, he was left by Alexander in charge of Novgorod. Upon Alexander's death in 1264, however, the Novgorodians expelled Dmitry to his native Pereslavl-Zalessky, citing his youth
2 CONC  as a pretext. Four years later, when Dmitry had turned 18, he was welcomed back to Novgorod and — together with his future son-in-law, Daumantas of Pskov — led a local militia against Livonian Knights in the Battle of Rakvere. Du
2 CONC ring the following decade, he struggled for control of Novgorod against his uncles, Yaroslav III and Vasily of Kostroma. In 1276, when his elders died, he finally ascended the coveted thrones of Vladimir and Novgorod. Two years la
2 CONC ter, he founded a great fortress of Koporye, which he intended to rule himself. The Novgorodians revolted, forcing Dmitry to leave Koporye and Novgorod altogether. While Dmitry was preoccupied with pacifying Novgorod, Andrey of Go
2 CONC rodets (Dmitry's younger brother) went to the Golden Horde and received from the khan permission to replace Dmitry as the grand duke. In 1281, Andrey returned to Russia, joined his forces with princes of Rostov and Yaroslavl and, 
2 CONC after much devastation to Dmitry's lands, seized his capital Pereslavl. Dmitry fled to Koporye but, failing to win support of Novgorodians, had to retreat further northward, probably to Scandinavia. Two years later, Dmitry returne
2 CONC d to Russia, only to find his lands ravaged by the Mongols and his brother Andrey. Thereupon he went to the Black Sea and met Nogai Khan, who was the greatest enemy of legaL khan Telebuga in the Golden Horde at that time. Wishing 
2 CONC to increase his authority in Russia, Nogai vowed to support Dmitry in his struggle for the grand ducal throne. On hearing about this, Andrey renounced his claims to Vladimir and Novgorod and returned to Gorodets. In 1285 Andrey ag
2 CONC ain brought Mongol hordes to Russia, but these were expelled by Dmitry and his allies. Finally, in 1293 Andrey managed to unite the Mongols and Russian princes in opposition to Dmitry. Reluctant to renew fratricidal hostilities, D
2 CONC mitry took monastic vows in 1293 and died the next year. He was buried in the Saviour Cathedral of Pereslavl-Zalessky.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ==[https://www.geni.com/people/Grand-Prince-Dmitry-Alexandrovich/6000000000628853381 Grand Prince Dmitry Alexandrovich] F1b1 (mtDNA); N1a (Y-DNA)
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000221019251919 ''Third cervical vertebra from the sarcophagus of Prince Dmitry Alexandrovich Transfiguration Cathedral in Pereslavl.'']
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221020001834&size=medium}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Researchers analyzed bone samples (metacarpal, patella, navicular) from a burial in the Transfiguration Cathedral, Pereslavl-Zalessky, believed to be that of Prince Dmitry Alexandrovich (died 1294).The genetic sex was confirmed as
2 CONC  male, mitochondrial haplogroup F1b (or F1b1), and Y-chromosome haplogroup N1a.Phenotypic predictions: likely brown eyes (P = 0.962), dark or brown hair, and an intermediate skin tone.Comparisons with modern Rurikid descendants re
2 CONC vealed clustering within the N1a haplogroup branch, suggesting continuity in paternal lineage.Genomic components indicate a complex origin, involving East-Eurasian, Scandinavian ("Viking-age"), and steppe nomadic ancestry. The N1a
2 CONC  Y-haplogroup matches modern individuals claiming descent via the Rurikid line, lending credence to that lineage's continuity
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10615192/
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000221019251919
2 CONT * https://www.heritagedaily.com/2021/04/archaeologists-identify-royal-tombs-of-pereslavl-princes/138861
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT = “The Rurikids: The First Experience of Reconstructing the Genetic Portrait of the Ruling Family of Medieval Rus’ Based on Paleogenomic Data”
2 CONT Article Overview
2 CONT
2 CONT Published in: Acta Naturae (2023) 
2 CONT RCSI Journals Platform
2 CONT PMC
2 CONT
2 CONT Researchers analyzed bone samples (metacarpal, patella, navicular) from a burial in the Transfiguration Cathedral, Pereslavl-Zalessky, believed to be that of Prince Dmitry Alexandrovich (died 1294) 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Using the best-preserved sample (Nev2.3), they generated over 15 million sequencing reads and identified 532,154 SNPs 
2 CONT
2 CONT The genetic sex was confirmed as male, mitochondrial haplogroup F1b (or F1b1), and Y-chromosome haplogroup N1a 
2 CONT
2 CONT Phenotypic predictions: likely brown eyes (P = 0.962), dark or brown hair, and an intermediate skin tone 
2 CONT
2 CONT Comparisons with modern Rurikid descendants revealed clustering within the N1a haplogroup branch, suggesting continuity in paternal lineage 
2 CONT
2 CONT Genomic components indicate a complex origin, involving East-Eurasian, Scandinavian ("Viking-age"), and steppe nomadic ancestry 
2 CONT
2 CONT First-of-its-kind: This study marks the inaugural genome-wide genetic reconstruction of a medieval Rurikid, significantly advancing our understanding of early Rus’ nobility 
2 CONT
2 CONT Unbroken paternal line? The N1a Y-haplogroup matches modern individuals claiming descent via the Rurikid line, lending credence to that lineage's continuity 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference
2 CONT
2 CONT Trapezov, R.O., Trapezova, L.I., Zhukova, O.V., Kostryukova, E.S., Tyazhelova, T.V., Andreeva, T.V., Gusev, F.E., Utkina, I.S., Koshlyakova, E.E., Konovalova, O.V., Yurasov, R.A., Kazakov, S.V., Trefilov, D.I., Vasiliev, A.L., Khr
2 CONC apov, E.A., Agarkov, A.A., Shilov, A.G., Kulikov, D.V., Semyanov, V.V., Markov, A.V., Medvedeva, I.V., Pan'kov, S.V., Fedorova, L.P., Fedorov, A.N., & Gelfand, M.S. (2023).
2 CONT The Rurikids: The First Experience of Reconstructing the Genetic Portrait of the Ruling Family of Medieval Rus’ Based on Paleogenomic Data.
2 CONT Acta Naturae, 15(3), 96–111.
2 CONT https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10615192 
2 CONT
