1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Descendant of kings [https://www.geni.com/people/William-the-Conqueror-king-of-England/5597380726000028489 William the Conqueror], [https://www.geni.com/people/Stephen-I-King-of-England/6000000000424802839?through=
2 CONC 6000000003051107855 Stephen I of England] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Louis-VII-the-Young-king-of-France/6000000005791896564 Louis VII of France]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_V_Palaeologus John V Palaiologos] (or Palaeologus) (Greek: Ίωάννης Ε' Παλαιολόγος, Iōannēs V Palaiologos) (18 June 1332 – February 16, 1391) was the son of Emperor Andronikos III Palaiologos and 
2 CONC Anna of Savoy. His maternal grandparents were Count Amadeus V of Savoy and his second wife Maria of Brabant. He succeeded his father as Byzantine Emperor in 1341, at age nine.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_V_Palaeologus
2 CONT
2 CONT John VI Kantakouzenos, his father's friend, served as his regent and co-emperor (1347–1354), after having fought a civil war (1341–1347) against the regency for young Juan V headed by his mother Anna of Savoy, the Patriarch Juan X
2 CONC IV Kalekas and the megas doux Alexios Apokaukos. Forced to fight Juan Kantakouzenos, who had usurped the throne during his minority, Juan V became sole emperor in 1354. In 1343, Anna of Savoy pawned the Byzantine crown jewels for 
2 CONC 30,000 Venetian ducats. His long reign was marked by the gradual dissolution of the imperial power. In his reign the Ottomans, led by Suleyman Paşa the son of the Ottoman sultan, took Adrianople and Philippopolis, and exacted trib
2 CONC ute from the emperor. After the Ottoman Turks gained control of Gallipoli and threatened Constantinople, Juan V appealed to the West for help, proposing to end the schism between the Byzantine and Latin churches by submitting to t
2 CONC he supremacy of the Roman Church. Impoverished by war, he was detained as a debtor when he visited Venice (1369). In 1371 he recognized the suzerainty of the Ottoman sultan Murad I, who later helped him to regain the throne (1379)
2 CONC  after he was deposed by his son Andronikos IV Palaiologos in 1376. In 1390 his grandson, Juan VII Palaiologos, briefly usurped the throne, but was quickly overthrown. Juan V was succeeded by his son Manuel II Palaiologos. His you
2 CONC nger son Theodore I Palaiologos succeeded to the so-called Despotate of Morea in 1383.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of his reign, in 1390, Juan ordered the strengthening of the Constantinople Golden Gate, utilizing marble from the decayed churches in and around the city. Upon the completion of this construction, Bayezid I, threa
2 CONC tening war and the blinding of his son Manuel (whom he held in captivity), demanded that Juan raze these new works. Juan V filled the Sultan's order, but is said to have suffered from this humiliation and, according to historians,
2 CONC  died on February 16, 1391.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Helena Kantakouzene, daughter of Juan VI Kantakouzenos and Irene Asanina. They were parents of several children including:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Andronikos IV Palaiologos, Byzantine emperor 1376–1379
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Manuel II Palaiologos, Byzantine emperor 1391–1425
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Michael Palaiologos, despotēs
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Theodore I Palaiologos, despotēs in Morea
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Eirene Palaiologina, who married her maternal first cousin, Halil, the son of the Ottoman Sultan Orhan and Maria Kantakouzene, who, like John's wife Helena, was a daughter of Juan VI Kantakouzenos and his wife, Irene Asanina.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan V Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan V Palaiologos or Palaeologus (Greek: Ίωάννης Ε' Παλαιολόγος, Iōannēs V Palaiologos), (18 June 1332 – February 16, 1391) was the son of Emperor Andronikos III Palaiologos and Anna of Savoy. His maternal grandparents were Count
2 CONC  Amadeus V of Savoy and his second wife Maria of Brabant. He succeeded his father as Byzantine Emperor in 1341, at age nine.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan VI Kantakouzenos, his father's friend, served as his regent and co-emperor (1347–1354), after having fought a civil war (1342–1347) against the regency for young John V headed by his mother Anna of Savoy, the Patriarch Juan X
2 CONC IV Kalekas and the megas doux Alexios Apokaukos. Forced to fight Juan Kantakouzenos, who had usurped the throne during his minority, Juan V became sole emperor in 1354. In 1343, Anna of Savoy pawned the Byzantine crown jewels for 
2 CONC 30,000 Venetian ducats. His long reign was marked by the gradual dissolution of the imperial power. In his reign the Ottomans, led by Suleyman Paşa the son of the Ottoman sultan, took Adrianople and Philippopolis, and exacted trib
2 CONC ute from the emperor. After the Ottoman Turks gained control of Gallipoli and threatened Constantinople, Juan V appealed to the West for help, proposing to end the schism between the Byzantine and Latin churches by submitting to t
2 CONC he supremacy of the Roman Church. Impoverished by war, he was detained as a debtor when he visited Venice (1369). In 1371 he recognized the suzerainty of the Ottoman sultan Murad I, who later helped him to regain the throne (1379)
2 CONC  after he was deposed by his son Andronikos IV Palaiologos in 1376. In 1390 his grandson, Juan VII Palaiologos, briefly usurped the throne, but was quickly overthrown. Juan V was succeeded by his son Manuel II Palaiologos. His you
2 CONC nger son Theodore I Palaiologos succeeded to the so-called Despotate of Morea in 1383.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of his reign, in 1390, Juan ordered the strengthening of the Constantinople Golden Gate, utilizing marble from the decayed churches in and around the city. Upon the completion of this construction, Bayezid I, threa
2 CONC tening war and the blinding of his son Manuel (whom he held in captivity), demanded that John raze these new works. Juan V obeyed the Sultan's order, but is said to have suffered from this humiliation and, according to historians,
2 CONC  died of a nervous shock on February 16, 1391.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Helena Kantakouzene, daughter of Juan VI Kantakouzenos and Irene Asanina. They were parents of several children including:
2 CONT
2 CONT Andronikos IV Palaiologos, Byzantine emperor 1376-1379
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel II Palaiologos, Byzantine emperor 1391-1425
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Palaiologos, despotēs
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore I Palaiologos, despotēs in Morea
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene Palaiologina, who married Halil, the son of Sultan Orhan of the Ottoman Empire
