2 CONT  Hirsch, Theodor; Töppen, Max; Strehlke, Ernst, eds. (1863). Scriptores rerum Prussicarum: die Geschichtsquellen der Preussischen Vorzeit bis zum Untergange der Ordensherrschaft. 2. Leipzig: Verlag von S. Hirzel. pp. 489–490. OCLC
2 CONC  16348289.
2 CONT  Nikžentaitis, Alvydas (1990). Žemaičių praeitis. 1990 m. Varnių konferencijos medžiaga (in Lithuanian). I. Vilnius: Mokslas. pp. 93–99. OCLC 35805682.
2 CONT  Nikžentaitis, Alvydas (1989). Gediminas (in Lithuanian). Vilnius: Vyriausioji enciklopedijų redakcija. pp. 10–11. OCLC 27471995.
2 CONT  Baronas, Darius (2008). "Pilėnai ir Margiris: faktai ir fikcijos". Istorijos šaltinių tyrimai (in Lithuanian). I: 39, 47, 53, 57. ISSN 2029-0705.
2 CONT  Rowell, S. C. (1994). Lithuania Ascending: A Pagan Empire Within East-Central Europe, 1295–1345. Cambridge Studies in Medieval Life and Thought: Fourth Series. Cambridge University Press. p. 240. ISBN 978-0-521-45011-9.
2 CONT  Baranauskas, Tomas (2011-02-25). "Pilėnai: žygdarbis ir mįslė" (in Lithuanian). Alkas.lt. Retrieved 2016-09-02. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margier (died 1336) was a Duke of Samogitia, a medieval Lithuanian/Samogitian prince, mentioned in the chronicle of Wigand of Marburg as the heroic defender of Pilėnai in 1336. Not able to defend the castle against the siege of th
2 CONC e Teutonic Knights, Margiris and other defenders decided to commit suicide, burning the castle and leaving no loot for the enemy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margiris held a high position in the Lithuanian society as evidenced by his duel with John of Luxemburg, King of Bohemia, who joined the Teutonic crusade against Lithuania and captured Medvėgalis in early 1329. After the rules of 
2 CONC the duel were broken, Margiris had to pay a ransom. He did so with coins minted by Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor stolen during the 1326 raid into Brandenburg. If Margiris was not from the ruling class, the King of Bohemia would con
2 CONC sider it degrading to fight with him, and Margiris would not have a share of the spoils of war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding his family, chronicles mention only Margiris' son, who went to France after 1329 and whose wife was countess of Clermont of unknown name. Since the sources attest the high status and wealth of Margiris, historian Alvydas
2 CONC  Nikžentaitis concluded that Margiris must have been a son of Butvydas and brother of Gediminas, Grand Duke of Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Polish: Margier is also the title of a historic epic poem about the heroic prince, written by Władysław Syrokomla in 1855.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margiris 
