1 NOTE {geni:about_me} **Barïm~shï'ïratu-Qabitchï~ba'atur~buqa Borjigit** is a historical figure from the Mongol era, notable for being a part of the **Borjigin** clan. The Borjigin clan is famously associated with the **Mongol Empire**,
2 CONC  particularly with **Genghis Khan** and his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Background on Barïm~shï'ïratu-Qabitchï~ba'atur~buqa Borjigit
2 CONT
2 CONT - **Barïm~shï'ïratu-Qabitchï~ba'atur~buqa Borjigit** is recognized as a prominent member of the **Borjigin** clan, a noble lineage within the Mongol Empire.
2 CONT - The Borjigin clan, from which Genghis Khan (originally named Temujin) emerged, is one of the most significant and revered clans in Mongol history.
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Sub-Tribes and Modern Groups Claiming Lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT Several modern groups and sub-tribes in Mongolia and surrounding regions claim descent from the **Borjigin** clan, including those linked to **Barïm~shï'ïratu-Qabitchï~ba'atur~buqa Borjigit**:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. **Mongol Clans**:
2 CONT    - **Borjigin**: The direct lineage to the Borjigin clan includes several modern Mongol groups and tribes. The Borjigin is a prominent and historically significant clan that has been central to Mongol identity.
2 CONT    - **Jebe**: This tribe is known for its historical connections to the Borjigin clan. The Jebe people have claimed descent from Genghis Khan’s lineage and his Borjigin ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. **Khalkha Mongols**:
2 CONT    - The **Khalkha Mongols** are the largest Mongol group in modern Mongolia and include several sub-tribes and clans that trace their heritage back to the Borjigin clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. **Oirat Mongols**:
2 CONT    - The **Oirat Mongols**, who historically were part of the Western Mongol tribes, also have groups that claim descent from Borjigin ancestry. The Oirat have significant historical ties to the Mongol Empire and its leaders.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. **Buryat Mongols**:
2 CONT    - The **Buryat Mongols**, an ethnic group living in Siberia and Russia, have historical and cultural ties to the Borjigin clan. They are part of the broader Mongolic-speaking population and have connections to the historical Mo
2 CONC ngol lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. **Khalka and Khorchin Clans**:
2 CONT    - Within Mongolia, specific **Khalka** and **Khorchin** clans claim descent from the Borjigin lineage. These groups are part of the larger Mongol ethnic and cultural community and have historical connections to the ancient Mong
2 CONC ol aristocracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ### Conclusion
2 CONT
2 CONT The Borjigin clan, including figures like **Barïm~shï'ïratu-Qabitchï~ba'atur~buqa Borjigit**, has had a profound impact on Mongol history. Modern Mongol ethnic groups, including the Khalkha, Oirat, Buryat, and others, claim variou
2 CONC s degrees of descent from this influential lineage. These claims often reflect a broader cultural and historical connection to the Mongol Empire and its noble clans.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...........................................................................................................................................................................
2 CONT
2 CONT in  :  "  Histoire Secrète des Mongols "   " Secrète " au sens de " Réservée " aux descendants de Gengis~Qahan ~~ Restitution du texte mongol et traduction française des chapitres I à VI .~~oeuvres posthumes de Paul Pelliot,  Pari
2 CONC s,   1949.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~Chapitre 1
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT §44 - Quand Bodontchar~mungqaq ne fut plus, Qäbïtshï~ba'atur dit : "il y a constamment à la maison l'homme Adangqa~Uriangqadan; Ce Djäwürädäi pourrait bien être de lui". ~~  Et il fit sortir Djäwürädäi de l'endroit de la perche de
2 CONC  sacrifice.  Celui~ci prit le nom de clan de Djäwüräyit (Je'üren), et c'est lui qui fut l'ancêtre des Djäürät (les~Je'üren).
2 CONT
2 CONT (C'est le doute quant à l'origine du Je'üren (Djäwürädaï) qui conduit ici à son exclusion du sacrifice clanique, qui réunit les descendants consanguins d'un seul et même ancêtre, appartenant à un même "os" ou lignée. La connaissan
2 CONC ce de sa propre généalogie conditionne l'observation de la règle exogamique. Il s'agissait d'une affaire d'importance pour les Mongols, qui devaient être capables de citer leurs ancêtres sur neuf générations.)
2 CONT
2 CONT §45 -  Le fils de Barïm~shï'ïratu-Qabïtchï-ba'atur ( Qabïtchï~Courtejambe ) fut  Mänän~tudun ( Vaste~tudun ) "Vaste" au sens de fondateur d'une grande famille..  Les fils de Mänän~tudun  furent  Qatchï~külük  ( Durin~le~Brave ),  
2 CONC Qatchïn ( Duron ),  Qatchï'u ( Dureux ),  Qatchula  ( Durard ),  Qaraldaï  ( Noireaud ),  Qatchï'un  ( Duret ), et  Natchïn~ba'atur  ( Crécerelle~le~Preux ),  sept en tout.
2 CONT
2 CONT §46 -  Le fils de Qatchï~külük fut  Qaïdu~qahan,  né de Nomolun~äkä.  (placée ici selon "l'Histoire Secrète des Mongols"...) Il faut en réalité la placer comme épouse de Mänän~tudun (NdL)
