1 TITL VAKIL
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Following Hasan Ali Bey’s diplomatic success in restoring the family's hold over Maragheh in 1730, his son Aliqli Khan Moghadam (historically spelled Aliqoli Khan) inherited the leadership of the tribe.
2 CONT His career marks a crucial turning point where the Moghadam family shifted from being local regional governors under an empire to behaving as fully autonomous monarchs during the chaotic collapse of mid-18th-century Iran.
2 CONT His career unfolded across several distinct phases:
2 CONT 1. The Indian Expedition with Nader Shah (1738–1739)
2 CONT Aliqoli Khan did not just stay behind to govern Maragheh; he was a highly trusted military officer in the inner circle of Nader Shah Afshar.
2 CONT •	The Vanguard Cavalry: When Nader Shah launched his landmark military campaign to invade the Mughal Empire, Aliqoli Khan personally commanded a massive contingent of elite Moghadam cavalrymen.
2 CONT •	The Siege of Delhi: His horsemen distinguished themselves on the battlefields of Northern India, playing a vital role in the Battle of Karnal and the subsequent occupation of Delhi. As a reward for his battlefield performance, N
2 CONC ader Shah showered Aliqoli Khan with looted Mughal wealth and formalised his position as a chief military amir (general) of Iran.
2 CONT 2. The Great Autonomy Shock (1747)
2 CONT In 1747, Nader Shah was assassinated, throwing Iran into a massive, multi-sided civil war. Aliqoli Khan immediately used his seasoned cavalry and newly acquired wealth to declare the Maragheh Khanate a completely independent, sove
2 CONC reign state.
2 CONT •	Expelling Rivals: He systematically drove out any remaining Afshar tribe officials from the region, completing the task his father had started.
2 CONT •	Establishing a Principality: Under his administration, Maragheh was no longer treated as just a province; it functioned as an independent principality with its own standing military, fiscal taxation system, and independent forei
2 CONC gn policy.
2 CONT 3. Navigating the Post-Afsharid Power Struggle
2 CONT Because Maragheh sat on a highly strategic geopolitical axis in Azerbaijan, Aliqoli Khan spent the 1750s playing rival warlords against one another to preserve his family’s independence:
2 CONT •	The Azad Khan Azad-Factions: He initially resisted the expansionist pushes of the Afghan warlord Azad Khan Afghan.
2 CONT •	The Alliance with the Qajars: Recognizing the rising power of Mohammad Hasan Khan Qajar (the father of Agha Mohammad Khan), Aliqoli Khan shrewdly entered into a military coalition with the early Qajars. This critical geopolitica
2 CONC l decision ensured that while other Azerbaijani khans were completely wiped out, the Moghadam family was explicitly preserved.
2 CONT 4. Passing the Torch to Ahmed Khan
2 CONT Aliqoli Khan’s career successfully bridged the gap between the ruthless centralization of Nader Shah and the rise of the Qajar Dynasty. By the time he passed the leadership to his son, Haj Ali Mohammad Khan (who in turn passed it 
2 CONC to the legendary general Ahmed Khan Moghadam), he had elevated the family from mere tribal chieftains into hereditary royalty.
2 CONT
2 CONT -
2 CONT Aliqoli Khan Moghadam was the supreme head of the Moghadam tribe and the Maragheh Khanate, he did carry the prestigious title of Vakil (representative/advocate), and he was absolutely taken as a political hostage by Karim Khan Zan
2 CONC d
2 CONT In 1725, Nader Shah (then still known as Nader Qoli Beg) was a regional warlord in Khorasan and had not yet marched into Azerbaijan. Northwestern Iran was entirely under Ottoman rule.
2 CONT As established previously, it was Aliqoli's father, Hasan Ali Bey, who famously met Nader Shah outside the gates of Maragheh when the city was actually liberated in 1730. Aliqoli Khan took over the tribal leadership years later, n
2 CONC otably commanding the Moghadam cavalry during Nader Shah's Indian campaign in 1738–1739. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Why Was He Called "Vakil"?
2 CONT In 18th-century Iran, the title Vakil (meaning deputy, regent, or legal representative) was a highly prestigious political honorific. 
2 CONT •	The Tribal Meaning: Aliqoli Khan was designated as the Vakil-e Shah (the Shah's representative) for the Moghadam tribe. This meant that the central government officially recognized him as the sole legal and political mediator be
2 CONC tween the crown and the 10,000 Moghadam families.
2 CONT •	The Zand Contrast: This title became highly emotionally charged during the Zand era because Karim Khan Zand famously refused the title of "Shah," choosing instead to rule the empire under the humble title Vakil al-Ra'aya (Deputy
2 CONC  of the People). 
2 CONT . The Hostage of Karim Khan Zand (The 1760s)
2 CONT Your reading is entirely correct about the hostage situation. When Karim Khan Zand consolidated his rule over western Iran in the 1760s, he faced constant threats of rebellion from the powerful, semi-autonomous Azerbaijani Khans.
2 CONT To guarantee the absolute loyalty of the Moghadam tribe and ensure they would continue to pay their taxes and supply cavalry to the state, Karim Khan implemented a standard imperial tactic:
2 CONT •	The Summons to Shiraz: Karim Khan forced Aliqoli Khan Moghadam to leave Maragheh and move to the Zand imperial capital of Shiraz. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT •	Life in the Citadel: While technically a "hostage" to prevent a Moghadam rebellion, Aliqoli Khan was treated with immense honour as a noble political guest. He lived in luxury within the orbit of the court. 
2 CONT
2 CONT •	The Governance Gap: While Aliqoli Khan was detained in Shiraz as a guarantor of peace, the day-to-day administrative governance back home in Maragheh was handed over to his capable son, Haj Ali Mohammad Khan, who ruled the local
2 CONC  home front on his father's behalf during the 1760s and 1770s.
