2 CONT '''m''' secondly (bigamously, 8 Jul 1339) EIRENE, daughter of --- (-after 19 Jun 1382).  The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos records the marriage 8 Jul 1339 of "the emperor lord Basil…to the empress lady Irene from Trebizond"[96]. 
2 CONC  Co-regent of Trebizond 22 Dec 1349-[1354].  The bigamous nature of this marriage is shown by a later passage in the Chronicle of Michael Panaretos which records that "his sons, the lord Alexios and the lord Kalojannes, were sent…
2 CONC along with their mother" to Constantinople after the death of "the emperor lord Basil the Grand Komnenos" in 1340, while also recording that "the widowed lady Irene Palaiologina…seized the throne"[97].  The Chronicle of Michael Pa
2 CONC naretos records that "the emperor lord Alexios, his mother the lady Irene" were "at the Soumela monastery in Matzouka", in a passage after the record of a solar exclipse 5 May 1361[98].  The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos records 
2 CONC that "the emperor…the empress and the empress mother" descended into "Lazica with a force by land and sea" in Jun 1366[99].  The Chronicle of Michael Panaretos records that "his great grandmother the empress Lady Irene" was presen
2 CONC t at the baptism of her great grandson Manuel, who was born 19 Jun 1382[100]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Basileios had four illegitimate children by his second bigamous marriage:  
