1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joseph ben Ṭodros ha-Levi Abulafia, the brother of Me’ir ben Todros ha-Levi Abulafia, was born in Burgos. Active in the first half of the twelfth century, he spent most of his life in Burgos but apparently settled 
2 CONC in Toledo at some time where he remained until his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph ben Ṭodros was an anti-rationalist thinker, and as such he took an active part, together with other Jews from the Spanish kingdoms and France, such as Solomon ben Abraham of Montpellier, in the dispute pertaining to the wor
2 CONC ks of Maimonides, which were condemned as rationalist because in them he used reason and philosophy to understand faith and tradition. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter to the sages of Provence, Joseph ben Ṭodros criticized the position of David Qimhi, an all-in defender of the rationalist ideas accepted in Castile, and blamed him for the religious laxity in Castilian philosophical ci
2 CONC rcles, thereby bringing to the fore the danger threatening traditional Judaism. A certain duality of attitude can be observed in Joseph ben Ṭodros, however, for while he did not side with the Maimondeans in giving unconditional ap
2 CONC proval to the Guide of the Perplexed (in Arabic), he did not join in the attack that the anti-Maimonideans in Provence launched on Sefer ha-Madaʿ (Book of Knowledge), Maimonides’s introduction to the Mishne Torah, his halakhic com
2 CONC pendium and explication of the Mishna, in which he demonstrates a preference for Aristotle and Greek philosophy, allowing for revelation through reason. With the Dominican order’s consent, these works would be burned in 1232.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the second half of the thirteenth century, a grandson of Joseph ben Ṭodros ha-Levi Abulafia, with the exact same name as his grandfather, was a kabbalist and an influential figure at the court of King Alfonso X. According to th
2 CONC e Seville (1253) and Jerez (1266) Land Allotments, he came into some real estate and land together with his father, Ṭodros ben Joseph ha-Levi Abulafia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yolanda Moreno Koch
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Baer, Yitzhak. A History of the Jews in Christian Spain, 2 vols. (Philadelphia: Jewish Publication Society, 1971). Historia de los judíos en la España cristiana, 2 vols. (Madrid: Altalena, 1981).
2 CONT
2 CONT Orfali, Moisés. Biblioteca de autores lógicos hispanojudíos (Siglos XI–XV) (Granada: Univ. de Granada, 1997), pp. 77 ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sáenz-Badillos, Angel, and Judit Targarona Borrás. Diccionario de Autores Judíos (Sefarad. Siglos X–XV) (Cordova: Ed. El Almendro, 1988), p. 181.
2 CONT
2 CONT Septimus, Bernard. Hispano-Jewish Culture in Transition: The Career and Controversies of Ramah (Cambridge, Mass.: Harvard University Press, 1982).
2 CONT
