1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Rogvolod (Vasilko) Borisovich (Rogvolodovich)''' (d. After 1171), Prince of Polotsk 1144-1151, 1159-1162, Prince Drutsky 1127-1129, 1140-1144, 1158-1159, 1162 — after 1171, son of the Polotsk prince Rogvolod (Bo
2 CONC ris ) Vseslavich. Descended from the Drutsk line of the Polotsk princes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Name 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the naming system of Rurikovich, this prince mentions two names and patronymics: the pagan Rogvold Rogvoldovich and the Christian Vasily Borisovich. Anthroponymic researcher F.B. Ouspensky emphasizes that the Rurikovi
2 CONC chs had a real taboo on giving a child the same pagan name that a living father bore - that is, such a combination of name and patronymic is absolutely rare [1]. He puts forward a version that the prince's father was not really "R
2 CONC ogvold" and bore only one name "Boris", which combined both functions, but there are disputes about this (see the article about the father) [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT There are also ambiguities regarding the identity of the son. Based on the testimony of the Moscow Chronicle Code, BN Florea suggested that Vasily Rogvoldovich is the same prince who appears in later chronicles as Rogvold Borisovi
2 CONC ch [3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogvolodov stone [4]
2 CONT Rogvolod was born at the beginning of the 12th century. Until 1129, he owned some kind of inheritance in the Polotsk land. Since he later ruled in the Drutsk principality, then, according to the established point of view, this lot
2 CONC  was precisely Drutsk. He probably received it in 1127, when his father became a prince in Polotsk. When the Polotsk people drove out his uncle, Prince David, they invited the young Rogvolod-Vasily. Because of this, a conflict beg
2 CONC an between David and Rogvolod, in which the Grand Duke of Kiev Mstislav intervened and organized a large campaign in the Polotsk land. Polotsk, taking Rogvolod with them, went to Mstislav and, probably, persuaded him to approve Ro
2 CONC gvolod as a Polotsk prince, contrary to the right of seniority. Perhaps Rogvolod should have recognized the supreme power of Kiev, which has not happened since the time of his great-grandfather Bryachislav.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1129, Mstislav was going to a campaign against the Polovtsy and ordered the Polotsk princes to take part in it, but Rogvolod and others refused with mocking words. Moreover, when Mstislav fought with the Polovtsy, Rogvolod foug
2 CONC ht with Davyd and attacked the possessions of Mstislav's relatives. Returning from the campaign, Mstislav sent the governor to the Polotsk land, they captured many Polotsk princes, including Rogvolod, and brought them to Kiev. In 
2 CONC 1130, the "trial of the Russian princes" took place over the Polotsk Izyaslavichi, according to which they, together with their families, were deprived of their inheritance and sent to Byzantium.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the permission of Grand Duke Yaropolk Vladimirovich, Rogvolod returned from exile in 1140 with his brother Ivan. It is possible that he again received the Drutsk principality. In 1143, according to the Nikon Chronicle, he mar
2 CONC ried the daughter of one of the sons of Mstislav the Great, Izyaslav Mstislavich, then the prince of Pereyaslavl [5]. It is possible that it was thanks to this marriage that in 1144 [6] after the death of Vasilko Svyatoslavich he 
2 CONC managed to sit down in Polotsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, soon in the Polotsk land feuds began between representatives of various lines of the Polotsk princes. In 1151, the inhabitants of Polotsk revolted against Rogvolod, captured him and handed him over to the Minsk prince Ros
2 CONC tislav Glebovich, who imprisoned Rogvolod. Rostislav himself sat in Polotsk, and in the ancestral inheritance of Rogvolod (Drutsk) he planted his son Gleb. Later, at the request of the Suzdal prince Yuri Dolgoruky, Rogvolod was re
2 CONC leased from prison and found shelter in Chernigov with Prince Svyatoslav Olgovich, an ally of Yuri Dolgoruky.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1158 the inhabitants of Drutsk invited him to reign. Rogvolod, with the help of the army that Svyatoslav Olgovich gave him, expelled Gleb Rostislavich from Drutsk, regaining his inheritance. The expulsion of Gleb Rostislavich f
2 CONC rom Drutsk caused a war between the Minsk Glebovichs and Rogvolod - the Glebovichs besieged Drutsk, but the assaults were unsuccessful. Finally, they made peace, according to which they left Drutsk to him and added a number of oth
2 CONC er volosts, and he refused Polotsk in their favor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1159, by agreement, the Polotsk people expelled Rostislav Glebovich from Polotsk and again called Rogvolod, asking him for forgiveness, which had once been given to the Minsk princes. After that Rogvolod made an attempt to unit
2 CONC e the Polotsk land under his rule. In the same year 1159, he captured Izyaslavl, expelling Vsevolod Glebovich, Rostislav's brother, who was sitting there, and imprisoning Bryachislav Vasilkovich from the Vitebsk line there. Rogvol
2 CONC od gave Vsevolod Strezhev. After that Rogvolod went on a campaign to Minsk (the lot of Rostislav Glebovich), but after a ten-day siege of the city he made peace with Rostislav.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1160 Rostislav Glebovich captured Izyaslavl, capturing Bryachislav Vasilkovich and his brother Volodsha. In response, Rogvolod undertook a new campaign against Minsk. After a six-week siege, Rostislav was forced to free Bryachi
2 CONC slav and Volodya and conclude a new peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, a more serious rival for Rogvolod turned out to be Rostislav's brother, Volodar Glebovich, who fortified himself in 1159 in Gorodets. In 1162, Rogvolod laid siege to Gorodets, however, according to the Volyn Chronicle, Vo
2 CONC lodar at night, with the help of the Lithuanians, undertook a sortie from the city and defeated the army of Rogvolod, who fled to Slutsk, and from there to Drutsk. In Polotsk, he decided not to return, in his place the Polotsk rec
2 CONC ruited Vseslav Vasilkovich. Probably until his death, he reigned in Drutsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1162, there is no news about Rogvolod, but in 1171 he was still alive. An inscription on a boulder (Rogvolodov stone) found near Orsha, dating from 1171, says:
2 CONT In the summer of 6679 of the month of May, on the 7th day, this cross was erected. Lord, help your servant Vasily in baptism, named Rogvolod, son of Borisov [7].
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogvolod died before 1180, when his son Gleb was mentioned as a prince in Drutsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: from 1143 N, daughter of the Grand Duke of Kiev Izyaslav Mstislavich [5] [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gleb (d. C. 1186), Prince Drutsky after 1171-c. 1186.
2 CONT (?) Boris [8] - a participant in the battle of 1196 against the Smolyans, took prisoner Mstislav Romanovich the Old.
2 CONT (?) Vseslav [8] (d. After 1186), Prince Drutsky from approx. 1186
2 CONT Euphrosyne (d. 8 May 1243); husband: Yaroslav Vladimirovich (d. about 1245), Prince of Izborsky, Novotorzhsky and Pskov
2 CONT (?) Rostislav [9]
2 CONT Iconography of the Prince (Obverse Chronicle of Ivan IV the Terrible)
2 CONT
2 CONT Polotsk invite Rogvold Drutsky to reign
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Izyaslav Mstislavich and Vsevolod Olgovich marry Izyaslav's daughter on Rogvold in Polotsk
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rogvolod agrees with the Druchans about the occupation of the prince's table
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rostislav, Vsevolod, Volodar, Izyaslav Glebovich and Rostislav's son Gleb besiege Drutsk. Prince Rostislav makes peace with Rogvolod, the troops of the princes withdraw from Drutsk
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rostislav flees from Polotsk to Minsk, Rogvolod becomes Prince of Polotsk
2 CONT
