1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicholas_Pok
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Nicholas from the kindred Pok (Hungarian: Pok nembeli Miklós)'''
2 CONT ; c. 1245 – after 19 August 1319; fl. 1270–13
2 CONT Was a Hungarian influential lord in the Kingdom of Hungary at the turn of the 13th and 14th centuries. He held positions in the royal court in the 1270s. He acquired extensive landholdings and estates in the area between the river
2 CONC s Tisza and Szamos (Someș). He was among the so-called oligarchs, who ruled de facto independently their dominion during the era of feudal anarchy. He was also ancestor of the Meggyesi noble family, thus later charters also referr
2 CONC ed to him as '''Nicholas Meggyesi'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT He was born into the wealthy Pok kindred, which originated from Győr County. He belonged to the Mórichida branch, which erected a Premonstratensian monastery in 1251 at Mórichida.[1] The branch was founded by Maurice I, Nicholas' 
2 CONC grandfather, who served King Andrew II as his Master of the stewards from 1233 to 1235.[2] His only known son was Maurice II, Nicholas' father, who held several positions (most notably Master of the treasury) in the court of King 
2 CONC Béla IV at least until 1269.[3] He married a daughter of Dominic I Rátót (she died before 1267) whom Nicholas was born around 1245. He had three younger brothers, Maurice III, Stephen I and Dominic, who were mentioned only once in
2 CONC  1280 when they were excommunicated due to "tyrannical behaviour". After Maurice's death, the brothers jointly owned Pok, Tét, Baráti and Mórichida in Győr County, along with other sporadic lands throughout the kingdom.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas appeared first in contemporary records in 1270. According to the sources he married twice; his first wife was Elizabeth, a daughter of Mojs (II) Dárói, Palatine of Hungary between 1270 and 1272. She died before 1280. They
2 CONC  had at least three sons: Maurice IV, the ispán of Győr County from 1337 to 1338, Stephen II, the ispán of Máramaros County between 1326 and 1327, and magister Nicholas II. Maurice's son was Simon Meggyesi, Ban of Croatia, thus Ni
2 CONC cholas was also an ancestor of the influential Meggyesi family. After Elizabeth's death, he married for the second time to Catherine, daughter of Andrew from the Kaplon kindred. She survived her husband and died sometime after 133
2 CONC 1.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is a widely accepted academic standpoint that the wife of Palatine Mojs II (Nicholas' mother-in-law) had family relationship with the Árpád dynasty, the royal house of Hungary through Queen Elizabeth the Cuman thus Nicholas Pok
2 CONC  was also part of the Árpáds' distant kinship.[6] Furthermore, Palatine Mojs' other daughter was engaged to Henry II Kőszegi.[7] Through his granddaughter Anne, Nicholas Pok was also maternal ancestor of the royal house of Báthory
2 CONC  de Somlyó, which ruled in Transylvania and Poland in the 16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT B1. Miklós Meggyesi, Erdélyi vajda (Vajda of Transylvania) (1315-16), Pohárnokmester (magister pincernarum), Bereg, Szolnok, Máramaros Ugocsa vármegyék főispánja, fl 1270, +after 1319; 1m: Erzsébet Dárói, dau.of Moys Dárói by Pss 
2 CONC Erzsébet of Halics; 2m: Erzsébet Nagymihályi, dau.of András Nagymihályi, from Kaplyon family(euweb)
2 CONT
