1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Constantine "Armless"''' (Konstantin Bezrukiy) (1240 - 1280s) - Duke of Polotsk (1270 - 1280s). He came from the Dukes of Vitebsk, most likely the son of the Polotsk prince Bryachislav II, Tautvilas' brother-in-
2 CONC law.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took the throne of Polotsk for the first time in 1263 with the support of Treniota, after the attack on Tautvilas and the capture of the Polotsk boyars. In the same year, 1263, he handed over a part of the Polotsk estate in eas
2 CONC tern Latgale to the Order of the Knights, probably to gain support in the struggle for the throne of Polotsk. The so-called "gift of Constantine" was approved by the Pope. These actions of Constantine did not satisfy the Polotsk p
2 CONC eople, who overthrew him in 1264. With the support of Wojalka, probably right after Treniota freed the Polotsk boyars. Konstantin returned to Vitebsk, and Gerdzen took the table in Polotsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Izyaslav II, he took the Polotsk table for the second time, this time by the will of the people of Polotsk. Bishop of Polotsk Semyon mentions him somewhere in the 1870s - 1880s as a "good Polotskian prince", and
2 CONC  also between 1285 and 1300 in a letter from the Riga magistrate to his son, the Vitebsk prince Mikhail Konstantinovich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor: Prince Izyaslav II of Polotsk 70's - 1280 
2 CONT Heir: Warrior-Basil 
