1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Saint Dmitry (Dimitrii) Ivanovich Donskoy (Russian: Дми́трий Ива́нович Донско́й) also known as Dmitry of the Don, sometimes referred to as Dmitry I
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 12 October 1350, Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 19 May 1389, Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT Father:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan II of Moscow
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandra Ivanovna Velyaminova
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudoxia Dmitriyevna
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniil Dmitriyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Yury Vasiliyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily Vasilyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Sofiya Dmitriyevna
2 CONT
2 CONT Yury Dmitriyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Dmitriyevna
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia Dmitriyevna
2 CONT
2 CONT Simeon Dmitriyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Dmitriyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrey Dmitriyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Pyotr Dmitriyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Dmitriyevna
2 CONT
2 CONT Konstantin Dmitriyevich
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor: Ivan II http://www.geni.com/people/Ivan-II-The-Fair-Grand-Prince-of-Moscow/361885079530012036#/tab/overview
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor: Vasily I http://www.geni.com/people/Vasily-I-Grand-Prince-of-Moscow/6000000000671850343
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dmitri_Donskoi
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Dmitry reigned as the Prince of Moscow from 1359 and Grand Prince of Vladimir from 1363 to his death. He was the first prince of Moscow to openly challenge Mongol authority in Russia. His nickname, Donskoy (i.e., "of the Don
2 CONC "), alludes to his great victory against the Tatars in the Battle of Kulikovo (1380) which took place on the Don River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry ascended the throne of Principality of Moscow at the age of 9. During his minority, the government was actually run by Metropolitan Aleksey of Russia. In 1360 the highest dignity among Russian princes, that of Grand Prince 
2 CONC of Vladimir, was transferred by a Khan of the Golden Horde upon Dmitry Konstantinovich of Nizhniy Novgorod. In 1363, when that prince had been deposed, Dmitry Ivanovich was finally crowned at Vladimir. Three years later, he made p
2 CONC eace with Dmitriy Konstantinovich and married his daughter Eudoxia. In 1376, their joined armies ravaged Volga Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most important event during the early years of Dmitry's reign was construction of the first stone Moscow Kremlin, completed in 1367. The new fortress allowed the city to withstand two sieges by Algirdas of Lithuania, in 1368 a
2 CONC nd 1370. Attempt for the third siege in 1372 ended in Treaty of Lyubutsk. In 1375, Dmitry managed to settle his conflict with Mikhail II of Tver over Vladimir in his favour. Other princes of Northern Russia also acknowledged his a
2 CONC uthority and contributed their troops to his impending struggle against the Horde. By the end of his reign, Dmitry more than doubled territory of Moscow principality.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry's thirty-year reign saw the beginning of the end for Mongol domination of parts of what is now Russia. The Golden Horde was severely weakened by civil war and dynastic rivalries. Dmitry took advantage of this lapse in Mongo
2 CONC l authority to openly challenge the Tatars.
2 CONT
2 CONT While he kept the Khan's patent to collect taxes for all of Russia, Dmitry is also famous for leading the first Russian military victory over the Mongols. Mamai, a Mongol general and claimant to the throne, tried to punish Dmitry 
2 CONC for attempting to increase his power. In 1378 Mamai sent a Mongol army, but it was defeated by Dmitry's forces in the Battle of Vozha River Two years later Mamai personally led a large force against Moscow. Dmitry met and defeated
2 CONC  it at the Battle of Kulikovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The defeated Mamai was presently dethroned by a rival Mongol general, Tokhtamysh. That khan reasserted Mongol rule over parts of what now is Russia and overran Moscow for Dmitry's resistance to Mamai. Dimitry, however, pledged his
2 CONC  loyalty to Tokhtamysh and to the Golden Horde and was reinstated as Mongol principal tax collector and Grand Duke of Vladimir. Upon his death in 1389, Dimitry was the first Grand Duke to bequeath his titles to his son Vasiliy wit
2 CONC hout consulting the Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Eudoxia of Nizhniy Novgorod. She was a daughter of Dmitry of Suzdal and Vasilisa of Rostov. They had at least twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniil Dmitriyevich (c. 1370 - 15 September 1379).
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasiliy I of Moscow (30 September 1371 – 27 February 1425).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sofia Dmitriyevna. Married Fyodor Olegovich, Prince of Ryazan (reigned 1402-1427).
2 CONT
2 CONT Yuriy Dmitriyevich, Duke of Zvenigorod and Galich (26 November 1374 – 5 June 1434). Claimed the throne of Moscow against his nephew Vasiliy II of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Dmitriyevna (d. 15 May 1399). Married Lengvenis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia Dmitriyevna. Married Ivan Vsevolodovich, Prince of Kholm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simeon Dmitrievich (d. 11 September 1379).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Dmitriyevich (d. 1393).
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrey Dmitriyevich, Prince of Mozhaysk (14 August 1382 – 9 July 1432).
2 CONT
2 CONT Pyotr Dmitriyevich, Prince of Dmitrov (29 July 1385 – 10 August 1428).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Dmitriyevna (born 8 January 1387). Married Yury Patrikiyevich. Her husband was a son of Patrikas, Prince of Starodub and his wife Helena. His paternal grandfather was Narimantas. The marriage solidified his role as a Boyar at
2 CONC tached to Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Konstantin Dmitriyevich, Prince of Pskov (14 May/15 May 1389 - 1433).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dmitry Donskoy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Saint Dmitry Ivanovich Donskoi (Russian: Дми́трий Донско́й), or Dimitri of the Don, sometimes referred to as Dmitry I (12 October 1350, Moscow – 19 May 1389, Moscow), son of Ivan II Krasnyi, reigned as the Prince of Moscow from 13
2 CONC 59 and Grand Prince of Vladimir from 1363 to his death. He was the first prince of Moscow to openly challenge Tatar authority in Russia. His nickname, Donskoi (i.e., "of the Don"), alludes to his great victory against the Tatars i
2 CONC n the Battle of Kulikovo (1380) which took place on the Don River.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Early reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry ascended the throne of Principality of Moscow at the age of 9. During his minority, the government was actually run by Metropolitan Alexis of Russia. In 1360 the highest dignity among Russian princes, that of Grand Prince o
2 CONC f Vladimir, was transferred by a Khan of the Golden Horde upon Dmitri Konstantinovich of Nizhny Novgorod. In 1363, when that prince had been deposed, Dmitri Ivanovich was finally crowned at Vladimir. Three years later, he made pea
2 CONC ce with Dmitri Konstantinovich and married his daughter Eudoxia. In 1376, their joined armies ravaged Volga Bulgaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most important event during the early years of Dmitri's reign was construction of the first stone Moscow Kremlin, completed in 1367. The new fortress allowed the city to withstand two sieges by Algirdas of Lithuania, in 1368 a
2 CONC nd 1370. Attempt for the third siege in 1372 ended in Treaty of Lyubutsk. In 1375, Dmitri managed to settle his conflict with Mikhail II of Tver over Vladimir in his favour. Other princes of Northern Russia also acknowledged his a
2 CONC uthority and contributed their troops to his impending struggle against the Horde. By the end of his reign, Dmitri more than doubled territory of Moscow principality.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Struggle against Mamai
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitri's thirty-year reign saw the beginning of the end for Mongol domination of parts of what is now Russia. The Golden Horde was severely weakened by civil war and dynastic rivalries. Dmitri took advantage of this lapse in Mongo
2 CONC l authority to openly challenge the Tatars.
2 CONT
2 CONT While he kept the Khan's patent to collect taxes for all of Russia, Dmitri is also famous for leading the first Russian military victory over the Mongols. Mamai, a Mongol general and claimant to the throne, tried to punish Dmitri 
2 CONC for attempting to increase his power. In 1378 Mamai sent a Mongol army, but it was defeated by Dmitri's forces in the Battle of Vozha River Two years later Mamai personally led a large force against Moscow. Dmitri met and defeated
2 CONC  it at the Battle of Kulikovo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The defeated Mamai was presently dethroned by a rival Mongol general, Tokhtamysh. That khan reasserted Mongol rule over parts of what now is Russia and overran Moscow for Dmitri's resistance to Mamai. Dmitri, however, pledged his 
2 CONC loyalty to Tokhtamysh and to the Golden Horde and was reinstated as Mongol principal tax collector and Grand Duke of Vladimir. Upon his death in 1389, Dmitri was the first Grand Duke to bequeath his titles to his son Vasili withou
2 CONC t consulting the Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Eudoxia of Nizhny Novgorod. She was a daughter of Dmitry of Suzdal and Vasilisa of Rostov. They had at least twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniil Dmitrievich (c. 1370 - 15 September 1379).
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasily I of Moscow (30 September 1371 - 27 February 1425).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sofia Dmitrievna. Married Fyodor Olegovich, Prince of Ryazan (reigned 1402-1427).
2 CONT
2 CONT Yury Dmitrievich, Duke of Zvenigorod and Galich (26 November 1374 - 5 June 1434). Claimed the throne of Moscow against his nephew Vasily II of Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Dmitrievna (d. 15 May 1399). Married Lengvenis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anastasia Dmitrievna. Married Ivan Vsevolodich, Prince of Kholm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simeon Dmitrievich (d. 11 September 1379).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Dmitrievich (d. 1393).
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrei Dmitrievich, Prince of Mozhaysk (14 August 1382 - 9 July 1432).
2 CONT
2 CONT Piotr Dmitrievich, Prince of Dmitrov (29 July 1385 - 10 August 1428).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Dmitrievna (born 8 January 1387). Married Yuri Patrikievich. Her husband was a son of Patrikej, Prince of Starodub and his wife Helena. His paternal grandfather was Narimantas. The marriage solidified his role as a Boyar atta
2 CONC ched to Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Konstantin Dmitrievich, Prince of Pskov (14 May/15 May 1389 - 1433).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
