1 TITL Prince
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Leo II of Galicia, also known as Lev Yurevich (unknown – 1323) was Prince of Lutsk and Galicia, one of the last two Romanovichi kings of Rus` (r. 1308–1323; according to some sources, 1315–23[citation needed]). He 
2 CONC was a member of the senior branch of Monomakhovichi.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of King Yuri I (1252–1308), whom he succeeded on the royal throne of Galicia-Volhynia. After the death of his father, he ruled the kingdom together with his brother Andrew. His mother was Euphemia of Kuyavia. Accord
2 CONC ing to most sources, the brothers were co-kings, managing the kingdom together, but there are sources indicating that Lev II was seated in Halych and Andrew in Volodymyr (and yet others stating a similar arrangement, but with the 
2 CONC seats reversed), which would seem to imply split jurisdiction. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Both King Lev and his brother King Andrew were apparently much respected in the west. In May 1323, Polish King Władysław I, in a letter to the Pope, wrote with obvious regret: "Two last Ruthenian (Ukrainian) kings, that had been f
2 CONC irm shields for Poland from Tatars, left this world and after their death Poland is directly under Tatar threat.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1321, Lev's son, Prince George of Lutsk, the last of the line, had died in a battle with the forces of Grand Duke Gediminas of Lithuania while defending the castle in Lutsk. After the deaths of Lev II and his brother two years 
2 CONC later, the line of the direct descendants of Roman Mstislavich ended, and the Galician state was left without a ruling dynasty. The Galician boyars (nobility) subsequently attempted to rule the state themselves. Fearing subjugatio
2 CONC n by Władysław I, they apparently sought the protection of the Tatars. But soon more conservative elements amongst the boyars gained the upper hand, and the Galician throne was given to the Mazovian Duke Boleslaw Trojdenowicz (who
2 CONC  was related to the deceased royal brothers through the marriage of his father Trojden I of Masovia to their sister Maria). - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_II_of_Galicia
