1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Wingold''' (''Wingont, Вингонт, Wingolt, Вонгут''; c.1325 – August 12, 1399 Battle of the Vorskla River) was the Grand Prince of Trubetsk February 14]], 1386 – 1388, Pskov 1341 – 1343, 134? – 1348, 1377 – 1386, 
2 CONC 1394 – 1396, Polotsk 1342 – 1387, and Lukoml 1342 – 1387. In 1342 he was baptized Andrey (Andrew) in Moscow as Orthodox, and on February 14, 1386, in Krakow as Roman Catholic. He is from the Gediminid dynasty, the eldest son of th
2 CONC e Grand Duke of Lithuania Olgierd and the Duke of Vitsebsk Maria Yaroslavna.
2 CONT
2 CONT He spent his childhood in Vitebsk. In 1341 he came to Pskov with his father Algirdas and his uncle Kęstutis. At the request of the Pskov people, who were hoping for help in fighting the Livonian Order, Wingold was appointed prince
2 CONC  of Pskov. In 1342 he became the wealthier prince of Polotsk, but he also continued to rule in Pskov, where he appointed Yuri Vitovtovich as deputy ruler. In 1348 Pskov became again independent of the Duchy of Novgorod and establi
2 CONC shed again The Principality of Pskov, which was an aristocratic oligarchy, commonly called the Pskov Republic.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the later part of the 14th century, Wingold’s nephew Grand Prince Vytautas and Dmitry Donskoi of Moscow started a rivalry for fertile southern lands of Ruthenia, formerly controlled by the Golden Hord. After the death of the de
2 CONC puty governor in 1348, the people of Pskov took advantage of the situation and no longer recognized Wingold as their own prince. In 1359, Wingold exiled Vasily Mikhailovich's deputy rulers from the city of Rzhev and returned the c
2 CONC ity to Vsevolod Alexandrovich Holmski, who had been hiding from the persecution of Vasily Kashinsky and the Tatars in Lithuania since 1358. In 1362 he took part in the Battle of the Blue Waters near South Bug.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1368 and 1370, his father took part in campaigns against the principality of Moscow, which was undertaken to restore the rights of Mikhail Alexandrovich, the prince of Tver, which the prince of Moscow did not want
2 CONC  to recognize. Simeon's letopiss says that in 1373 Wingold and Kęstutis went on a looting trip in the possession of the Prince of Perejaslav.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April and September 1374 and November 1375, he led a military expedition to the Vana-Väina Order Fortress. After the German invasion of Lithuania in February 1375, Wingold, together with his three brothers, Kęstutis and the son
2 CONC  of the Duke of Smolensk (Sviatoslavich), looted the estates of the Archbishop of Riga and the Knights of Livonia. In August 1376, Rēzekne (Rēzekne) attacked the castle. In March 1377, the Russian-Lithuanian forces took part in a 
2 CONC military expedition to Courland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Wingold was probably his father's eldest child, his stepmother Juliana of Tver's sons did not allow him to rule after the death of his father, Grand Duke Algirdas, in Vilnius in 1377, and he entered the principality of Mo
2 CONC scow under contracts with Grand Duke Dmitry Donskoy and Vladimir Andreevich Serpukhovsky. Wingold was named Grand Duke in Moscow documents. With Dmitry's permission, he regained control of the Principality of Pskov. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the winter of 1377/1378, he came to Novgorod, from where he moved on to Moscow. In 1378, he took part in the Battle of the Vojvodina River, in which Russian troops defeated Begeth, the leader of the Golden Horde. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1379, after the Mongol invasion of Ruthenia, The Principality of Trubetsk passed to Wingold’s brother Dymitr Korybut, the Grand Prince of Briańsk etc., and then to Wingold, and the other|Princes of Trubetsk. In the winter of 13
2 CONC 79-1380, Wingold fought with his step-brother (vassal of his father Algirdas) Dmitry of Bryansk against his half-brothers (sons of Juliana) to defend the land of the prince in the Bryansk, Starodub, Trubetsk, Novgorod-Seversk regi
2 CONC ons, which they owned jointly. In 1380, they brought their comrades into the forces of Dmitry Donskoy to take part in the Battle of Kulikovo, which prevented the forces of Grand Duke Jogaila and the Golden Horde of the Grand Duchy
2 CONC  of Lithuania, Russia and Samogitia from joining. Dmitry Donskoy's troops won the battle. As the Tatar power was on the wane, Dmitry soundly defeated the Horde at the Battle of Kulikovo (1380), only to be besieged in Moscow severa
2 CONC l years later by Khan Tokhtamysh.
2 CONT
2 CONT The strengthening ties of The Principality of Pskov with Muscovy had been caused by economic development and foreign policy objectives, Pskov’s participation in the Battle of Kulikovo in 1380, and the successful joint struggle aga
2 CONC inst The Monastic state of the Teutonic Knights.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Kęstutis exiled Jogaila and Juliana to Kreva and Vitsebsk in 1381, Wingold returned to the Grand Duchy of Lithuania to rule Polotsk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When King Louis I of Hungary died in 1382, the Archbishop of Cracow Jan Radlica crowned the younger daughter as "Jadwiga, King of Poland" – technically speaking, she was a King, not a Queen. The szlachta prevailed upon her to end 
2 CONC her engagement with William, Duke of Austria, and instead to marry Wingold’s brother Jogaila, Grand Prince of Lithuania, whose country had returned to Slavic paganism after a brief period of the Christian Kingdom of Lithuania in t
2 CONC he 1250s.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of 1385, Wingold opposed the Krevo Union, stating that "Jogaila… wants and desires to accept the Catholic faith of the Holy Roman Church'' and "… promises to unite his Lithuanian and Russian lands with the crown of the 
2 CONC Kingdom of Poland forever."
2 CONT
2 CONT In October-November, Wingold formed a coalition against Jogaila. By February 2, 1386, he conquered the area around Vilnius and Ašmena and in February subjugated Lukomli. Jogaila quickly sent Vytautas and Skirgaila to Lithuania wit
2 CONC h Polish forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 14-15, 1386 brothers Wingold, Jogaila, Korybut, Lengvenis, Karigaila, Wigunt-Aleksander etc. were baptized and converted to Catholicism at the Wawel Cathedral in Krakow. Jogaila was crowned King of Poland as Władysław,
2 CONC  a name chosen in honor of Jadwiga's great-grandfather, the unifier. They managed to stop the Knights of the German Order, defeat Wingold Lukomlis, defeat Sviatoslav Ivanovich in the battle of Mstislav in the spring of 1386, and c
2 CONC onquer Polotsk in the spring of 1387. Grand Duke Wingold was fraudulently imprisoned and deported to Chęciny Castle in Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1390, Vytautas exiled Skirgaila from Polotsk. Either after this event or after the Astravas agreement between Jogaila and Vytautas, Wingold returned to Polotsk. In the autumn of 1393, Wingold came to Novgorod with the ambassado
2 CONC rs of Pskov to sign an agreement. In the spring of 1394, metropolitan Kiprian also came to Novgorod. However, both the people of Novgorod and the metropolitan people renounced it.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1399. As a result of the new conflicts within the Golden Horde, Tokhtamysh was dethroned by the party of khan Temur Kutlukh and emir Edigey, supported by the great Tamerlane. When Tokhtamysh asked Vytautas for assistance, the latt
2 CONC er readily gathered a huge army that included Ruthenians, Lithuanians, Mongols, Muscovites, Poles, Romanians, and Teutonic knights. His army met the Tatars at the Vorskla River, a tributary to the Dnieper. Although the Lithuanian 
2 CONC army was well equipped with cannons, it could not resist a rear attack from Edigey's reserve units. Vytautas could hardly escape alive, many princes of his kin were killed, although as Wingold, his step-brother Dmitry "the Elder" 
2 CONC with son Iwan Skindyr, Stefan Musat, Prince of Moldavia, and two of his brothers, while the fourth was badly injured, and the victorious Tatars besieged Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''And the Christian blood had flown like a water, up to the Kievan walls", 
2 CONT
2 CONT as one chronicler put it. Meanwhile, Temur Kutlukh died from the wounds received in the battle, and Tokhtamysh was killed by one of his own men. Like many other Lithuanian-Russian princes, Wingold fell in the Battle of the Vorskla
2 CONC  River on August 12, 1399. Vytautas's defeat at the Vorskla effectively blocked the expansion of The Grand Principality of Lithuania to Southern Ruthenia. His enormous state also lost hard-won access to the Black Sea. The politica
2 CONC l activity of Lithuanians was then switched to the fight for northern principalities, such as The Principality of Smolensk. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Memory 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2009, a statue of Wingold was erected in Polotsk (sculptor I. Golubev). The Wingold Museum is located in Polotsk, at 3 Engels Street. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mother: Maria of Vitebsk (? – 1346)
2 CONT * Father: Algirdas (c.1296 – end of May 1377), Grand Prince of Lithuania 1345 – 1377
2 CONT
