1 TITL Begum.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Qutlugh Nigar Khanum (also spelled Kutlak Nigar Khanum; d. 1505) was the first wife and chief consort[1] of Umar Shaikh Mirza II, the ruler of Ferghana Valley. She was a princess of Moghulistan by birth and was a d
2 CONC aughter of Yunus Khan, the Great Khan of Moghulistan.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also the mother of Emperor Babur, the founder of the Mughal Empire of India as well as its first emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and lineage Qutlugh Nigar Khanum was born as a princess of Moghulistan and was the second daughter of Yunus Khan, the Great Khan of Moghulistan, and his chief consort Aisan Daulat Begum.[2] Her paternal grandfather was Uwai
2 CONC s Khan, the Moghul Khan of Moghulistan and her father's predecessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qutlugh was a direct descendant of Chenghiz Khan, the founder and Great Khan (Emperor) of the Mongol Empire through her father's side. Being the daughter of a Khan, Qutlugh held the title of "Khanum" ("daughter of a Khan or prince
2 CONC ss") by birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT All of Qutlugh's siblings became her sisters-in-law since her elder sister, Mihr Nigar Khanum, married Sultan Ahmed Mirza, the eldest son of Abu Sa'id Mirza. Her younger sister, Khub Nigar Khanum, married Sultan Ahmed Mirza's succ
2 CONC essor, Sultan Mahmud Mirza.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Qutlugh married Prince Umar Shaikh, the fourth son of Abu Sa'id Mirza, the Emperor of the Timurid Empire in 1475. She was his first wife and chief consort. Before his death, her father-in-law had divided his empire betwee
2 CONC n his sons and Umar was given to rule Ferghana Valley in today's Uzbekistan. Here, at Andizhan, she gave birth to Umar's eldest son, Prince Babur on 14 February 1483.[4] Babur went on to become the founder of the Mughal Empire of 
2 CONC India and was the first Mughal emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qutlugh also gave birth to Umar's eldest daughter, Princess Khanzada Begum, who was five years older than Babur and was born sometime in 1478. Qutlugh, being a Mongol princess, was well educated. With the untimely death of her hus
2 CONC band, when her son Babur was only ten, Qutlugh and her mother Aisan Daulat Begum, raised him themselves.[5] She accompanied her son during most of his guerrilla expeditions and throne-less times.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death Qutlugh Nigar had fever for six days and died in June 1505, five or six months after Babur conquered Kabul. She, therefore, did not live to see her son become Mughal emperor. She was buried in New Year's Garden. Babur paid 1
2 CONC ,000 coined misqal to his kinsmen, the owners of the garden, and laid her there.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qutlugh_Nigar_Khanum
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Qutlugh Nigar Khanum (also spelled Kutlak Nigar Khanum; d. 1505) was the first wife and chief consort of Umar Shaikh Mirza II, the King of Ferghana Valley. She was born a princess of the Chagatai Khanate as a daughter of Yunus Kha
2 CONC n, the Great Khan of Moghulistan and his chief consort Aisan Daulat Begum.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was also the mother of Emperor Babur, the founder of the Mughal Empire of India as well as its first Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qutlugh Nigar Khanum was born a princess of the Chagatai Khanate as the second daughter of Yunus Khan, the Great Khan of Moghulistan and his chief consort Aisan Daulat Begum.[1] Her paternal grandfather was Uwais Khan, the Moghul 
2 CONC Khan of Mughalistan and her father's predecessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qutlugh was a direct descendant of Genghis Khan, the founder and Great Khan (Emperor) of the Mongol Empire through her father's side. Being the daughter of a Khan, Qutlugh held the title of "Khanum" ("daughter of a Khan or princes
2 CONC s") by birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT All of Qutlugh's siblings became her sisters-in-law since her elder sister, Mihr Nigar Khanum, married Sultan Ahmed Mirza, the eldest son of Qutlugh's father-in-law, Abu Sa'id Mirza. Her younger sister was Khub Nigar Khanum, who m
2 CONC arried Sultan Mahmud Mirza.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage[edit] Qutlugh married Prince Umar Shaikh, the fourth son of Abu Sa'id Mirza, the Emperor of the Timurid Empire in 1475. She was his first wife and chief consort. Before his death, her father-in-law had divided his empire 
2 CONC between his sons and Umar was given to rule Ferghana Valley in today's Uzbekistan. Here, at Andizhan, she gave birth to Umar's eldest son, Prince Babur on 14 February, 1483.[3] Babur went on to become the founder of the Mughal Emp
2 CONC ire of India and was the first Mughal Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Qutlugh also gave birth to Umar's eldest daughter, Princess Khanzada Begum who was five years older than Babur and was born sometime in 1478. Qutlugh, being a Mongol princess, was well educated. With the untimely death of her husb
2 CONC and, when her son Babur was only ten, Qutlugh and her mother Aisan Begum raised him themselves.[4] She accompanied her son during most of his guerrilla expeditions and throne-less times.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Qutlugh Nigar had fever for six days and died in June of 1505, five or six months after Babur conquered Kabul. She, therefore, did not live to see her son become Mughal Emperor. She was buried in New Year's Garden. Babur paid 1,00
2 CONC 0 coined misqal to his kinsmen, the owners of the garden, and laid her there.
