1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Kara Koyunlu or Qara Qoyunlu, also called the Black Sheep were a Shitite Turcoman tribal federation that ruled over the territory comprising the present-day Armenia, Republic of Azerbaijan, Iranian Azerbaijan, 
2 CONC western Iran, eastern Turkey and Iraq from about 1375 to 1468.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kara Koyunlu Turkomans at one point established their capital in Herat in eastern Persia, and were vassals of the Jalayirid dynasty in Baghdad and Tabriz from about 1375, when the leader of their leading tribe, ruled over Mosu
2 CONC l. However, they rebelled against the Jalayirids, and secured their independence from the dynasty with the conquest of Tabriz by Qara Yusuf.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1400, the armies of Tamerlane defeated the Kara Koyunlu, and Qara Yusuf fled to Egypt seeking refuge with the Mamluks. He gathered an army and by 1406 had taken back Tabriz. In 1410, the Kara Koyunlu captured Baghdad. The insta
2 CONC llation of a subsidiary Black Sheep Turkomans line there hastened the downfall of the Jalayirids whom they had once served. Despite internal fighting amongst Kara Yusuf's descendants after his death in 1420, and the increasing thr
2 CONC eat of the Timurids, the Black Sheep Turkomans maintained a strong grip over the areas they controlled. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For further information please visit the below links:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rulers_of_Kara_Koyunlu
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qara_Yusuf
2 CONT
2 CONT http://cahanshah.blogspot.com/
2 CONT
2 CONT Also read: https://www.academia.edu/3604069/Armenia_from_the_fall_of_the_Cilician_Kingdom_1375_to_the_forced_emigration_under_Shah_Abbas_1604_1997?email_work_card=thumbnail
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [He (Tamerlan) punished the Turkomans of Kara Yusuf for their depredations and captured their stronghold of Van.] p.666 of the History of the Mongols, the Mongols of Persia, by Sir Howorth.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # Nicol, Donald M. “The Last Centuries of Byzantium, 1261-1453.”  2nd edition, Cambridge University Press.  1993. Page 403-404. < [https://books.google.com/books?id=y2d6OHLqwEsC&lpg=PA403&vq=Koyunlu&pg=PA404#v=onepage&q=Koyunlu&f=
2 CONC true GoogleBooks] > Manuel III is mentioned but no daughters married into the Koyunlu.
