1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ivan Asen I of Bulgaria
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivan_Asen_I_of_Bulgaria Wikipedia], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Asen I (also Ioan Asen I, Ioan Asan, in English John Asen I) (Bulgarian: Иван Асен I) ruled as emperor (tsar) of Bulgaria 1189-1196. The year of his birth is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1185, the brothers Theodore (Todor) and Ivan Asen appeared before the Byzantine Emperor Isaac II Angelos at Kypsela to request a pronoia. Their request was turned down, and when Ivan Asen dared to argue against the emperor's de
2 CONC cision, he was slapped across the face [1]. Irate, the brothers returned home and, taking advantage of the discontent at the heavy taxation levied by Isaac II to finance his wars against William II of Sicily and his marriage to Ma
2 CONC rgaret of Hungary, raised a revolt against Byzantine rule in late 1185.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Ivan Asen played the more active part in the operations against the Byzantines, his older brother Theodore was proclaimed Emperor of the Bulgarians under the name Peter IV (Petăr IV). An early assault on the old capital P
2 CONC reslav failed, and the probable center of the revolt, Tărnovo, became the capital of the Second Bulgarian Empire. During 1185 and 1186 the Bulgarians took over most of Moesia and raided across the Balkan mountains into Thrace.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1186 Isaac II Angelos marched with a large army against the Bulgarians, and penetrated into Moesia. While Peter IV showed himself ready to negotiate with the Byzantine emperor, Ivan Asen fled across the Danube and
2 CONC  raised a strong force of Cumans, with whom he returned to relieve his brother. Isaac II had already left for Constantinople, contenting himself with Peter IV's promises for obedience. With his new force, Ivan Asen proceeded to ra
2 CONC id Thrace again, skillfully avoiding pitched battles against superior Byzantine armies.
2 CONT
2 CONT A new expedition led by Isaac II proceeded to Sredec (Sofia) in 1186, but it did not impact the territories under the control of the restored Bulgarian state. The Byzantine Emperor attacked again in 1187, this time threatening Tăr
2 CONC novo and besieging Loveč for three months without success. During the course of the siege the Byzantines captured the wife of Ivan Asen, who was exchanged for Ivan Asen's younger brother Kaloyan (Kalojan) as a hostage at the concl
2 CONC usion of a truce. But neither side had intentions to keep the peace. When the Third Crusade led by Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor was advancing towards Constantinople, representatives of Peter IV and Ivan Asen approached him with
2 CONC  offers of military assistance against the latently hostile Isaac II Angelos at Niš and again at Adrianople.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the passing of the Third Crusade, Isaac II Angelos decided to deal with the Bulgarians decisively. The expedition was planned on a grand scale and reached Tărnovo before besieging it for a protracted period. By this time Pet
2 CONC er IV had crowned Ivan Asen I as co-emperor in 1189 and, without abdicating, retired to Preslav. In charge of the defense of the Tărnovo, Ivan Asen I incited the Byzantine emperor to hastily retreat by spreading rumors of the arri
2 CONC val of a great Cuman army to the relief of the besieged city. The retreating Byzantine army was ambushed by Ivan Asen I in the Balkan passes and Isaac II barely escaped with his life in 1190.
2 CONT
2 CONT Success now definitely swung in favor of the Bulgarians, who captured the areas of Sredec (Sofia) and Niš in 1191, of Belgrade in 1195, of Melnik and Prosek in 1196, while raiding parties reached as far south as Serres. During his
2 CONC  return from the southwest, Ivan Asen I was murdered by Ivanko, one of his military commanders, who was threatened with punishment for an affair with the sister of Ivan Asen I's wife. The murderer attempted to assume control in Tă
2 CONC rnovo and negotiated with Constantinople, to which he soon afterwards fled.
2 CONT
2 CONT For other events of Ivan Asen I's reign, and for a short note on the ethnic and political character of the Second Bulgarian Empire, see under Peter IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Asen I was married first to a certain Maria, buried at Červen, and then to Elena (religious name Evgenija), whose antecedents are unknown. She is sometimes alleged to be a daughter of Stefan Nemanja of Serbia, but this relati
2 CONC onship is questionable and would have caused various canonical impediments to marriages between their descendants. By his marriage to Elena, Ivan Asen I had at least two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Asen II, emperor of Bulgaria 1218-1241
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander (Aleksandăr), sebastokratōr, who died after 1232; Alexander had a son named Kaliman Asen II, emperor of Bulgaria in 1256
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Honour
2 CONT
2 CONT Asen Peak on Livingston Island in the South Shetland Islands, Antarctica is named for Ivan Asen I.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Nicetas Choniates, Historia, ed. J.-L. Van Dieten, 2 vols. (Berlin and New York, 1975); trans. as O City of Byzantium, Annals of Niketas Choniates, by H.J. Magoulias (Detroit; Wayne State University Press, 1984)
2 CONT
2 CONT John V.A. Fine, Jr., The Late Medieval Balkans, Ann Arbor, 1987.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivan Asen I, also known as Asen, I or John Asen I (Bulgarian: Иван Асен I; died in 1196) was emperor (or tsar) of Bulgaria from 1187 or 1188 to 1196 as the co-ruler with his elder brother, Peter II. He was the son of a wealthy she
2 CONC pherd from the mountains of the Byzantine theme (district) of Paristrion. All contemporaneous chronicles describe Asen I and his brothers as Vlachs.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Asen-7
