0 @N6000000002187576449@ NOTE Ariq Boke
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ariq Boke or Arigh Bukha (died 1266), the youngest son of Tolui, was a grandson of Genghis Khan and a claimant to the Mongol Empire. His brothers were Möngke Khan, Hulagu Khan and Kublai Khan.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1259 the Great Khan Möngke died. As Ariq Boke was commander of the Mongol homelands, and as Kublai Khan was campaigning against Song China at the time of Möngke's death, he decided to attempt to make himself Khan. Hearing of th
1 CONC is, Kublai aborted his Chinese expedition and had himself elected as Khan in April of 1260. Still, Ariq Boke had his supporters and was elected as a rival to Kublai at Karakorum. The brothers then engaged in a series of battles, e
1 CONC nding with Ariq Boke's capture in 1264. Kublai held him prisoner until he died two years later.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ariq Boke is also known for being sympathetic towards Christianity.
0 @N6000000002188002122@ NOTE Kulan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kulan or Khulan, was an empress of Genghis Khan and head of the second Court of Genghis Khan. Her status in the Mongol Empire was second only to Grand Empress Borte.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kulan was a daughter of a Merkit chief. She was offered to Genghis Khan as a gift after the chief's surrender. Genghis Khan was enamored of Kulan and following his coronation as Khan, installed her as an empress. She had a son, Ge
1 CONC lejian, with Genghis Khan, and Gelejian's status was second only to Borte's four sons as he grew up. Genghis Khan was very fond of Kulan, and most of the time she was the only empress accompanying him on many campaigns, notably th
1 CONC e western campaign against the Khwarezmid Empire. She continued to travel with Genghis Khan until she died during one of Genghis Khan's campaign against India. She was buried under thick ice.
1 CONT
1 CONT As one of many wives of Genghis Khan, Kulan held a special place both in the Mongol Empire and in Genghis Khan's heart.
0 @N6000000002188002149@ NOTE Yesugei
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei Baghatur or Yesugei The Brave (11XX–1180) (Yesügei, Yesükhei) was a Mongol Khan (or ruler). He was the son of Bartan-baghatur, who was the son of Qabul, who was recognized as a Khagan by the Jin Dynasty. Qabul Khagan was, 
1 CONC in turn, the grandson of the Mongol chief Qaidu, the first to try to unite all of the Mongols.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei was chief of the Kiyad (possibly the Taichiut or Yakka) khanate between approximately 1153 and 1180. He had four sons and one daughter by his chief wife, Hoelun: Temujin (son, later known as Genghis Khan), Kasar (son), Kha
1 CONC ji'un (son), Temuga (son), and Temulin (daughter). He also had two sons by a lesser wife: Bektor and Beigutai. He was poisoned by Tartars while sharing a meal on the way home from finding his son Temujin (Genghis Khan) a wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yesugei's wife was Oyelun (Oelun, Ho’elun, Hoelun), a daughter of the Olkunut forest tribe. Oyelun was abducted by Yesugei and his brothers from her newlywed husband of the Merkit tribe as they were traveling back to the Merkit ca
1 CONC mp. Yesugei then made Oyelun his chief wife, a rare honor since Khans had several wives, and only one could bear his heirs.
0 @N6000000002188002161@ NOTE Hoelun
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hoelun was the mother of Mongol Khan Genghis Khan and the grandmother of Ogedei Khan, first emperor of Yuan Dynasty. She was the wife of Yesükhei, the chief of the Kiyad clan. After Yesükhei's death, his clan abandoned Hoelun and 
1 CONC his sons including Genghis Khan (Temujin by birth). Therefore Genghis Khan was raised in the harsh environment of the Mongolian steppes. According to Mongolian legend, Hoelun taught Genghis and his brothers the basics of unity and
1 CONC  support for one another, even though Temujin killed his brother in a disagreement over hunting spoils.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hoelun was born to the Olkunut tribe. She later married a member of the Merkit tribe, but was abducted by Yesugei on her way back to the Merkit camp. Yesugei made Hoelun his chief wife. This was an honor, since only the chief wife
1 CONC  could give birth to his heirs.
0 @N6000000002188002170@ NOTE Jöchi Khasar
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jöchi Khasar (or Qasar) was one of Chinggis Khan's three full brothers. According to the Jami al-Tawarikh, his given name was Jöchi and he got the nickname Khasar after his distinguished bravery. He was also called Khabutu Khasar 
1 CONC (Khasar the Archer) because he was a skillful archer.
1 CONT
1 CONT Being given the people and territories by the khan, Chinggis Khan's full brothers Khasar, Khachiun and Temüge Odchigin formed the Left Wing of the Mongol Empire in the eastern edge of Greater Mongolia while Chinggis Khan's three s
1 CONC ons Jochi, Chaghatai and Ögödei made up the Right Wing in the western edge. The Right Wing saw a significant expansion to the west but the Left Wing did not have so much land to be conquered.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unlike the Right Wing where properties were equally divided, Odchigin was favored over Khasar and Khachiun in the Left Wing. Khasar's ulus (people and secondarily, territory) was significantly smaller than Odchigin's. His original
1 CONC  territory was located to the west of the Khingan Mountains and was surrounded by the Argun (Ergüne) and Khayilar Rivers, and the Külün Mountain. After the conquest of China, Khasarid princes had at least two additional territorti
1 CONC es in Shandong and Jiangxi respectively.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Descendants
1 CONT The princely houses of Khasar, Khachiun and Odchigin tended to coordinate with the five powerful clans Jalayir, Khunggirad, Ikires, Uruud and Mangghud. They were usually led by princes from Odchigin's house. At Arigh Bukha's rebel
1 CONC lion, the three princely houses supported Khubilai under leadership of Odchigin's grandson Taghachar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among Khasarid princes, the third family head Yesüngge is probably the most famous. He was a son of Khasar and succeeded his brother Yegü. He is the hero of the Yesüngge Inscription (formerly known as the Chinggis Stone). The prin
1 CONC cely house was succeeded by Yesüngge's son Esen Emügen and then Emügen's son Shigdür. Although Shigdür joined the rebellion against Khubilai led by Odchigin's great-great-grandson Nayan, the princely house survived without confusi
1 CONC on. The sixth head Babusha was given the title of Qi Wang (??) by Khayishan Külüg Khan in 1307. It seems that Khasarid princes continued to hold the title even after the empire retreated from China.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is unknown what happened to Khasarid princes from the late Yuan Dynasty to the middle 15th century because of the confusion caused by the Mongols' withdrawal from China. Mongolian chronicles compiled from the 17th century to th
1 CONC e early 18th century contain some records on Khasar's descendants but they are considered mostly unhistorical by historians. In particular, Altan Tobchi by Mergen Gegen (not to be confused with Lubsandanjin's Altan Tobchi) exagger
1 CONC ated the influence of Khasarid princes as the author himself descended from Khasar.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is widely accepted that Khasar's descendant Bolunai was a historical figure since his existence is confirmed in contemporary Chinese sources of 1463, 1467 and 1470. Mongolian chronicles say that Bolunai's brother Ünebalad kille
1 CONC d Muulikhai of the Ongliud, a descendant of Chinggis's half brother Belgütei. Another famouns story about Ünebalad tells that he proposed to Mandukhai Khatun, a widow of Manduulun Khan but that she chose the Chinggisid infant Batu
1 CONC  Möngke (Dayan Khan) over him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bolunai led the Khorchin Mongols. His descendants ruled the Khorchin, Jalayid, Dörbed and Ghorlus of the Jirim League, the Aru Khorchin of the Juu Uda League and the Dörben Keüked, Muu Mingghan and Urad of the Ulaanchab League in 
1 CONC the Manchu Qing Empire's administration. Among them, Khorchin princes established matrimonial relationship with the imperial family of Aisin Gioro at the early stage of the Manchu rise to power, and held top-ranking princely title
1 CONC s (hošoi cin wang) throughout the Qing Dynasty.
0 @N6000000002188002426@ NOTE Abaqa Khan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Abaqa Khan (1234–1282), the son of Hulagu and Oroqina Khatun, a Mongol Christian. As the second Il-Khan emperor in Persia, he reigned from 1265–1282.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his reign, Abaqa, a devout Buddhist, attempted to convert the Muslims and harassing them mercilessly by promoting Nestorian and Buddhist interests ahead of the Muslims, by sending embassies to Pope Gregory X and Edward I of
1 CONC  England. During his harsh reign, many Muslims had attempted to assassinate Abaqa. In 1265, upon his succession, he received the hand of Maria Despina Palaiologina, the illegitimate daughter of Emperor Michael VIII Palaeologus, in
1 CONC  marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of Hulagu's death from illness in 1265, Abaqa, on his father's behalf, continued to fight the Golden Horde forces of Berke Khan until 1267, the time of Berke's death. However, Barrak Khan tried to annex Iran in 1270 an
1 CONC d thus starting war against Abaqa in the city of Herat. Abaqa successfully defended Iran, leaving his people victorious. Abaqa was succeeded by his Muslim brother Tekuder.
1 CONT
1 CONT Abaqa was later assassinated. The assassin viewed Maria as part of his rightful inheritance, but she fled back to Constantinople where her father, apparently wishing to spare his capital the fate that befell Baghdad, tried to marr
1 CONC y her off again to another Mongol khan. Maria could not accept the offer, became a nun and founded The Church of Panagia Mouchliotissa around 1285.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Khan genealogy 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hulagu Ilkhanid Dynasty
1 CONT 1265–1282 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tekuder 
0 @N6000000002188002279@ NOTE Michael VIII Palaiologos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Michael VIII Palaiologos or Palaeologus (Greek: ???a?? ?? ?a?a???????, Mikhael VIII Palaiologos) (1224/1225 – December 11, 1282) reigned as Byzantine emperor 1259–1282. Michael VIII was the founder of the Palaeologan dynasty that 
1 CONC would rule the Byzantine Empire until the Fall of Constantinople in 1453. He recovered Constantinople from the Latin Empire in 1261 and transformed the Empire of Nicaea into a restored Byzantine Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Road to the throne 
1 CONT 2 Reign 
1 CONT 3 Family 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Road to the throne
1 CONT Michael VIII Palaiologos was the son of the megas domestikos Andronikos Doukas Komnenos Palaiologos by Theodora Angelina Palaiologina, the granddaughter of Emperor Alexios III Angelos and Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina. Even with 
1 CONC our imperfect knowledge of Byzantine genealogy, no less than eleven emperors may be traced among his ancestors. He was one of the noblest men among the Byzantine aristocracy, and may have succeeded to the throne in regular fashion
1 CONC  if the Fourth Crusade had not been diverted to Constantinople in 1203.
1 CONT
1 CONT At an early age he rose to distinction, and ultimately became commander of the Latin mercenaries in the employment of the emperors of Nicaea. A few days after the death of Emperor Theodore II Doukas Laskaris in 1259, Michael Palai
1 CONC ologos replaced the influential bureaucrat George Mouzalon (when the latter was murdered), becoming joint guardian for the eight-year old Emperor John IV Doukas Laskaris together with the patriarch Arsenios. Michael was invested w
1 CONC ith the titles of megas doux and, in November 1258, of despotes. On January 1, 1259 Michael VIII Palaiologos was proclaimed co-emperor at Nymphaion with the help of the Republic of Genoa.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT On July 25, 1261, Michael VIII's general Alexios Strategopoulos captured Constantinople from its last Latin Emperor, Baldwin II. Michael VIII entered the city on August 15 and had himself crowned together with his infant son Andro
1 CONC nikos II Palaiologos.
1 CONT
1 CONT In December John IV, who had been left behind at Nicaea, was blinded and relegated to a monastery. Patriarch Arsenios excommunicated Michael VIII, and the ban was not removed until six years later (1268) on the appointment of new 
1 CONC patriarch Joseph I. After rendering John IV ineligible for the throne, Michael VIII quickly married off John's sisters to foreigners, so their descendants could not threaten his own children's claim to the imperial succession.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his entrance in Constantinople, Michael VIII Palaiologos abolished all Latin customs and reinstated most Byzantine ceremonies and institutions as they had existed before the Fourth Crusade, repopulating the capital and restorin
1 CONC g damaged churches, monasteries, and public buildings. He was acutely aware of the danger posed by the possibility that the Latin West, particularly his neighbors in Italy (Charles I of Sicily, Pope Martin IV, and the Venetians) w
1 CONC ould unite against him and attempt the restoration of Latin rule in Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Michael kneeling in front of Christ, in this coin issued to celebrate the liberation of the capital of the Empire from the Crusaders.In 1259 Michael VIII had defeated the alliance of William II Villehardouin, Prince of Achaea, and
1 CONC  Michael II of Epirus at the Battle of Pelagonia. With the help of Pope Urban IV Michael VIII concluded peace with his former enemies in 1263 and 1264, respectively. By the terms of the treaties, William II was obliged to cede Mys
1 CONC tras, Monemvasia and Maina in the Morea to the Byzantines. Michael VIII relied on an alliance with Genoa against Venice and the Latin states of the Aegean Sea, but in the end made treaties with both Genoa and Venice, seeking to ma
1 CONC intain a balance of power advantageous to the Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT To drive a wedge between the pope and supporters of the Latin Empire, Michael VIII decided to unify the Orthodox and Catholic Churches. A tenuous union between the Greek and Latin church was signed at the Second Council of Lyons i
1 CONC n 1274. Michael VIII's concession was met with determined opposition at home, and prisons filled with many opponents to the union.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the same time the unionist controversy helped drive Byzantium's Orthodox neighbors Serbia and Bulgaria into the camp of Michael VIII's opponents. This threat did not materialize in a significant way during Michael VIII's reign,
1 CONC  and the emperor took advantage of a civil war in Bulgaria to conquer the Bulgarian portion of Thrace in the late 1270s and to temporarily impose his son-in-law Ivan Asen III on the Bulgarian throne. For a while the diplomatic int
1 CONC ent of the union worked out in the West, but in the end Pope Martin IV, an ally of Charles of Anjou, excommunicated Michael VIII.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a rare manifestation of truly "Byzantine" diplomacy, Michael VIII secretly incited the Sicilian Vespers, a rebellion against Charles of Anjou in Palermo, and the invasion of Sicily by the Catalans of King Peter III of Aragon. T
1 CONC his halved the kingdom of Charles of Anjou, who was forced to spend the remainder of his life unsuccessfully trying to reassert his control over Sicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT In reconstituting the Byzantine Empire Michael VIII restored the old administration without endeavouring to correct its failures, and by debasing the coinage he hastened the decay of Byzantine commerce. In recovering Constantinopl
1 CONC e and investing in the defense of his European provinces, Michael VIII began to denude the Anatolian frontier of its troops and was forced to lower their pay or cancel their tax exemptions. This policy led to the gradual collapse 
1 CONC of the frontier, which was infiltrated by Turkish bands even before the death of Michael VIII in Thrace in December 1282. The Palaiologan dynasty he established ruled the Byzantine Empire for almost two centuries, longer than any 
1 CONC other in Roman history.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT In 1253, Michael VIII Palaiologos married Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, a grandniece of John III Doukas Vatatzes, Emperor of Nicaea. Orphaned in childhood, she was raised by her great-uncle John III, who was said to have "loved her
1 CONC  like a daughter", and who arranged for her marriage to Michael. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Manuel Palaiologos (c. 1254–1259) 
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos (1259–1332) 
1 CONT Constantine Palaiologos (1261–1306) 
1 CONT Eirene Palaiologina, who married emperor Ivan Asen III of Bulgaria 
1 CONT Anna Palaiologina, who married Demetrios Angelos 
1 CONT Eudokia Palaiologina, who married Emperor John II of Trebizond 
1 CONT Theodora Palaiologina, who married King David VI Narin of Georgia and Imereti 
1 CONT By a mistress, a Diplovatatzina, Michael VIII also had two illegitimate daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Euphrosyne Palaiologina, who married Nogai Khan of the Golden Horde 
1 CONT Maria Palaiologina, who married Abaqa Khan of Ilkhanid Persia 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Nicol, Donald. The Last Centuries of Byzantium, 1261-1453, 1993 
1 CONT Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991 
1 CONT Vannier, J-F. Les premiers Paléologues (Etudes prosopographiques), 1989 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Michael coinage: http://www.wildwinds.com/coins/byz/michael_VIII/t.html 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT John IV Byzantine Emperor
1 CONT 1259–1282
1 CONT with John IV in 1259–1261 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Andronikos II 
0 @N6000000002188002318@ NOTE Andronikos II Palaiologos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos or Andronicus II Palaeologus (Greek: ??d??????? ?' ?a?a???????) (1259/1260 – February 13, 1332), reigned as Byzantine emperor 1282–1328. Andronikos II Palaiologos was the eldest surviving son of Michael V
1 CONC III Palaiologos and Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, grandniece of John III Doukas Vatatzes.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos was acclaimed co-emperor in 1261, after his father Michael VIII recovered Constantinople from the Latin Empire, but he was crowned only in 1272. Sole emperor from 1282, Andronikos II immediately repudiate
1 CONC d his father's unpopular Church union with the Papacy, but was unable to resolve the related schism within the Orthodox clergy until 1310. Andronikos II was also plagued by economic difficulties and during his reign the value of t
1 CONC he Byzantine hyperpyron depreciated precipitously while the state treasury accumulated more than seven times less revenue (in nominal coins) than it had done previously. Seeking to increase revenue and reduce expenses, Andronikos 
1 CONC II raised taxes and reduced tax exemptions, and dismantled the Byzantine fleet in 1285, thereby making the Empire increasingly dependent on the rival republics of Venice and Genoa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos sought to resolve some of the problems facing the Byzantine Empire through diplomacy. After the death of his first wife, he married Yolanda (renamed Eirene) of Montferrat, putting an end to the Montferrat
1 CONC  claim to the Kingdom of Thessalonica. Andronikos II also attempted to marry off his son and co-emperor Michael IX Palaiologos to the Latin Empress Catherine I of Courtenay, thus seeking to eliminate Western agitation for a restor
1 CONC ation of the Latin Empire. Another marriage alliance attempted to resolve the potential conflict with Serbia in Macedonia, as Andronikos II married off his five-year old daughter Simonis to King Stefan Milutin in 1298.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spite of the resolution of problems in Europe, Andronikos II was faced with the collapse of the Byzantine frontier in Asia Minor. After the failure of the co-emperor Michael IX to stem the Turkish advance in Asia Minor in 1300,
1 CONC  the Byzantine government hired the Catalan Company of Almogavars (adventurous from Aragon and Catalonia) led by Roger de Flor to clear Byzantine Asia Minor from the enemy. In spite of some successes, the Catalans were unable to s
1 CONC ecure lasting gains. They quarreled with Michael IX, and eventually turned on their Byzantine employers after the murder of Roger de Flor in 1305, devastating Thrace, Macedonia, and Thessaly on their road to Latin Greece. There th
1 CONC ey conquered the Duchy of Athens and Thebes. The Turks continued to pentrate the Byzantine possessions, and Prousa fell in 1326. By the end of Andronikos II's reign, much of Bithynia was in the hands of the Ottoman Turks of Osman 
1 CONC I and his son and heir Orhan.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Empire's problems were exploited by Theodore Svetoslav of Bulgaria, who defeated Michael IX and conquered much of northeastern Thrace in c. 1305–1307. The conflict ended with yet another dynastic marriage, between Michael IX's
1 CONC  daughter Theodora and the Bulgarian emperor. The dissolute behavior of Michael IX's son Andronikos III Palaiologos led to a rift in the family, and after Michael IX's death in 1320, Andronikos II disowned his grandson, prompting 
1 CONC a civil war that raged, with interruptions, until 1328. The conflict precipitated Bulgarian involvement, and Michael Asen III of Bulgaria attempted to capture Andronikos II under the guise of sending him military support. In 1328 
1 CONC Andronikos III entered Constantinople in triumph and Andronikos II was forced to abdicate. He died as monk in 1332.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT In 1274 Andronikos II married as his first wife Anna a daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary, with whom he had two sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Michael IX Palaiologos 
1 CONT Constantine Palaiologos, despotes 
1 CONT After Anna died in 1281, in 1284 Andronikos II then married Yolanda (renamed Eirene), a daughter of Marquis William IX of Montferrat, with whom he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT John Palaiologos (c. 1286–1308), despotes 
1 CONT Theodore I, Marquis of Montferrat (1291–1338) 
1 CONT Demetrios Palaiologos (d. after 1343), despotes 
1 CONT Simonis Palaiologina (1294–after 1336), who married King Stefan Milutin of Serbia 
1 CONT Andronikos II also had at least two illegitimate daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eirene, who married John II Doukas, ruler of Thessaly 
1 CONT Maria, who married Toqta, khan of the Golden Horde 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Michael VIII Byzantine Emperor
1 CONT 1282–1328
1 CONT with Michael IX in 1294/1295–1320
1 CONT with Andronikos III in 1325–1328 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Andronikos III 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium, Oxford University Press, 1991. 
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
0 @N6000000002188002347@ NOTE Eudokia Palaiologina
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudokia Palaiologina or Eudocia Palaeologina (Greek: ??d???a ?a?a???????a) (c. 1265 – 1302) was the third daughter of Byzantine Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos and his wife, Theodora Doukaina Vatatzina, a grandniece of Emperor Jo
1 CONC hn III Doukas Vatatzes of Nicaea.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1282 Eudokia married in Constantinople John II Megaskomnenos, Emperor of Trebizond with whom she had two sons, Alexios and Michael. In 1298, after her husband's death and ascension of her son Alexios II, she took the younger so
1 CONC n with her and returned to her brother's court at Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andronikos II Palaiologos received his sister well, promising to send her back whenever she wished to her elder son, whose position — such was the official Byzantine version — he did all in his power to consolidate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudokia had become entirely devoted to Trapezuntine interests. She refused a second marriage with King Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia, as she wished to keep her troth to her first consort, and secretly opposed her brother’s plan
1 CONC  of marrying Alexios II to a daughter of the high court official Nikephoros Choumnos. She pretended, however, to approve this match, so that, under pretence of making arrangements for her son’s wedding, she might return to Trebizo
1 CONC nd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, Alexios II decided for himself to marry an Iberian princess. His uncle Andronikos II wanted this marriage annulled, so Eudokia, on the pretext of inducing her son to dissolve the marriage, succeeded in returning to Treb
1 CONC izond in 1301, where she advised her son to keep his Iberian wife. Eudokia died in the following year, and was perhaps buried in the church of Saint Gregory.
0 @N6000000000437345766@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Hungary
0 @N6000000002187576487@ NOTE Stefan Dragutin of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Stefan Dragutin, king of Serbia (1276-1282), king of Srem (1282-1316), fresco, detail, 1296 Kingdom of SremStefan Dragutin (died March 12, 1316) was King of Serbia from 1276 to 1282 and king of Srem from 1282 to 1316.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Family 
1 CONT 3 See also 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Dragutin was the eldest son of king Stefan Uroš I by Helena. He married Katarina, daughter of Hungarian king Stephen V. He was the leading Hungarophile in Serbian politics, and because of this he came in conflict with his father, 
1 CONC from whom he then usurped the throne in 1276 with help of the Hungarians. He began to attack the Byzantine Empire but had no success.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1282 he broke his leg while hunting and became ill; he hence gave the throne to his younger brother Stefan Milutin (the council at Deževo 1282) while keeping for himself some northern parts of the country. Since his son Vladisl
1 CONC av married the cousin of Hungarian king Andrija II, Dragutin in 1284 gained from Hungarian king areas of Macva with Belgrade, and the Bosnian areas Soli and Usora, which he ruled until 1316, establishing family relations with Bosn
1 CONC ian ban Stefan I Kotromanic. His new state was named Kingdom of Srem. First capital of his state was Debrc (between Belgrade and Šabac), and latter he moved his residence to Belgrade. He is the first of the Serb rulers who ruled f
1 CONC rom the capital of Belgrade.
1 CONT
1 CONT In that time the name Srem was designation for two territories: Upper Srem (present day Srem) and Lower Srem (present day Macva). Kingdom of Srem under the rule of Stefan Dragutin was actually Lower Srem. Some historical sources m
1 CONC ention that Stefan Dragutin also ruled over Upper Srem and Slavonija, but the other sources mention another local ruler who ruled over Upper Srem. The name of this ruler was Ugrin Cak.
1 CONT
1 CONT Near the end of the 13th century Stefan Dragutin expanded his territory, adding Branicevo and Kucevo. He came into conflict with his brother Milutin when Milutin wanted to create stronger political ties with the Byzantine Empire a
1 CONC nd obviously did not intend to give the throne to Dragutin's son Vladislav as agreed. Conflict between the brothers was calmed by the clergy in 1313. Near the end of his life he separated from his Hungarian friends and strengthene
1 CONC d his connections in Serbia. He later became a monk and changed his name to Teoktist. He died in 1316 and was buried in the Ðurdevi Stupovi monastery near Novi Pazar. After Dragutin died, new ruler of the Kingdom of Srem became hi
1 CONC s son Stefan Vladislav II.
1 CONT
1 CONT His legacy includes some splendid monasteries, most notable among them: monastery Mala Remeta on Fruška Gora, dedicated to the Shroud of the Mother of God; St. Achilleos church near Arilje (1296); and a monastery Ðurdevi stupovi w
1 CONC ith St. George church in Ras (now Novi Pazar), where he was buried.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT His marriage in about 1268 with Katarina, daughter of Stephen V of Hungary produced at least four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Jelisaveta or Elisaveta (??????????), married in 1284 to the Bosnian ban Stefan I Kotromanic. 
1 CONT Ursula, married before 1300 to Pavle Šubic (????? ?????). 
1 CONT Stefan Vladislav II, king of Srem (1316-1325). 
1 CONT Urošic (??????), died as a monk. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT History of Serbia 
1 CONT History of Vojvodina 
1 CONT List of Serbian monarchs 
1 CONT Rulers of Vojvodina 
1 CONT Srem 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš I King of Serbia
1 CONT 1276–1282 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš II Milutin 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT John V.A. Fine, Jr., The Late Medieval Balkans, Ann Arbor, 1987. 
1 CONT Miomir Filipovic - Fica, Tri cara i trideset i jedan kralj srpskog naroda, Cikago, 1992. 
1 CONT Drago Njegovan, Prisajedinjenje Vojvodine Srbiji, Novi Sad, 2004. 
1 CONT Small encyclopedia "Sveznanje" published by "Narodno delo", Belgrade, in 1937 which is today in public domain. This article is written from the point of view of that place and time and may not reflect modern opinions or recent dis
1 CONC coveries. 
1 CONT Age, marriage and progeny information from The geneaology and coats of arms of Serbian dynasties and feudals (?????????? ??????? ? ?????? ??????? ????????? ? ????????); editors Aleksa Ivic (1928), Dušan Spasic, Aleksandar Palavest
1 CONC ra and Dušan Mrdenovic (1987); Bata, Belgrade, ISBN 86-7685-007-0 (1928), ISBN 86-7335-050-6 (1987) (in Serbian language). 
0 @N6000000002187576528@ NOTE Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Uroš II Milutin (Serbian: ?????? ???? II ???????), (born around 1253-died in 1321), was a king of Serbia (reigned 1282–1321), and member of Nemanjic dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the youngest son of King Stefan Uroš I and his wife, Queen Helena. Unexpectedly he became king of Serbia after abdication of his brother Stefan Dragutin. He was around 29. Immediately upon his rise to throne he attacked Byz
1 CONC antine lands in Macedonia. In 1282 he conquered northern parts of Macedonia with the city of Skoplje, wnich became his capital. Byzantine emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos began preparations for the war but he died before their com
1 CONC pletion. Next year Milutin advanced with his brother deep in Byzantine territory all the way to Kavala. In 1294 Milutin also gain control in northern Albania and city of Dyrrachion, today Durrës. For the next 15 years there were n
1 CONC o changes in the war. The peace was concluded in 1299 when Milutin kept conquered lands as dowry of Simonis, daughter of emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1287 or 1288 Milutin helped his brother Stefan Dragutin to pacify two Bulgarian nobles in today's north-east Serbia, in Branicevo region. But those nobles were vassals of Bulgarian prince of Vidin Shishman who sought reveng
1 CONC e. Shishman attacked Milutin but was defeated and Milutin in return sacked his capital Vidin. But Shishman was vassal of Nogai Khan, Khan of Golden Horde. Nogai Khan threaten to punish Milutin for his insolence, but changed his mi
1 CONC nd when Serbian king sent him gifts and hostages. Among the hostages was his son Stefan Decanski who managed to escape back to Serbia after Nogai Khan's death in 1299.
1 CONT
1 CONT Disputes began between Milutin and Dragutin after peace treaty with Byzantine empire was signed in 1299. Dragutin in the meantime held lands from Branicevo in the east to Bosna river in the West. His capital was Belgrade, for the 
1 CONC first time in Serbian history. The war broke between the brothers and lasted, with sporadic cease-fire, until Dragutin's death in 1314. During this war Milutin appointed Stefan Decanski as regent in Zeta, modern Montenegro. This m
1 CONC eant that Stefan Decanski was to be heir to the throne in Serbia and not Dragutin's son Stefan Vladislav II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon Stefan Dragutin's death in 1314 Milutin conquered most of his lands including Belgrade. But in the 1319 Charles I of Hungary regained control over Belgrade and banovina Macva while Milutin held control in Branicevo. In the ye
1 CONC ar of 1314 Milutin's son Stefan Decanski rebelled against his father, but was captured, blinded and sent to exile in Constantinople. For the rest of Milutin's reign his youngest son Stefan Constantine was considered a heir to the 
1 CONC throne. But in spring 1321 Stefan Decanski returned to Serbia and was pardoned by his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the end of Milutin's life many things changed in Serbia. Serbia was one of the strongest countries in the south-eastern Europe except for Hungary. During his reign many court ceremonials were taken over from Byzantine court and
1 CONC  Byzantine culture overflow Serbia. Milutin was also well known as founder of many monasteries: Gracanica monastery, Our Lady of Ljeviš, Church of Vavedenje in Hilandar and many more. After his death a short civil war followed, af
1 CONC ter wnich Serbian throne was succeeded by his eldest son, Stefan Decanski.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT By his first wife, Helena, the daughter of John I Doukas of Thessaly, Stefan Uroš II Milutin had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Uroš III Decanski, who succeeded as king 
1 CONT Anna (Neda), who married Michael Asen III of Bulgaria 
1 CONT By his second wife, Elizabeth, the daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary or by his third wife Anna, the daughter of George Terter I of Bulgaria, Milutin had:
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Constantine, rival king of Serbia from 1321 to 1322 
1 CONT By his fourth wife Simonis, the daughter of Emperor Andronikos II Palaiologos, he had no children.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Dragutin King of Serbia
1 CONT 1282–1321 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš III Decanski 
0 @N6000000002187837154@ NOTE Roman the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman the Great or Roman Mstislavich (c. 1150-1205) was the Prince of Novgorod, Volhynia, and Halych.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman was the son of Mstislav II of Kiev. During 1168-1170 he reigned in the Great Novgorod. From 1170 to 1199 he ruled in Volodymyr-Volynsky, which he inherited from his father. In 1187, he made an unsuccessful attempt to wrestle
1 CONC  from the boyars the principality of Halych. In 1197 he married Anna, by whom he had four children, including Danylo of Halych.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman Mstislavich gained nation-wide fame by his victorious campaign against Polovtsi in 1197-1198, 1201, and 1204. In 1199, he became Prince of Halych, uniting Halichina and Volhynia lands in the mighty principality of Halych-Vol
1 CONC hynia. In 1202, he also captured Kiev, uniting all South-west Ruthenia under his rule. Prominent as a warlord, Roman the Great was also generally considered a wise politician. He lived mostly in peace with Hungary and Poland, but 
1 CONC had uneasy relationships with several other neighbouring states. He perished near the Polish city Zawichost during a march against Prince Leszek the White of Lesser Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ingvar I Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by:
1 CONT Rostislav II 
