0 @N6000000002187821006@ NOTE Alexios III Angelos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexios III Angelos or Alexius III Angelus (Greek: ??????? G' ???e???) (c. 1153 - 1211) was Byzantine emperor from 1195 to 1203.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Reign 
1 CONT 3 Fourth Crusade 
1 CONT 4 Life in exile 
1 CONT 5 Family 
1 CONT 6 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT Alexios III Angelos was the second son of Andronikos Angelos and Euphrosyne Kastamonitissa. Andronikos was himself a son of Theodora Komnene, the youngest daughter of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos and Eirene Doukaina. Thus Alexios An
1 CONC gelos was a member of the extended imperial family. Together with his father and brothers, Alexios had conspired against Emperor Andronikos I Komnenos (c. 1183), and thus he spent several years in exile in Muslim courts, including
1 CONC  that of Saladin.
1 CONT
1 CONT His younger brother Isaac Angelos, was threatened with execution under orders of their first cousin once removed Andronikos I Komnenos on September 11, 1185. Isaac made a desperate attack on the imperial agents and killed their le
1 CONC ader Stephanos Hagiochristoporites. He then took refuge in the church of Hagia Sophia and from there appealed to the populace. His actions provoked a riot, which resulted in the deposition of Andronikos I, and the proclamation of 
1 CONC Isaac II Angelos as emperor. Alexios was now closer to the imperial throne than ever before.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT By 1190 Alexios Angelos had returned to the court of his younger brother, from whom he received the elevated title of sebastokrator. In 1195, while Isaac II was away hunting in Thrace, Alexios was acclaimed as emperor by the troop
1 CONC s with the conniving of Alexios' wife Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina. Alexios captured Isaac at Stagira in Macedonia, put out his eyes, and kept him henceforth a close prisoner, though he had been redeemed by him from captivity at
1 CONC  Antioch and loaded with honours.
1 CONT
1 CONT To compensate for this crime and to solidify his position as emperor, Alexios had to scatter money so lavishly as to empty his treasury, and to allow such licence to the officers of the army as to leave the Empire practically defe
1 CONC nceless. He consummated the financial ruin of the state. The able and forceful empress Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina tried in vain to sustain his credit and his court; Vatatzes, the favourite instrument of her attempts at reform,
1 CONC  was assassinated by the emperor's orders.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the east the Empire was overrun by the Seljuk Turks; from the north Bulgarians and Vlachs descended unchecked to ravage the plains of Macedonia and Thrace, and Kaloyan of Bulgaria annexed several important cities, while Alexios
1 CONC  squandered the public treasure on his palaces and gardens and attempted to deal with the crisis through diplomatic means. The emperor's attempts to bolster the empire's defenses by special concessions to Byzantine and Bulgarian n
1 CONC otables in the frontier zone backfired, as the latter built up regional autonomy. Byzantine authority survived, but in a much weakened state.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Fourth Crusade
1 CONT Soon Alexios was threatened by a new and yet more formidable danger. In 1202 the Western princes assembled at Venice launched the Fourth Crusade. Alexios Angelos, the son of the deposed Isaac II, had recently escaped from Constant
1 CONC inople and now appealed to the crusaders, promising to end the schism of East and West, to pay for their transport, and to provide military support to the crusaders if they helped him to depose his uncle and sit on his father's th
1 CONC rone.
1 CONT
1 CONT The crusaders, whose objective had been Egypt, were persuaded to set their course for Constantinople before which they appeared in June 1203, proclaiming Alexios as emperor and inviting the populace of the capital to depose his un
1 CONC cle. Alexios III took no efficient measures to resist, and his attempts to bribe the crusaders failed. His son-in-law, Theodore Laskaris, who was the only one to attempt anything significant, was defeated at Scutari, and the siege
1 CONC  of Constantinople began.
1 CONT
1 CONT On July 17/18 the crusaders, led by the aged Doge Enrico Dandolo, scaled the walls and took the city by storm. During the fighting and carnage that followed Alexios III hid in the palace, and finally, with one of his daughters, Ei
1 CONC rene, and such treasures as he could collect, got into a boat and escaped to Develton in Thrace, leaving his wife and his other daughters behind. Isaac II, drawn from his prison and robed once more in the imperial purple, received
1 CONC  his son in state.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life in exile
1 CONT Alexios attempted to organize a resistance to the new regime from Adrianople and then Mosynopolis, where he was joined by the later usurper Alexios V Doukas Mourtzouphlos in April 1204, after the definitive fall of Constantinople 
1 CONC to the crusaders and the establishment of the Latin Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first Alexios III received Alexios V well, even allowing him to marry his daughter Eudokia. Later Alexios V was blinded and deserted by his father-in-law, who fled from the crusaders into Thessaly. Here Alexios III eventually s
1 CONC urrendered, with Euphrosyne, to Marquis Boniface of Montferrat, who was establishing himself as ruler of the Kingdom of Thessalonica.
1 CONT
1 CONT Trying to escape Boniface's "protection", Alexios III attempted to seek shelter with Michael I Doukas, the ruler of Epirus, in 1205. Captured by Boniface, Alexios III and his retinue were sent to Montferrat, before being brought b
1 CONC ack to Thessalonica c. 1209. At that point the deposed emperor was ransomed by Michael I of Epirus, who sent him to Asia Minor, where Alexios' son-in-law Theodore I Laskaris of the Empire of Nicaea was holding his own against the 
1 CONC Latins.
1 CONT
1 CONT Here Alexios III conspired against his son-in-law after the latter refused to recognize Alexios' authority, and received the support of Kay Khusrau I, the sultan of Rüm. In the battle of Antioch on the Maeander River in 1211, the 
1 CONC sultan was defeated and killed, and Alexios III was captured by Theodore Lascaris. Alexios III was relegated to a monastery at Nicaea, where he died later in 1211.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT By his marriage to Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina Alexius had three daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eirene Angelina, who married (1) Andronikos Kontostephanos, and (2) Alexios Palaiologos, by whom she was the grandmother of Emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos. 
1 CONT Anna Angelina, who married (1) the sebastokrator Isaac Komnenos, great-nephew of emperor Manuel I Komnenos, and (2) Theodore I Laskaris, emperor of Nicaea. 
1 CONT Eudokia Angelina, who married (1) King Stefan I Prvovencani of Serbia, then (2) Emperor Alexios V Doukas, and (3) Leo Sgouros, ruler of Corinth. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT Michael Angold, The Byzantine Empire, 1025-1204: A Political History, second edition (London and New York, 1997) 
1 CONT C.M. Brand, Byzantium Confronts the West (Cambridge, MA, 1968) 
1 CONT Jonathan Harris, Byzantium and the Crusades (London and New York, 2003). 
1 CONT The Oxford Dictionary of Byzantium (Oxford , 1991), 3 vols. 
1 CONT K. Varzos, E genealogia ton Komnenon (Thessalonica, 1984) 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Isaac II Byzantine Emperor
1 CONT 1195–1203 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Isaac II and Alexios IV 
