1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Anna of Byzantium was a Byzantine noblewoman (+ after 1219), the Princess of Galicia ca 1200 - 1205, and the Grand Princess of Kiev 1203 - 1205.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Husband
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman the Great (born ca 1150, murdered near Zawichost 19 June 1205), Prince of Novgorod 1168 - 1170, Prince of Volynia 1173 - 1187, 1188 - 1205, Prince of Galicia 1187 - 1188, 1199 - 1205, Grand Prince of Kiev 1203 - 1205 
1 CONT Sons
1 CONT
1 CONT Danylo (*1201, +1264), Prince of Galicia 1211 - 1213 and 1229 - 1264, Prince of Volynia 1215 - 1264, crowned by a papal archbishop in Dorohychyn 1253/1255 as the 1st King of Galicia 
1 CONT Vasiliko (* 1203, + 1269), Prince of Belz 1207 - 1211, Prince of Brest 1221 - 1231, Prince of Volhynia 1231 - 1269 
1 CONT Daughters
1 CONT
1 CONT Feodora of Galicia (+ after 1200), m. 1187 (div 1188) Vasilko of Galicia 
1 CONT Maria of Galicia (+ after 1241), m. before 1200 Mikhail of Chernigov and Kiev 
0 @N6000000002188005061@ NOTE Daniel of Halych
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Danylo of Halych (Ukrainian: ?????? ?????????, Danylo Halytsky), (Russian: ?????? ????????, Daniil Galitski), (1201-1264) Knyaz of Halych (1205–1206, 1211–1212, 1229–1231, 1233–1235, 1238–1255), Peremyshl (1211), and Volodymyr-Vol
1 CONC ynsky (1212–1231). He was crowned by a papal archbishop in Dorohychyn 1253 as the 1st King of Halych-Volhynia (1253–1264).
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Biography 
1 CONT 2 Family 
1 CONT 3 See also 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT In 1205, after the death of his father, Roman Mstyslavich, the ruler of Halych-Volynia, the boyars of Galicia forced the four-year-old Danylo into exile with his mother Anna (probably of Greek extraction) and brother Vasylko. Afte
1 CONC r the boyars proclaimed one of their own as prince in 1213, the Poles and Hungarians invaded the principality, ostensibly to support the claims of young Danylo and Vasylko, and divided it between themselves. In 1219 he renounced h
1 CONC is claims to Halych in favour of his father-in-law Mstislav the Bold.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1221 Danylo re-established his rule over Volhynia, where the boyars and populace had remained loyal to his dynasty. By 1238, he had defeated the Teutonic Knights, and regained most of Galicia, including the capital at Halych. W
1 CONC hile the Prussians were under pressure from the Teutonic Order, Daniel attempted to conquer the related Yatvingians.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following year, Danylo acquired Kiev, the traditional capital of the defunct state of Kievan Rus'. Faced with the Mongol menace, he sent his commander Dmytro to defend the city. However, after a long siege its walls were breac
1 CONC hed and despite fierce fighting within the city, Kiev fell on December 6, 1240 and was largely destroyed. A year later, the Mongols passed through Galicia and Volhynia while campaigning against the Poles and Hungarians, destroying
1 CONC  Halych. On 17 August 1245, Danylo defeated a combined force of the Prince of Chernihiv, disaffected boyars, and Hungarian and Polish (see also Order of Dobrin) elements at Yaroslav and finally took the remainder of Galicia, thus 
1 CONC reconstituting his father's holdings. He made his brother Vasylko ruler of Volhynia and retained the Galician title for himself, though he continued to exercise real powers in both places.
1 CONT
1 CONT Danylo's domestic policies focused on stability and economic growth. During his rule, German, Polish, and Ukrainian merchants and artisans were invited into Galicia, and numbers of Armenians and Jews established themselves in the 
1 CONC towns and cities. Danylo founded the towns of Lviv (1256) and Kholm (naming the former for his son), and fortified many others. He appointed officials to protect the peasantry from aristocratic exploitation and formed peasant-base
1 CONC d heavy infantry units.
1 CONT
1 CONT Yet Danylo's successes and his failed defense of Kiev attracted the further attention of the Mongols. In 1246, he was summoned to the capital of the Golden Horde at Sarai on the Volga River and was forced to accept Mongol overlord
1 CONC ship. According to the Ukrainian historian Orest Subtelny, Danylo was handed a cup of fermented mare's milk by the Mongol khan Batu and told to get used to it, as "you are one of ours now."
1 CONT
1 CONT While formally accepting the Mongols as overlords, and supplying them with soldiers as required, Danylo built a foreign policy around opposition to the Golden Horde. He established cordial relations with the rulers of Poland and H
1 CONC ungary, and requested aid from Pope Innocent III in the form of a crusade. In return for papal assistance, Danylo offered to place his lands under the ecclesiastical authority of Rome, a pledge never realized. Wooed by the prospec
1 CONC t of extending his authority, the pope encouraged Danylo's resistance to the Mongols and his Western orientation, and in 1253, had a papal representative crown Danylo at Dorohochyn on the Bug River. The following year, Danylo repe
1 CONC lled Mongol assaults on Ponyzia and Volhynia and dispatched an expedition with the aim of taking Kiev. Despite initial successes, in 1259, a Mongol force under Burundai entered Galicia and Volhynia and offered an ultimatum: Danylo
1 CONC  was to destroy his fortifications or Burundai would assault the towns. Danylo complied and pulled down the city walls.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the last years of his reign, Danylo engaged in dynastic politics, marrying a son and a daughter to the offspring of Mindovg of Lithuania and acquiring territorial concessions in Poland from the latter. He also arranged for the 
1 CONC marriage of his son Roman to Gertrude, the Babenburg heiress, but was unsuccessful in his bid to have him placed on the ducal throne of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his death in 1264, Danylo had reconstructed and expanded the territories held by his father, held off the expansionist threats of Poland and Hungary, minimized Mongol influence on Western Ukraine, and raised the economic and so
1 CONC cial standards of his domains. He was succeeded in Galicia by his son Lev.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Roman Mstislavich Prince of Halych-Volynia
1 CONT 1238–1264 Succeeded by:
1 CONT ' 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Rostislav III of Kiev Grand Prince of Kiev
1 CONT 1239–1264 
1 CONT [edit]
