0 @N6000000002187825310@ NOTE Mieszko III the Old
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  Mieszko III the Old (Polish: Mieszko III Stary; 1121? – March 13, 1202), of the Piast Dynasty, was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and High Duke of all Poland, with interruptions, 1173-1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was the son of Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Duke of Poland, and Salome of Berg. Mieszko was Boleslaw's third son, and the ducal couple's second.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko was Duke of Greater Poland, 1138-1202, and Duke of Kraków and High Duke of all Poland, 1173-1177, 1190, 1198/1199, 1202.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko married:
1 CONT
1 CONT ca. 1140, Elizabeth of Hungary, daughter of Prince Álmos, brother of the King of Hungary; 
1 CONT ca. 1154, Eudoksia of Kiev, daughter of Izjaslav II, Grand Duke of Kyiv (Kievan Rus'). 
1 CONT Mieszko's children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Odon I of Greater Poland; 
1 CONT Boleslaw of Kujawy; 
1 CONT Mieszko of Kalisz; and 
1 CONT Wladyslaw III Spindleshanks. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT In accordance with the testament of Poland's Duke Boleslaw III Wrymouth, Poland was divided into 4-5 hereditary provinces distributed among his sons, with the royal province of Kraków being reserved for the eldest son, who was to 
1 CONC be High Duke of all Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the provinces, Greater Poland, was granted to Boleslaw's third-oldest son, Mieszko (and was subsequenly divided among Mieszko's descendants and successors). In 1173 Mieszko was promoted to the throne of Kraków and became Hi
1 CONC gh Duke of Poland (Latin: dux maximus, dux totius Poloniae); but he was soon expelled from Kraków by his younger brother, Kazimierz II the Just, and from Greater Poland by his son. Mieszko III never abdicated his rights or ambitio
1 CONC ns, and returned to Kraków four times before he died in 1202.
0 @N6000000002187837678@ NOTE Vsevolod II of Kiev
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vsevolod II Olgovich (Russian: ???????? II ????????) (? - August 1, 1146), Kniaz' (Prince) of Chernihiv (1127-1139) and Velikiy Kniaz (Grand Prince) of Kiev (1139-1146), son of Oleg Svyatoslavych, Kniaz' (Prince) of Chernihiv.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vsevolod married Maria, the daughter of Grand Duke Mstislav of Kiev. They had two sons and two daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sviatoslav III of Kiev 
1 CONT Yaroslav Vsevolodovich, born in 1139 
1 CONT Anna of Chernihiv, married a prince of Galicia according to some chronicles 
1 CONT Zvenislava of Chernihiv, married Boleslaus IV of Poland 
1 CONT Though he had two sons, Vsevolod's chosen successor was his brother, Igor, and he obtained pledges from his subjects to accept Igor as his heir. According to one accout, Vsevolod even had the Kievans kiss the Holy Cross and swear 
1 CONC loyalty to Igor, which they resented. Shortly before his death, Vsevolod became a monk under the name Gavriil.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Dimnik, Martin. The Dynasty of Chernigov, 1146-1246, 2003 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Yaropolk II Grand Prince of Kiev Succeeded by:
1 CONT Igor II 
0 @N6000000002322323103@ NOTE Kujawy was one of the small principalities formed after the Old Polish realm
1 CONT had been divided up two centuries earlier. This Casimir was its ruler. Later,
1 CONT his son, Wladyslaw I the Short, had secured Great Poland, and was crowned
1 CONT King of Poland in 1320 with the consent of the Roman Curia. This act restored
1 CONT the Polish Crown through a national dynasty, and for the next 475 years the
1 CONT crown was to remain the symbol of unity for Poland. The Piast Dukes welcomed
1 CONT immigrant Germans & Flemings into Polish territories & settlement took place.
