0 @N6000000000337202301@ NOTE (Research):Ottocar I Ottocar I Pronounced As: otkär or Premysl Ottocar I przhemisl ôtôkär , d. 1230, duke (1197-98) and king (1198-1230) of Bohemia. The struggle within the Holy Roman Empire for the imperial crown enabled Ottocar 
1 CONC to obtain (1198) from Philip of Swabia the royal title. Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II confirmed the title (1212) and the election (1216) of Ottocar's son (later Wenceslaus I) as his successor. In 1221, Ottocar was forced to surr
1 CONC ender disciplinary rights over the clergy to the bishop of Prague but retained control over secular church affairs.
0 @N6000000000337202228@ NOTE (Research):Wenceslas III Encyclopædia Britannica Article    Article   Index Entry    E-mail this article   Print this article   Cite this article     born Oct. 6, 1289 died Aug. 4, 1306, Olomouc, Moravia, Bohemia   last king of th
1 CONC e Premyslid dynasty of Bohemia, king of Hungary from 1301 to 1304, and claimant to the Polish throne; his brief reign in Bohemia was cut short by his assassination, which also prevented him from asserting his right to Poland.   We
1 CONC nceslas renounced his hereditary rights to Austria and his Hungarian crown before succeeding his father, King Wenceslas II, on the Bohemian throne in 1305. An intelligent and well-educated yet dissolute person, he determined to en
1 CONC force his claim to Poland and was raising an army when he died at the hand of an unknown assassin. The male line of the Premyslid dynasty, which had ruled Bohemia for nearly four centuries, died with him.
0 @N6000000002187834943@ NOTE Wenceslaus III of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslaus III Premyslid (Czech and Slovak Václav, Hungarian Vencel, Polish Waclaw, Croatian: Vjenceslav), (October 6, 1289 – August 4, 1306) was the King of Hungary (1301 - 1305) and King of Bohemia (1305 - 1306).
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslaus III was the son of Wenceslaus II, King of Bohemia and Poland, and Judith von Habsburg, the daughter of Rudolf I, King of the Romans. He faced the problem of internal quarrels in Hungary and in Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslaus was the last of the male Premyslid rulers of Bohemia. His sister, Elisabeth (Eliška), heiress of Bohemia, married John "The Blind" of Luxembourg, who assumed the Bohemian throne in his wife's right.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Kingdom of Hungary
1 CONT
1 CONT His father accepted the crown of Hungary on behalf of Wenceslaus III in 1301. On August 27, 1301, Wenceslaus III was crowned in Székesfehérvár as the King of Hungary and as such assumed the name Ladislaus (Hungarian: László, Czech
1 CONC , Slovak and Croatian: Ladislav). At that time the Kingdom of Hungary was split into several de-facto principalities, and Wenceslaus was only accepted as the King of Hungary by the rulers in modern Slovakia (Matthew Csák and the A
1 CONC bas) and Burgenland (the Güssings [Köszegs]). But the Abas and Matthew Csák switched sides in 1303 and started to support Wenceslaus' rival Charles Robert of Anjou. Consequently, the young Wenceslaus, in Buda, became afraid and wr
1 CONC ote to his father in Prague for help. His father took a large army and invaded Buda, but having considered the situation, he took his son and the Hungarian crown and returned to Bohemia. Ivan of Güssing was named to represent Wenc
1 CONC eslaus III in Hungary. After his father's death, Wenceslaus III decided to renounce the Hungarian throne, and on December 6, 1305, he relinquished the crown to Otto, Duke of Lower Bavaria. But Otto, supported only by the Güssings,
1 CONC  was imprisoned in 1307 and abdicated the throne in 1308, leaving Charles Robert as ruler of Hungary. In Hungarian historiography he is noted as an anti-king during the interregnum of 1301-1310.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Poland
1 CONT
1 CONT Wenceslaus III, however, wanted to claim his hereditary right to the Polish throne, but was murdered under mysterious circumstances in Olomouc, Moravia on August 4, 1306, while on a campaign to that end.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * History of Poland (966-1385)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Wenceslaus II King of Bohemia Succeeded by
1 CONT Henry of Carinthia
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Andrew III King of Hungary Succeeded by
1 CONT Otto III of Bavaria
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Venceslaus II King of Poland
1 CONT 1305-1306 Succeeded by
1 CONT Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high
0 @N6000000002176663839@ NOTE Claimant to the throne of Poland, he renounced his hereditiary rights to
1 CONT Austria and his Hungarian crown before succeeding his father, King Wenceslas
1 CONT II, on the Bohemian throne in 1305. An intelligent and well-educated yet
1 CONT dissolute person, he determined to enforce his claim to Poland and was
1 CONT raising an army when he died at the hand of an unknown assassin. The male
1 CONT line of the Premyslid dynasty, which had ruled Bohemia for nearly four
1 CONT centuries, died with him. See #1751 and #1755 for the next king of Bohemia.
0 @N6000000002240804827@ NOTE Claimant to the throne of Poland, he renounced his hereditiary rights to
1 CONT Austria and his Hungarian crown before succeeding his father, King Wenceslas
1 CONT II, on the Bohemian throne in 1305. An intelligent and well-educated yet
1 CONT dissolute person, he determined to enforce his claim to Poland and was
1 CONT raising an army when he died at the hand of an unknown assassin. The male
1 CONT line of the Premyslid dynasty, which had ruled Bohemia for nearly four
1 CONT centuries, died with him. See #1751 and #1755 for the next king of Bohemia.
