0 @N6000000000945059944@ NOTE Markgreve av Østerrike 1141-1156. 
1 CONT Hertug av Østerrike 1156-1177.
1 CONT Heinrich ble også hertug av Bayern, men måtte senere avstå dette til Henrik Löwe. Til
1 CONT gjengjeld fikk han avstått til seg det nåværende Øvre Østerrike og begge sine land forenet til
1 CONT Hertugdømmet Østerrike, utskilt fra den tidligere avhengighet av Bayern.
1 CONT Han forla sitt fyrstesete til Wien og påbegynte bygningen av Stefanskirken.
1 CONT I 1147 deltok han i det annet store korstog.
1 CONT Han var første gang gift 01.05.1142 med Gjertrud, datter til keiser Lothar III. Hun
1 CONT døde 18.04.1143.
1 CONT Heinrich døde da han falt av hesten i 1177.
0 @N6000000002187651428@ NOTE Henry II, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Heinrich (Henry) II, (1107 – January 13, 1177), Count Palatine of the Rhine, 1140-1141, Margrave of Austria from 1141 to 1156 and, as Heinrich (Henry) XI, also Duke of Bavaria from 1141 to 1156, Duke of Austria, 1156-1177, was a p
1 CONC rince of the Babenberg dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the son of Markgrave Leopold III, he first became Count Palatine of the Rhine until being appointed Duke of Bavaria and Margrave of Austria when his brother Leopold IV unexpectedly died.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the course of the dispute between the Welfen and Staufen dynasties in the Holy Roman Empire, the duchy of Bavaria had been taken away from the Welf Henry the Proud by the emperor and given to the Babenberg dynasty. The new Empe
1 CONC ror Friedrich I tried to reach a compromise with the Welfs and endowed the son of Henry the Proud, Henry the Lion, with Bavaria in 1156. A replacement had to be found for the Babenberg family, namely the Privilegium Minus, by whic
1 CONC h Austria was elevated to a duchy and gained complete independence from Bavaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Statue in Vienna.Other than his father, who resided in Klosterneuburg for most of the time, Henry moved his residence to Vienna in 1145. Only by this act could the modern Austrian capital surpass cities such as Krems, Melk or Klos
1 CONC terneuburg. Since then, it has remained the capital of the country. Also in 1147, St. Stephen's Cathedral was completed, which became a visible landmark of the city, showing its prominence. In 1155, Henry founded the Schottenstift
1 CONC  monastery in Vienna, in the courtyard of which a statue of him stands to this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT Until 1143, he was married to Gertrud of Supplinburg, the daughter of Emperor Lothar II. In 1148 he married Theodora Comnena, a niece of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I. Both marriages strongly show the importance of the House of B
1 CONC abenberg in Central Europe in that period.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's brother was the important chronicler Otto of Freising. His sister Judith was the wife of William V of Montferrat.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's surname, Jasomirgott, was first documented during the 13th century in the form of Jochsamergott, the meaning of which is unclear. According to one theory, it is derived from an Arab word bearing a connection to the Second 
1 CONC Crusade where Henry participated in 1146. According to a popular etymology, it is derived from the formula Ja so mir Gott helfe (meaning: "Yes, God willing").
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Leopold IV Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT 1141–1156 Succeeded by
1 CONT Henry XII 
1 CONT Margrave, then Duke of Austria
1 CONT 1141–1177 Succeeded by
1 CONT Leopold V 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT William of Ballerstedt Count Palatine of the Rhine
1 CONT 1140–1141 Succeeded by
1 CONT Herman III of Stahleck 
0 @N6000000000945059845@ NOTE Hertug av Østerrike 1177-1194. 
1 CONT Leopold utmerket seg på det tredje korstog ved inntagelsen av Ptolemais. Under
1 CONT besettelsen av Akka, var Leopold den første som plantet sin fane på byens citadell. Men midt
1 CONT under hærens gjallende jubelrop over at Akka igjen tilhørte de kristne, grep Richard Løvehjerte
1 CONT av pur misunnelse hertugens fane, kastet den i bakken og satte sin egen opp istedet.
1 CONT Hertugen bet imidlertid harmen i seg og utsatte hevnen til et gunstigere tidspunkt.
1 CONT For vågehalsen Richard Løvehjerte gjorde imidlertid hjemferden slett ikke slutt på
1 CONT eventyrene. De ble tvert imot verre enn noensinne. Først ble skipet hans drevet ut av kurs av
1 CONT storm og strandet til slutt på nordkysten av Adriaterhavet. Med tanke på Filip August og andre
1 CONT fiender som lurte på ham for å ta ham til fange, forkledte hans seg nå som kjøpmann og
1 CONT fortsatte reisen til lands. Men i nærheten av Wien ble han oppdaget, falt i hendene på sin
1 CONT dødsfiende Leopold og ble holdt som fange i nesten to år, helt til hans trofaste vasaller greide å
1 CONT skaffe til veie den veldige løsesummen som ble forlangt for ham. Den skal ha beløpet seg til
1 CONT mer enn to års inntekter for den engelske krone.
1 CONT I 1192 knyttet Leopold Steiermark til sitt hertugdømme.
1 CONT Han ble begravet i Heilingenburg.
0 @N6000000002188005270@ NOTE Leopold V, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold V (1157 – December 31, 1194), the Virtuous, was a Babenberg duke of Austria from 1177 to 1194 and Styria from 1192 to 1194.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold was the son of Henry II Jasomirgott and his Byzantine wife Theodora Comnena. In 1172 he married Helena, daughter of King Géza II of Hungary, and their sons were Frederick I and Leopold VI.
1 CONT Leopold V.
1 CONT Leopold V.
1 CONT
1 CONT On August 17, 1186 the Georgenberg Pact was negotiated, by which Styria and the central part of Upper Austria were amalgamated into the Duchy of Austria after 1192. This was the first step towards the creation of modern Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold is mainly remembered outside Austria for his participation in the Third Crusade. He arrived to take part in the siege of Acre in spring 1191, having sailed from Zadar on the Adriatic coast. He took over command of what rem
1 CONC ained of the imperial forces after the death of Frederick VI, Duke of Swabia in January.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Acre surrendered, the banners of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, Richard I of England, Philip II of France and Leopold were raised in the city by Leopold's cousin, Conrad of Montferrat. However, Richard removed Leopold's flag (see
1 CONC  Siege of Acre). Richard was also suspected of involvement in the murder of Conrad, shortly after his election as King of Jerusalem in April 1192. On his journey back that winter, Richard, travelling in disguise, had to stop in Vi
1 CONC enna, where he was recognized (supposedly because of his signet ring) and was arrested in the Erdberg district (modern Landstraße). For some time the king was imprisoned in Dürnstein, and was then brought before Emperor Henry VI, 
1 CONC and accused of Conrad's murder. The immense ransom, supposedly six thousand buckets of silver, became the foundation for the mint in Vienna, and was used to build new city walls for Vienna, as well as to build Wiener Neustadt. How
1 CONC ever, the duke was excommunicated by Pope Celestine III for having taken a fellow crusader prisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1194 Leopold's foot was crushed when his horse fell on him at a tournament in Graz. He died of gangrene, still under excommunication.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Henry II Duke of Austria Succeeded by
1 CONT Frederick I
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ottokar IV Duke of Styria
1 CONT 1192-1194 Succeeded by
