0 @N6000000002187821133@ NOTE Isaac Comnenus (d. 1152)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Isaakios Comnenus (after 16 January 1093 – after 1152) was the third son of Alexius I Comnenus and Irene Ducaena. He was the brother of John II Comnenus and Anna Comnena.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the succession of John II in 1118, Isaac was received at the imperial court almost as an equal to the emperor. By 1130 John and Isaac had become estranged, and Isaac was forced to flee Constantinople, ultimately seeking refu
1 CONC ge at the court of the Malik (king) Gümüshtigin Ghazi ibn Danishmend at Melitene, near the Euphrates river. This ruler was, as the overlord of the ascendant Turkic power in Anatolia, the most serious obstacle to John's ambitions i
1 CONC n the Anatolian peninsula. There are vague reports that Isaac was plotting for the throne. This conflict within the top tier of the military aristocracy would continue into the reign of Manuel I Comnenus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT Between 1112 and 1116 he married Kata of Georgia, daughter of David IV of Georgia and Rusudan of Armenia. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT John Comnenus Tzelepes, a Muslim. 
1 CONT Andronicus I Comnenus. 
1 CONT Maria, married before 1166 Joseph Bryennios. 
1 CONT Anna Comnena, married John Arbantenos. 
1 CONT Eudocia Comnena, probably married Constantine Palaeologus. 
1 CONT Helena Comnena (d. 1183), probably married Yury Dolgoruky. 
1 CONT He also had an illegitimate son, Alexius Comnenus.
