0 @N6000000000337158072@ NOTE (Research):Andras II, King of Hungary 1205-1235, (1176-1235)  son of Bela III, King of Hungary 1173-1196  and Agnes de Chatillon  Born 1176   Died 26 October 1235  Married 1215  Yolande de Courtenay Died 1233    He succeeded his n
1 CONC ephew, the infant Ladislas III, in 1205. By his reckless generosity he impoverished the crown and made the monarchy dependent on the great feudatories, who reduced Hungary to a state bordering on anarchy. His first wife, Gertrude 
1 CONC of Meran, was murdered on 24 September 1213 by rebellious nobles. In 1217 Andras II set out  for the Holy Land with an army of 15,000 men, about 10,000 of whom sailed from Venice to Acre.  One prize he captured was a large earthen
1 CONC ware jar which was believed to be one of the waterpots which had been used at the marriage at Cana, when Christ had miraculously turned the water it held into wine. King Andras, who acquired this relic, was as delighted  with it a
1 CONC s if he had captured Jerusalem itself. However, he was getting more and more bored with the life in Acre and, welcoming the news that Bohemund IV of Antioch was planning to marry the half-sister of Hugh of Cyprus in Tripoli, he we
1 CONC nt off happily to join the wedding party there. But the festivities were cut short just after the New Year by the sudden and unexpected death of Hugh. This was too much for King Andras; he returned to Acre, gathered his men togeth
1 CONC er and, having made arrangements with the Seljuk Sultan of Rum for safe passage through his land, marched them home again through Anatolia.  On his return, the barons extorted from him the Golden Bull which has been called the Hun
1 CONC garian Magna Carta. Andras II was married three times. St.Elisabeth of Hungary was his daughter by his first wife.   Source: Leo van de Pas
