0 @N6000000002187757311@ NOTE Ladislaus II of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ladislaus II or László II (Hungarian: II. László; slovak language: Ladislav II.), (1131–January 14, 1163), king of Hungary from 1162 to 1163.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ladislaus was the second son of King Béla II of Hungary by Jelena of Serbia. In 1137 his father named him duke of Bosnia, but Ladislaus quarreled with his brother Géza II and was forced to go into exile. Like his younger brother S
1 CONC tephen, Ladislaus sought the support of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Géza II in May 1162, the throne passed to his young son Stephen III, but the Byzantine Emperor threatened the Hungarian nobility, forcing it to accept Ladislaus as king. The reign of Ladislaus lasted for about h
1 CONC alf a year, from July 15, 1162 to his death on January 14, 1163. Almost nothing is known about his short reign. Already recognized his brother's heir, Stephen IV succeeded to the throne. By a wife whose name is unknown, Ladislaus 
1 CONC had a daughter, Mária.
1 CONT
1 CONT In medieval times he was not counted as a king (being only an anti-king). So Ladislaus III counted as Ladislaus II and Ladislaus IV. counted as Ladislaus III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stephen III King of Hungary
1 CONT 1162–1163 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stephen IV 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT G. Vég, Magyarország királyai és királynoi, Maecenas, 1990. 
1 CONT (primary source) The Hungarian Illuminated Chronicle, A. West, trans., Corvina, 1969. 
1 CONT (primary source) John Kinnamos, Deeds of John and Manuel Comnenus, C.M. Brand, trans., Columbia University Press, 1976. 
0 @N6000000002187837710@ NOTE Oleg of Chernihiv
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Oleg Sviatoslavich of Chernigov, sometimes also styled as of Tmutarakan, was a Rurikid prince whose equivocal adventures ignited political unrest in Kievan Rus at the turn of the 11th and 12th centuries.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of the sons of Sviatoslav II of Kiev, Oleg was named after his grand uncle. In the 1070s, he ruled the towns of Rostov and Lutsk, whence he made a raid into Bohemia in 1076. The same year his father died in Kiev and was succee
1 CONC ded by his brother Vsevolod. Failing to get along with him, Oleg had to flee to a distant Chernigovian domain on the Black Sea shore, called Tmutarakan. There he made an alliance with the Kipchaks, and with their support returned 
1 CONC his father's patrimony, Chernigov, modern Chernihiv, (1078). It was the first time when Slavic princes, in order to achieve their ends, brought pagan hordes to the walls of Russian cities.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Oleg is buried in the Savior Cathedral of Chernigov (1031-36).On October 3, 1078 Oleg's forces clashed with Vsevolod of Kiev at the Nezhatinnaya Niva (or Nezhatina Niva, today's Nizhyn). He was defeated and escaped to Tmutarakan, 
1 CONC where the Khazars had him imprisoned and sent in chains to Constantinople. The emperor, who was a relative and ally of Vsevolod, exiled him to Rhodes. There he married a noble lady, Theophano Mouzalonissa, who bore him several chi
1 CONC ldren.
1 CONT
1 CONT Four years later, we again find him active in Tmutarakan, where he adopted the title "archon of Khazaria". In 1094, he returned with the Kipchaks to Rus and captured Chernigov. There ensued a prolonged internecine struggle with hi
1 CONC s cousins Sviatopolk and Vladimir Monomakh. One of the most prominent princes of Kievan period who never attained the Kievan throne, he died on August 1, 1115 and was buried in Chernigov.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Tale of Igor's Campaign styles him Gorislavich, poetically deriving his patronimic from the Russian word for sorrow. His descendants, known as Olgovichi, were archrivals of Vladimir's descendants (known as Monomakhovichi) in t
1 CONC heir struggle for supremacy in Rus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT Russian Primary Chronicle 
