0 @N6000000002187828800@ NOTE Boleslaus the Long
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus I Long (Boleslaw I Wysoki) (1127 - December 7 or 8 1201) - Duke of Silesia 1163 - 1172.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the oldest son of Wladislaus II the Exile and Agnes of Babenberg. After the partition of Silesia Province (1173) he became a Duke of Wroclaw. He lost Glogow (from 1177 to ca 1185). He was also a Duke of Opole (1201). He was
1 CONC  in exile three times (1146-1163, 1172-1173, 1177).
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT - Duke of Wroclaw Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry I the Bearded 
0 @N6000000002187891853@ NOTE Stephen Ladislaus I of Serbia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stephen Ladislaus I (Stefan Vladislav I, ?????? ????????? I) (died after 1264) was Serb king from 1233 up to 1242, son of Stefan Prvovencani and Eudocia Angelina, and son-in-law of Bulgarian tzar Ivan Asen II. He overthrew his old
1 CONC er brother, king Radoslav and succeeded with help from his father-in-law. He transferred body of Saint Sava from Trnovo to Mileševa. After death of his father-in-law, during Tatar invasion in Middle Europe he was overthrown by his
1 CONC  younger brother Uroš, who gave him to rule Zeta.
1 CONT
1 CONT His marriage with Beloslava, daughter of Ivan Asen II issued two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Desa 
1 CONT anonymous daughter, who married a Balkan nobleman. 
1 CONT See also: List of Serbian monarchs - History of Serbia
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Radoslav
1 CONT  House of Nemanjic
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Uroš I
1 CONT
0 @N6000000002187891844@ NOTE Stefan Radoslav
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Stefan Radoslav (?????? ????????) (around 1192-1234) was king of Serbia from 1228 to 1233.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the eldest son of Stefan Prvovencani and Eudocia Angelina. His maternal grandparents were Alexius III Angelus and Euphrosyne Doukaina Kamaterina.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father in 1228. As ruler he was blind and relied on his father-in-law Theodore I Ducas, despot of Epirus (1215-1230). When his father-in-law was beaten by the Bulgarians, Radoslav could no longer remain in power: 
1 CONC the nobility dethroned him and placed his younger brother Vladislav on the throne . Rejecting his family, Radoslav ended his life as a monk.
1 CONT
1 CONT His marriage with Anna, daughter of Theodore I Ducas, was childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: List of Serbian monarchs - History of Serbia
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Stefan Prvovencani
1 CONT  House of Nemanjic
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Stefan Vladislav I
1 CONT
